John	Jn	50	18	7	Again therefore he asked them: Whom seek ye? And they said, Jesus of Nazareth.
John	Jn	50	18	8	Jesus answered, I have told you that I am he. If therefore you seek me, let these go their way.
John	Jn	50	18	9	That the word might be fulfilled which he said: Of them whom thou hast given me, I have not lost any one.
John	Jn	50	18	10	Then Simon Peter, having a sword, drew it, and struck the servant of the high priest, and cut off his right ear. And the name of the servant was Malchus.
John	Jn	50	18	11	Jesus therefore said to Peter: Put up thy sword into the scabbard. The chalice which my Father hath given me, shall I not drink it?
John	Jn	50	18	12	Then the band and the tribune, and the servants of the Jews, took Jesus, and bound him:
John	Jn	50	18	13	And they led him away to Annas first, for he was father in law to Caiphas, who was the high priest of that year.
John	Jn	50	18	14	Now Caiphas was he who had given the counsel to the Jews: That it was expedient that one man should die for the people.
John	Jn	50	18	15	And Simon Peter followed Jesus, and so did another disciple. And that disciple was known to the high priest, and went in with Jesus into the court of the high priest.
John	Jn	50	18	16	But Peter stood at the door without. The other disciple therefore, who was known to the high priest, went out, and spoke to the portress, and brought in Peter.
John	Jn	50	18	17	The maid therefore that was portress, saith to Peter: Art not thou also one of this man's disciples? He saith: I am not.
John	Jn	50	18	18	Now the servants and ministers stood at a fire of coals, because it was cold, and warmed themselves. And with them was Peter also, standing, and warming himself.
John	Jn	50	18	19	The high priest therefore asked Jesus of his disciples, and of his doctrine.
John	Jn	50	18	20	Jesus answered him: I have spoken openly to the world: I have always taught in the synagogue, and in the temple, whither all the Jews resort; and in secret I have spoken nothing.
John	Jn	50	18	21	Why asketh thou me? ask them who have heard what I have spoken unto them: behold they know what things I have said.
John	Jn	50	18	22	And when he had said these things, one of the servants standing by, gave Jesus a blow, saying: Answerest thou the high priest so?
John	Jn	50	18	23	Jesus answered him: If I have spoken evil, give testimony of the evil; but if well, why strikest thou me?
John	Jn	50	18	24	And Annas sent him bound to Caiphas the high priest.
John	Jn	50	18	25	And Simon Peter was standing, and warming himself. They said therefore to him: Art not thou also one of his disciples? He denied it, and said: I am not.
John	Jn	50	18	26	One of the servants of the high priest (a kinsman to him whose ear Peter cut off) saith to him: Did I not see thee in the garden with him?
John	Jn	50	18	27	Again therefore Peter denied; and immediately the cock crew.
John	Jn	50	18	28	Then they led Jesus from Caiphas to the governor's hall. And it was morning; and they went not into the hall, that they might not be defiled, but that they might eat the pasch.
John	Jn	50	18	29	Pilate therefore went out to them, and said: What accusation bring you against this man?
John	Jn	50	18	30	They answered, and said to him: If he were not a malefactor, we would not have delivered him up to thee.
John	Jn	50	18	31	Pilate therefore said to them: Take him you, and judge him according to your law. The Jews therefore said to him: It is not lawful for us to put any man to death;
John	Jn	50	18	32	That the word of Jesus might be fulfilled, which he said, signifying what death he should die.
John	Jn	50	18	33	Pilate therefore went into the hall again, and called Jesus, and said to him: Art thou the king of the Jews?
John	Jn	50	18	34	Jesus answered: Sayest thou this thing of thyself, or have others told it thee of me?
John	Jn	50	18	35	Pilate answered: Am I a Jew? Thy own nation, and the chief priests, have delivered thee up to me: what hast thou done?
John	Jn	50	18	36	Jesus answered: My kingdom is not of this world. If my kingdom were of this world, my servants would certainly strive that I should not be delivered to the Jews: but now my kingdom is not from hence.
John	Jn	50	18	37	Pilate therefore said to him: Art thou a king then? Jesus answered: Thou sayest that I am a king. For this was I born, and for this came I into the world; that I should give testimony to the truth. Every one that is of the truth, heareth my voice.
John	Jn	50	18	38	Pilate saith to him: What is truth? And when he said this, he went out again to the Jews, and saith to them: I find no cause in him.
John	Jn	50	18	39	But you have a custom that I should release one unto you at the pasch: will you, therefore, that I release unto you the king of the Jews?
John	Jn	50	18	40	Then cried they all again, saying: Not this man, but Barabbas. Now Barabbas was a robber.
John	Jn	50	19	1	Then therefore, Pilate took Jesus, and scourged him.
John	Jn	50	19	2	And the soldiers platting a crown of thorns, put it upon his head; and they put on him a purple garment.
John	Jn	50	19	3	And they came to him, and said: Hail, king of the Jews; and they gave him blows.
John	Jn	50	19	4	Pilate therefore went forth again, and saith to them: Behold, I bring him forth unto you, that you may know that I find no cause in him.
John	Jn	50	19	5	(Jesus therefore came forth, bearing the crown of thorns and the purple garment.) And he saith to them: Behold the Man.
John	Jn	50	19	6	When the chief priests, therefore, and the servants, had seen him, they cried out, saying: Crucify him, crucify him. Pilate saith to them: Take him you, and crucify him: for I find no cause in him.
John	Jn	50	19	7	The Jews answered him: We have a law; and according to the law he ought to die, because he made himself the Son of God.
John	Jn	50	19	8	When Pilate therefore had heard this saying, he feared the more.
John	Jn	50	19	9	And he entered into the hall again, and he said to Jesus: Whence art thou? But Jesus gave him no answer.
John	Jn	50	19	10	Pilate therefore saith to him: Speakest thou not to me? knowest thou not that I have power to crucify thee, and I have power to release thee?
John	Jn	50	19	11	Jesus answered: Thou shouldst not have any power against me, unless it were given thee from above. Therefore, he that hath delivered me to thee, hath the greater sin.
John	Jn	50	19	12	And from henceforth Pilate sought to release him. But the Jews cried out, saying: If thou release this man, thou art not Caesar's friend. For whosoever maketh himself a king, speaketh against Caesar.
John	Jn	50	19	13	Now when Pilate had heard these words, he brought Jesus forth, and sat down in the judgment seat, in the place that is called Lithostrotos, and in Hebrew Gabbatha.
John	Jn	50	19	14	And it was the parasceve of the pasch, about the sixth hour, and he saith to the Jews: Behold your king.
John	Jn	50	19	15	But they cried out: Away with him; away with him; crucify him. Pilate saith to them: Shall I crucify your king? The chief priests answered: We have no king but Caesar.
John	Jn	50	19	16	Then therefore he delivered him to them to be crucified. And they took Jesus, and led him forth.
John	Jn	50	19	17	And bearing his own cross, he went forth to that place which is called Calvary, but in Hebrew Golgotha.
John	Jn	50	19	18	Where they crucified him, and with him two others, one on each side, and Jesus in the midst.
John	Jn	50	19	19	And Pilate wrote a title also, and he put it upon the cross. And the writing was: JESUS OF NAZARETH, THE KING OF THE JEWS.
John	Jn	50	19	20	This title therefore many of the Jews did read: because the place where Jesus was crucified was nigh to the city: and it was written in Hebrew, in Greek, and in Latin.
John	Jn	50	19	21	Then the chief priests of the Jews said to Pilate: Write not, The King of the Jews; but that he said, I am the King of the Jews.
John	Jn	50	19	22	Pilate answered: What I have written, I have written.
John	Jn	50	19	23	The soldiers therefore, when they had crucified him, took his garments, (and they made four parts, to every soldier a part,) and also his coat. Now the coat was without seam, woven from the top throughout.
John	Jn	50	19	24	They said then one to another: Let us not cut it, but let us cast lots for it, whose it shall be; that the scripture might be fulfilled, saying: They have parted my garments among them, and upon my vesture they have cast lots. And the soldiers indeed did these things.
John	Jn	50	19	25	Now there stood by the cross of Jesus, his mother, and his mother's sister, Mary of Cleophas, and Mary Magdalen.
John	Jn	50	19	26	When Jesus therefore had seen his mother and the disciple standing whom he loved, he saith to his mother: Woman, behold thy son.
John	Jn	50	19	27	After that, he saith to the disciple: Behold thy mother. And from that hour, the disciple took her to his own.
John	Jn	50	19	28	Afterwards, Jesus knowing that all things were now accomplished, that the scripture might be fulfilled, said: I thirst.
John	Jn	50	19	29	Now there was a vessel set there full of vinegar. And they, putting a sponge full of vinegar and hyssop, put it to his mouth.
John	Jn	50	19	30	Jesus therefore, when he had taken the vinegar, said: It is consummated. And bowing his head, he gave up the ghost.
John	Jn	50	19	31	Then the Jews, (because it was the parasceve,) that the bodies might not remain on the cross on the sabbath day, (for that was a great sabbath day,) besought Pilate that their legs might be broken, and that they might be taken away.
John	Jn	50	19	32	The soldiers therefore came; and they broke the legs of the first, and of the other that was crucified with him.
John	Jn	50	19	33	But after they were come to Jesus, when they saw that he was already dead, they did not break his legs.
John	Jn	50	19	34	But one of the soldiers with a spear opened his side, and immediately there came out blood and water.
John	Jn	50	19	35	And he that saw it, hath given testimony, and his testimony is true. And he knoweth that he saith true; that you also may believe.
John	Jn	50	19	36	For these things were done, that the scripture might be fulfilled: You shall not break a bone of him.
John	Jn	50	19	37	And again another scripture saith: They shall look on him whom they pierced.
John	Jn	50	19	38	And after these things, Joseph of Arimathea (because he was a disciple of Jesus, but secretly for fear of the Jews) besought Pilate that he might take away the body of Jesus. And Pilate gave leave. He came therefore, and took the body of Jesus.
John	Jn	50	19	39	And Nicodemus also came, (he who at the first came to Jesus by night,) bringing a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about an hundred pound weight.
John	Jn	50	19	40	They took therefore the body of Jesus, and bound it in linen cloths, with the spices, as the manner of the Jews is to bury.
John	Jn	50	19	41	Now there was in the place where he was crucified, a garden; and in the garden a new sepulchre, wherein no man yet had been laid.
John	Jn	50	19	42	There, therefore, because of the parasceve of the Jews, they laid Jesus, because the sepulchre was nigh at hand.
John	Jn	50	20	1	And on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalen cometh early, when it was yet dark, unto the sepulchre; and she saw the stone taken away from the sepulchre.
John	Jn	50	20	2	She ran, therefore, and cometh to Simon Peter, and to the other disciple whom Jesus loved, and saith to them: They have taken away the Lord out of the sepulchre, and we know not where they have laid him.
John	Jn	50	20	3	Peter therefore went out, and that other disciple, and they came to the sepulchre.
John	Jn	50	20	4	And they both ran together, and that other disciple did outrun Peter, and came first to the sepulchre.
John	Jn	50	20	5	And when he stooped down, he saw the linen cloths lying; but yet he went not in.
John	Jn	50	20	6	Then cometh Simon Peter, following him, and went into the sepulchre, and saw the linen cloths lying,
John	Jn	50	20	7	And the napkin that had been about his head, not lying with the linen cloths, but apart, wrapped up into one place.
John	Jn	50	20	8	Then that other disciple also went in, who came first to the sepulchre: and he saw, and believed.
John	Jn	50	20	9	For as yet they knew not the scripture, that he must rise again from the dead.
John	Jn	50	20	10	The disciples therefore departed again to their home.
John	Jn	50	20	11	But Mary stood at the sepulchre without, weeping. Now as she was weeping, she stooped down, and looked into the sepulchre,
John	Jn	50	20	12	And she saw two angels in white, sitting, one at the head, and one at the feet, where the body of Jesus had been laid.
John	Jn	50	20	13	They say to her: Woman, why weepest thou? She saith to them: Because they have taken away my Lord; and I know not where they have laid him.
John	Jn	50	20	14	When she had thus said, she turned herself back, and saw Jesus standing; and she knew not that it was Jesus.
John	Jn	50	20	15	Jesus saith to her: Woman, why weepest thou? whom seekest thou? She, thinking it was the gardener, saith to him: Sir, if thou hast taken him hence, tell me where thou hast laid him, and I will take him away.
John	Jn	50	20	16	Jesus saith to her: Mary. She turning, saith to him: Rabboni (which is to say, Master).
John	Jn	50	20	17	Jesus saith to her: Do not touch me, for I am not yet ascended to my Father. But go to my brethren, and say to them: I ascend to my Father and to your Father, to my God and your God.
John	Jn	50	20	18	Mary Magdalen cometh, and telleth the disciples: I have seen the Lord, and these things he said to me.
John	Jn	50	20	19	Now when it was late that same day, the first of the week, and the doors were shut, where the disciples were gathered together, for fear of the Jews, Jesus came and stood in the midst, and said to them: Peace be to you.
John	Jn	50	20	20	And when he had said this, he shewed them his hands and his side. The disciples therefore were glad, when they saw the Lord.
John	Jn	50	20	21	He said therefore to them again: Peace be to you. As the Father hath sent me, I also send you.
John	Jn	50	20	22	When he had said this, he breathed on them; and he said to them: Receive ye the Holy Ghost.
John	Jn	50	20	23	Whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them; and whose sins you shall retain, they are retained.
John	Jn	50	20	24	Now Thomas, one of the twelve, who is called Didymus, was not with them when Jesus came.
John	Jn	50	20	25	The other disciples therefore said to him: We have seen the Lord. But he said to them: Except I shall see in his hands the print of the nails, and put my finger into the place of the nails, and put my hand into his side, I will not believe.
John	Jn	50	20	26	And after eight days again his disciples were within, and Thomas with them. Jesus cometh, the doors being shut, and stood in the midst, and said: Peace be to you.
John	Jn	50	20	27	Then he saith to Thomas: Put in thy finger hither, and see my hands; and bring hither thy hand, and put it into my side; and be not faithless, but believing.
John	Jn	50	20	28	Thomas answered, and said to him: My Lord, and my God.
John	Jn	50	20	29	Jesus saith to him: Because thou hast seen me, Thomas, thou hast believed: blessed are they that have not seen, and have believed.
John	Jn	50	20	30	Many other signs also did Jesus in the sight of his disciples, which are not written in this book.
John	Jn	50	20	31	But these are written, that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God: and that believing, you may have life in his name.
John	Jn	50	21	1	After this, Jesus shewed himself again to the disciples at the sea of Tiberias. And he shewed himself after this manner.
John	Jn	50	21	2	There were together Simon Peter, and Thomas, who is called Didymus, and Nathanael, who was of Cana of Galilee, and the sons of Zebedee, and two others of his disciples.
John	Jn	50	21	3	Simon Peter saith to them: I go a fishing. They say to him: We also come with thee. And they went forth, and entered into the ship: and that night they caught nothing.
John	Jn	50	21	4	But when the morning was come, Jesus stood on the shore: yet the disciples knew not that it was Jesus.
John	Jn	50	21	5	Jesus therefore said to them: Children, have you any meat? They answered him: No.
John	Jn	50	21	6	He saith to them: Cast the net on the right side of the ship, and you shall find. They cast therefore; and now they were not able to draw it, for the multitude of fishes.
John	Jn	50	21	7	That disciple therefore whom Jesus loved, said to Peter: It is the Lord. Simon Peter, when he heard that it was the Lord, girt his coat about him, (for he was naked,) and cast himself into the sea.
John	Jn	50	21	8	But the other disciples came in the ship, (for they were not far from the land, but as it were two hundred cubits,) dragging the net with fishes.
John	Jn	50	21	9	As soon then as they came to land, they saw hot coals lying, and a fish laid thereon, and bread.
John	Jn	50	21	10	Jesus saith to them: Bring hither of the fishes which you have now caught.
John	Jn	50	21	11	Simon Peter went up, and drew the net to land, full of great fishes, one hundred and fifty-three. And although there were so many, the net was not broken.
John	Jn	50	21	12	Jesus saith to them: Come, and dine. And none of them who were at meat, durst ask him: Who art thou? knowing that it was the Lord.
John	Jn	50	21	13	And Jesus cometh and taketh bread, and giveth them, and fish in like manner.
John	Jn	50	21	14	This is now the third time that Jesus was manifested to his disciples, after he was risen from the dead.
John	Jn	50	21	15	When therefore they had dined, Jesus saith to Simon Peter: Simon son of John, lovest thou me more than these? He saith to him: Yea, Lord, thou knowest that I love thee. He saith to him: Feed my lambs.
John	Jn	50	21	16	He saith to him again: Simon, son of John, lovest thou me? He saith to him: Yea, Lord, thou knowest that I love thee. He saith to him: Feed my lambs.
John	Jn	50	21	17	He said to him the third time: Simon, son of John, lovest thou me? Peter was grieved, because he had said to him the third time: Lovest thou me? And he said to him: Lord, thou knowest all things: thou knowest that I love thee. He said to him: Feed my sheep.
John	Jn	50	21	18	Amen, amen I say to thee, when thou wast younger, thou didst gird thyself, and didst walk where thou wouldst. But when thou shalt be old, thou shalt stretch forth thy hands, and another shall gird thee, and lead thee whither thou wouldst not.
John	Jn	50	21	19	And this he said, signifying by what death he should glorify God. And when he had said this, he saith to him: Follow me.
John	Jn	50	21	20	Peter turning about, saw that disciple whom Jesus loved following, who also leaned on his breast at supper, and said: Lord, who is he that shall betray thee?
John	Jn	50	21	21	Him therefore when Peter had seen, he saith to Jesus: Lord, and what shall this man do?
John	Jn	50	21	22	Jesus saith to him: So I will have him to remain till I come, what is it to thee? follow thou me.
John	Jn	50	21	23	This saying therefore went abroad among the brethren, that that disciple should not die. And Jesus did not say to him: He should not die; but, So I will have him to remain till I come, what is it to thee?
John	Jn	50	21	24	This is that disciple who giveth testimony of these things, and hath written these things; and we know that his testimony is true.
John	Jn	50	21	25	But there are also many other things which Jesus did; which, if they were written every one, the world itself, I think, would not be able to contain the books that should be written.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	1	The former treatise I made, O Theophilus, of all things which Jesus began to do and to teach,
The Acts	Acts	51	1	2	Until the day on which, giving commandments by the Holy Ghost to the apostles whom he had chosen, he was taken up.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	3	To whom also he shewed himself alive after his passion, by many proofs, for forty days appearing to them, and speaking of the kingdom of God.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	4	And eating together with them, he commanded them, that they should not depart from Jerusalem, but should wait for the promise of the Father, which you have heard (saith he) by my mouth.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	5	For John indeed baptized with water, but you shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost, not many days hence.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	6	They therefore who were come together, asked him, saying: Lord, wilt thou at this time restore again the kingdom to Israel?
The Acts	Acts	51	1	7	But he said to them: It is not for you to know the times or moments, which the Father hath put in his own power:
The Acts	Acts	51	1	8	But you shall receive the power of the Holy Ghost coming upon you, and you shall be witnesses unto me in Jerusalem, and in all Judea, and Samaria, and even to the uttermost part of the earth.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	9	And when he had said these things, while they looked on, he was raised up: and a cloud received him out of their sight.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	10	And while they were beholding him going up to heaven, behold two men stood by them in white garments.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	11	Who also said: Ye men of Galilee, why stand you looking up to heaven? This Jesus who is taken up from you into heaven, shall so come, as you have seen him going into heaven.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	12	Then they returned to Jerusalem from the mount that is called Olivet, which is nigh Jerusalem, within a sabbath day's journey.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	13	And when they were come in, they went up into an upper room, where abode Peter and John, James and Andrew, Philip and Thomas, Bartholomew and Matthew, James of Alpheus, and Simon Zelotes, and Jude the brother of James.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	14	All these were persevering with one mind in prayer with the women, and Mary the mother of Jesus, and with his brethren.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	15	In those days Peter rising up in the midst of the brethren, said: (now the number of persons together was about an hundred and twenty:)
The Acts	Acts	51	1	16	Men, brethren, the scripture must needs be fulfilled, which the Holy Ghost spoke before by the mouth of David concerning Judas, who was the leader of them that apprehended Jesus:
The Acts	Acts	51	1	17	Who was numbered with us, and had obtained part of this ministry.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	18	And he indeed hath possessed a field of the reward of iniquity, and being hanged, burst asunder in the midst: and all his bowels gushed out.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	19	And it became known to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem: so that the same field was called in their tongue, Haceldama, that is to say, The field of blood.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	20	For it is written in the book of Psalms: Let their habitation become desolate, and let there be none to dwell therein. And his bishopric let another take.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	21	Wherefore of these men who have companied with us all the time that the Lord Jesus came in and went out among us,
The Acts	Acts	51	1	22	Beginning from the baptism of John, until the day wherein he was taken up from us, one of these must be made a witness with us of his resurrection.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	23	And they appointed two, Joseph, called Barsabas, who was surnamed Justus, and Matthias.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	24	And praying, they said: Thou, Lord, who knowest the hearts of all men, shew whether of these two thou hast chosen,
The Acts	Acts	51	1	25	To take the place of this ministry and apostleship, from which Judas hath by transgression fallen, that he might go to his own place.
The Acts	Acts	51	1	26	And they gave them lots, and the lot fell upon Matthias, and he was numbered with the eleven apostles.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	1	And when the days of the Pentecost were accomplished, they were all together in one place:
The Acts	Acts	51	2	2	And suddenly there came a sound from heaven, as of a mighty wind coming, and it filled the whole house where they were sitting.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	3	And there appeared to them parted tongues as it were of fire, and it sat upon every one of them:
The Acts	Acts	51	2	4	And they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and they began to speak with divers tongues, according as the Holy Ghost gave them to speak.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	5	Now there were dwelling at Jerusalem, Jews, devout men, out of every nation under heaven.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	6	And when this was noised abroad, the multitude came together, and were confounded in mind, because that every man heard them speak in his own tongue.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	7	And they were all amazed, and wondered, saying: Behold, are not all these, that speak, Galileans?
The Acts	Acts	51	2	8	And how have we heard, every man our own tongue wherein we were born?
The Acts	Acts	51	2	9	Parthians, and Medes, and Elamites, and inhabitants of Mesopotamia, Judea, and Cappadocia, Pontus and Asia,
The Acts	Acts	51	2	10	Phrygia, and Pamphylia, Egypt, and the parts of Libya about Cyrene, and strangers of Rome,
The Acts	Acts	51	2	11	Jews also, and proselytes, Cretes, and Arabians: we have heard them speak in our own tongues the wonderful works of God.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	12	And they were all astonished, and wondered, saying one to another: What meaneth this?
The Acts	Acts	51	2	13	But others mocking, said: These men are full of new wine.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	14	But Peter standing up with the eleven, lifted up his voice, and spoke to them: Ye men of Judea, and all you that dwell in Jerusalem, be this known to you, and with your ears receive my words.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	15	For these are not drunk, as you suppose, seeing it is but the third hour of the day:
The Acts	Acts	51	2	16	But this is that which was spoken of by the prophet Joel:
The Acts	Acts	51	2	17	And it shall come to pass, in the last days, (saith the Lord,) I will pour out of my Spirit upon all flesh: and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, and your young men shall see visions, and your old men shall dream dreams.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	18	And upon my servants indeed, and upon my handmaids will I pour out in those days of my spirit, and they shall prophesy.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	19	And I will shew wonders in the heaven above, and signs on the earth beneath: blood and fire, and vapour of smoke.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	20	The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and manifest day of the Lord come.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	21	And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord, shall be saved.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	22	Ye men of Israel, hear these words: Jesus of Nazareth, a man approved of God among you, by miracles, and wonders, and signs, which God did by him, in the midst of you, as you also know:
The Acts	Acts	51	2	23	This same being delivered up, by the determinate counsel and foreknowledge of God, you by the hands of wicked men have crucified and slain.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	24	Whom God hath raised up, having loosed the sorrows of hell, as it was impossible that he should be holden by it.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	25	For David saith concerning him: I foresaw the Lord before my face: because he is at my right hand, that I may not be moved.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	26	For this my heart hath been glad, and my tongue hath rejoiced: moreover my flesh also shall rest in hope.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	27	Because thou wilt not leave my soul in hell, nor suffer thy Holy One to see corruption.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	28	Thou hast made known to me the ways of life: thou shalt make me full of joy with thy countenance.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	29	Ye men, brethren, let me freely speak to you of the patriarch David; that he died, and was buried; and his sepulchre is with us to this present day.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	30	Whereas therefore he was a prophet, and knew that God hath sworn to him with an oath, that of the fruit of his loins one should sit upon his throne.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	31	Foreseeing this, he spoke of the resurrection of Christ. For neither was he left in hell, neither did his flesh see corruption.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	32	This Jesus hath God raised again, whereof all we are witnesses.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	33	Being exalted therefore by the right hand of God, and having received of the Father the promise of the Holy Ghost, he hath poured forth this which you see and hear.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	34	For David ascended not into heaven; but he himself said: The Lord said to my Lord, sit thou on my right hand,
The Acts	Acts	51	2	35	Until I make thy enemies thy footstool.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	36	Therefore let all the house of Israel know most certainly, that God hath made both Lord and Christ, this same Jesus, whom you have crucified.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	37	Now when they had heard these things, they had compunction in their heart, and said to Peter, and to the rest of the apostles: What shall we do, men and brethren?
The Acts	Acts	51	2	38	But Peter said to them: Do penance, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ, for the remission of your sins: and you shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	39	For the promise is to you, and to your children, and to all that are far off, whomsoever the Lord our God shall call.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	40	And with very many other words did he testify and exhort them, saying: Save yourselves from this perverse generation.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	41	They therefore that received his word, were baptized; and there were added in that day about three thousand souls.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	42	And they were persevering in the doctrine of the apostles, and in the communication of the breaking of bread, and in prayers.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	43	And fear came upon every soul: many wonders also and signs were done by the apostles in Jerusalem, and there was great fear in all.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	44	And all they that believed, were together, and had all things common.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	45	Their possessions and goods they sold, and divided them to all, according as every one had need.
The Acts	Acts	51	2	46	And continuing daily with one accord in the temple, and breaking bread from house to house, they took their meat with gladness and simplicity of heart;
The Acts	Acts	51	2	47	Praising God, and having favour with all the people. And the Lord increased daily together such as should be saved.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	1	Now Peter and John went up into the temple at the ninth hour of prayer.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	2	And a certain man who was lame from his mother's womb, was carried: whom they laid every day at the gate of the temple, which is called Beautiful, that he might ask alms of them that went into the temple.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	3	He, when he had seen Peter and John about to go into the temple, asked to receive an alms.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	4	But Peter with John fastening his eyes upon him, said: Look upon us.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	5	But he looked earnestly upon them, hoping that he should receive something of them.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	6	But Peter said: Silver and gold I have none; but what I have, I give thee: In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, arise, and walk.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	7	And taking him by the right hand, he lifted him up, and forthwith his feet and soles received strength.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	8	And he leaping up, stood, and walked, and went in with them into the temple, walking, and leaping, and praising God.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	9	And all the people saw him walking and praising God.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	10	And they knew him, that it was he who sat begging alms at the Beautiful gate of the temple: and they were filled with wonder and amazement at that which had happened to him.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	11	And as he held Peter and John, all the people ran to them to the porch which is called Solomon's, greatly wondering.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	12	But Peter seeing, made answer to the people: Ye men of Israel, why wonder you at this? or why look you upon us, as if by our strength or power we had made this man to walk?
The Acts	Acts	51	3	13	The God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob, the God of our fathers, hath glorified his Son Jesus, whom you indeed delivered up and denied before the face of Pilate, when he judged he should be released.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	14	But you denied the Holy One and the Just, and desired a murderer to be granted unto you.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	15	But the author of life you killed, whom God hath raised from the dead, of which we are witnesses.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	16	And in the faith of his name, this man, whom you have seen and known, hath his name strengthened; and the faith which is by him, hath given this perfect soundness in the sight of you all.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	17	And now, brethren, I know that you did it through ignorance, as did also your rulers.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	18	But those things which God before had shewed by the mouth of all the prophets, that his Christ should suffer, he hath so fulfilled.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	19	Be penitent, therefore, and be converted, that your sins may be blotted out.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	20	That when the times of refreshment shall come from the presence of the Lord, and he shall send him who hath been preached unto you, Jesus Christ,
The Acts	Acts	51	3	21	Whom heaven indeed must receive, until the times of the restitution of all things, which God hath spoken by the mouth of his holy prophets, from the beginning of the world.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	22	For Moses said: A prophet shall the Lord your God raise up unto you of your brethren, like unto me: him you shall hear according to all things whatsoever he shall speak to you.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	23	And it shall be, that every soul which will not hear that prophet, shall be destroyed from among the people.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	24	And all the prophets, from Samuel and afterwards, who have spoken, have told of these days.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	25	You are the children of the prophets, and of the testament which God made to our fathers, saying to Abraham: And in thy seed shall all the kindreds of the earth be blessed.
The Acts	Acts	51	3	26	To you first God, raising up his Son, hath sent him to bless you; that every one may convert himself from his wickedness.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	1	And as they were speaking to the people, the priests, and the officer of the temple, and the Sadducees, came upon them,
The Acts	Acts	51	4	2	Being grieved that they taught the people, and preached in Jesus the resurrection from the dead:
The Acts	Acts	51	4	3	And they laid hands upon them, and put them in hold till the next day; for it was now evening.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	4	But many of them who had heard the word, believed; and the number of the men was made five thousand.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	5	And it came to pass on the morrow, that their princes, and ancients, and scribes, were gathered together in Jerusalem;
The Acts	Acts	51	4	6	And Annas the high priest, and Caiphas, and John, and Alexander, and as many as were of the kindred of the high priest.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	7	And setting them in the midst, they asked: By what power, or by what name, have you done this?
The Acts	Acts	51	4	8	Then Peter, filled with the Holy Ghost, said to them: Ye princes of the people, and ancients, hear:
The Acts	Acts	51	4	9	If we this day are examined concerning the good deed done to the infirm man, by what means he hath been made whole:
The Acts	Acts	51	4	10	Be it known to you all, and to all the people of Israel, that by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom you crucified, whom God hath raised from the dead, even by him this man standeth here before you whole.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	11	This is the stone which was rejected by you the builders, which is become the head of the corner.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	12	Neither is there salvation in any other. For there is no other name under heaven given to men, whereby we must be saved.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	13	Now seeing the constancy of Peter and of John, understanding that they were illiterate and ignorant men, they wondered; and they knew them that they had been with Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	14	Seeing the man also who had been healed standing with them, they could say nothing against it.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	15	But they commanded them to go aside out of the council; and they conferred among themselves,
The Acts	Acts	51	4	16	Saying: What shall we do to these men? for indeed a known miracle hath been done by them, to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem: it is manifest, and we cannot deny it.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	17	But that it may be no farther spread among the people, let us threaten them that they speak no more in this name to any man.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	18	And calling them, they charged them not to speak at all, nor teach in the name of Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	19	But Peter and John answering, said to them: If it be just in the sight of God, to hear you rather than God, judge ye.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	20	For we cannot but speak the things which we have seen and heard.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	21	But they threatening, sent them away, not finding how they might punish them, because of the people; for all men glorified what had been done, in that which had come to pass.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	22	For the man was above forty years old, in whom that miraculous cure had been wrought.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	23	And being let go, they came to their own company, and related all that the chief priests and ancients had said to them.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	24	Who having heard it, with one accord lifted up their voice to God, and said: Lord, thou art he that didst make heaven and earth, the sea, and all things that are in them.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	25	Who, by the Holy Ghost, by the mouth of our father David, thy servant, hast said: Why did the Gentiles rage, and the people meditate vain things?
The Acts	Acts	51	4	26	The kings of the earth stood up, and the princes assembled together against the Lord and his Christ.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	27	For of a truth there assembled together in this city against thy holy child Jesus, whom thou hast anointed, Herod, and Pontius Pilate, with the Gentiles and the people of Israel,
The Acts	Acts	51	4	28	To do what thy hand and thy counsel decreed to be done.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	29	And now, Lord, behold their threatenings, and grant unto thy servants, that with all confidence they may speak thy word,
The Acts	Acts	51	4	30	By stretching forth thy hand to cures, and signs, and wonders to be done by the name of thy holy Son Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	31	And when they had prayed, the place was moved wherein they were assembled; and they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and they spoke the word of God with confidence.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	32	And the multitude of believers had but one heart and one soul: neither did any one say that aught of the things which he possessed, was his own; but all things were common unto them.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	33	And with great power did the apostles give testimony of the resurrection of Jesus Christ our Lord; and great grace was in them all.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	34	For neither was there any one needy among them. For as many as were owners of lands or houses, sold them, and brought the price of the things they sold,
The Acts	Acts	51	4	35	And laid it down before the feet of the apostles. And distribution was made to every one, according as he had need.
The Acts	Acts	51	4	36	And Joseph, who, by the apostles, was surnamed Barnabas, (which is, by interpretation, The son of consolation,) a Levite, a Cyprian born,
The Acts	Acts	51	4	37	Having land, sold it, and brought the price, and laid it at the feet of the apostles.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	1	But a certain man named Ananias, with Saphira his wife, sold a piece of land,
The Acts	Acts	51	5	2	And by fraud kept back part of the price of the land, his wife being privy thereunto: and bringing a certain part of it, laid it at the feet of the apostles.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	3	But Peter said: Ananias, why hath Satan tempted thy heart, that thou shouldst lie to the Holy Ghost, and by fraud keep part of the price of the land?
The Acts	Acts	51	5	4	Whilst it remained, did it not remain to thee? and after it was sold, was it not in thy power? Why hast thou conceived this thing in thy heart? Thou hast not lied to men, but to God.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	5	And Ananias hearing these words, fell down, and gave up the ghost. And there came great fear upon all that heard it.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	6	And the young men rising up, removed him, and carrying him out, buried him.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	7	And it was about the space of three hours after, when his wife, not knowing what had happened, came in.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	8	And Peter said to her: Tell me, woman, whether you sold the land for so much? And she said: Yea, for so much.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	9	And Peter said unto her: Why have you agreed together to tempt the Spirit of the Lord? Behold the feet of them who have buried thy husband are at the door, and they shall carry thee out.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	10	Immediately she fell down before his feet, and gave up the ghost. And the young men coming in, found her dead: and carried her out, and buried her by her husband.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	11	And there came great fear upon the whole church, and upon all that heard these things.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	12	And by the hands of the apostles were many signs and wonders wrought among the people. And they were all with one accord in Solomon's porch.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	13	But of the rest no man durst join himself unto them; but the people magnified them.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	14	And the multitude of men and women who believed in the Lord, was more increased:
The Acts	Acts	51	5	15	Insomuch that they brought forth the sick into the streets, and laid them on beds and couches, that when Peter came, his shadow at the least, might overshadow any of them, and they might be delivered from their infirmities.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	16	And there came also together to Jerusalem a multitude out of the neighbouring cities, bringing sick persons, and such as were troubled with unclean spirits; who were all healed.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	17	Then the high priest rising up, and all they that were with him, (which is the heresy of the Sadducees,) were filled with envy.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	18	And they laid hands on the apostles, and put them in the common prison.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	19	But an angel of the Lord by night opening the doors of the prison, and leading them out, said:
The Acts	Acts	51	5	20	Go, and standing speak in the temple to the people all the words of this life.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	21	Who having heard this, early in the morning, entered into the temple, and taught. And the high priest coming, and they that were with him, called together the council, and all the ancients of the children of Israel; and they sent to the prison to have them brought.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	22	But when the ministers came, and opening the prison, found them not there, they returned and told,
The Acts	Acts	51	5	23	Saying: The prison indeed we found shut with all diligence, and the keepers standing before the doors; but opening it, we found no man within.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	24	Now when the officer of the temple and the chief priests heard these words, they were in doubt concerning them, what would come to pass.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	25	But one came and told them: Behold, the men whom you put in prison are in the temple standing, and teaching the people.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	26	Then went the officer with the ministers, and brought them without violence; for they feared the people, lest they should be stoned.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	27	And when they had brought them, they set them before the council. And the high priest asked them,
The Acts	Acts	51	5	28	Saying: Commanding we commanded you, that you should not teach in this name; and behold, you have filled Jerusalem with your doctrine, and you have a mind to bring the blood of this man upon us.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	29	But Peter and the apostles answering, said: We ought to obey God, rather than men.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	30	The God of our fathers hath raised up Jesus, whom you put to death, hanging him upon a tree.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	31	Him hath God exalted with his right hand, to be Prince and Saviour, to give repentance to Israel, and remission of sins.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	32	And we are witnesses of these things and the Holy Ghost, whom God hath given to all that obey him.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	33	When they had heard these things, they were cut to the heart, and they thought to put them to death.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	34	But one in the council rising up, a Pharisee, named Gamaliel, a doctor of the law, respected by all the people, commanded the men to be put forth a little while.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	35	And he said to them: Ye men of Israel, take heed to yourselves what you intend to do, as touching these men.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	36	For before these days rose up Theodas, affirming himself to be somebody, to whom a number of men, about four hundred, joined themselves: who was slain; and all that believed him were scattered, and brought to nothing.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	37	After this man, rose up Judas of Galilee, in the days of the enrolling, and drew away the people after him: he also perished; and all, even as many as consented to him, were dispersed.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	38	And now, therefore, I say to you, refrain from these men, and let them alone; for if this council or this work be of men, it will come to nought;
The Acts	Acts	51	5	39	But if it be of God, you cannot overthrow it, lest perhaps you be found even to fight against God. And they consented to him.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	40	And calling in the apostles, after they had scourged them, they charged them that they should not speak at all in the name of Jesus; and they dismissed them.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	41	And they indeed went from the presence of the council, rejoicing that they were accounted worthy to suffer reproach for the name of Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	5	42	And every day they ceased not in the temple, and from house to house, to teach and preach Christ Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	1	And in those days, the number of the disciples increasing, there arose a murmuring of the Greeks against the Hebrews, for that their widows were neglected in the daily ministration.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	2	Then the twelve calling together the multitude of the disciples, said: It is not reason that we should leave the word of God, and serve tables.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	3	Wherefore, brethren, look ye out among you seven men of good reputation, full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	4	But we will give ourselves continually to prayer, and to the ministry of the word.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	5	And the saying was liked by all the multitude. And they chose Stephen, a man full of faith, and of the Holy Ghost, and Philip, and Prochorus, and Nicanor, and Timon, and Parmenas, and Nicolas, a proselyte of Antioch.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	6	These they set before the apostles; and they praying, imposed hands upon them.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	7	And the word of the Lord increased; and the number of the disciples was multiplied in Jerusalem exceedingly: a great multitude also of the priests obeyed the faith.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	8	And Stephen, full of grace and fortitude, did great wonders and signs among the people.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	9	Now there arose some of that which is called the synagogue of the Libertines, and of the Cyrenians, and of the Alexandrians, and of them that were of Cilicia and Asia, disputing with Stephen.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	10	And they were not able to resist the wisdom and the spirit that spoke.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	11	Then they suborned men to say, they had heard him speak words of blasphemy against Moses and against God.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	12	And they stirred up the people, and the ancients, and the scribes; and running together, they took him, and brought him to the council.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	13	And they set up false witnesses, who said: This man ceaseth not to speak words against the holy place and the law.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	14	For we have heard him say, that this Jesus of Nazareth shall destroy this place, and shall change the traditions which Moses delivered unto us.
The Acts	Acts	51	6	15	And all that sat in the council, looking on him, saw his face as if it had been the face of an angel.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	1	Then the high priest said: Are these things so?
The Acts	Acts	51	7	2	Who said: Ye men, brethren, and fathers, hear. The God of glory appeared to our father Abraham, when he was in Mesopotamia, before he dwelt in Charan.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	3	And said to him: Go forth out of thy country, and from thy kindred, and come into the land which I shall shew thee.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	4	Then he went out of the land of the Chaldeans, and dwelt in Charan. And from thence, after his father was dead, he removed him into this land, wherein you now dwell.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	5	And he gave him no inheritance in it; no, not the pace of a foot: but he promised to give it him in possession, and to his seed after him, when as yet he had no child.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	6	And God said to him: That his seed should sojourn in a strange country, and that they should bring them under bondage, and treat them evil four hundred years.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	7	And the nation which they shall serve will I judge, said the Lord; and after these things they shall go out, and shall serve me in this place.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	8	And he gave him the covenant of circumcision, and so he begot Isaac, and circumcised him the eighth day; and Isaac begot Jacob; and Jacob the twelve patriarchs.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	9	And the patriarchs, through envy, sold Joseph into Egypt; and God was with him,
The Acts	Acts	51	7	10	And delivered him out of all his tribulations: and he gave him favour and wisdom in the sight of Pharao, the king of Egypt; and he appointed him governor over Egypt, and over all his house.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	11	Now there came a famine upon all Egypt and Chanaan, and great tribulation; and our fathers found no food.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	12	But when Jacob had heard that there was corn in Egypt, he sent our fathers first:
The Acts	Acts	51	7	13	And at the second time, Joseph was known by his brethren, and his kindred was made known to Pharao.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	14	And Joseph sending, called thither Jacob, his father, and all his kindred, seventy-five souls.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	15	So Jacob went down into Egypt; and he died, and our fathers.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	16	And they were translated into Sichem, and were laid in the sepulchre, that Abraham bought for a sum of money of the sons of Hemor, the son of Sichem.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	17	And when the time of the promise drew near, which God had promised to Abraham, the people increased, and were multiplied in Egypt,
The Acts	Acts	51	7	18	Till another king arose in Egypt, who knew not Joseph.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	19	This same dealing craftily with our race, afflicted our fathers, that they should expose their children, to the end they might not be kept alive.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	20	At the same time was Moses born, and he was acceptable to God: who was nourished three months in his father's house.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	21	And when he was exposed, Pharao's daughter took him up, and nourished him for her own son.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	22	And Moses was instructed in all the wisdom of the Egyptians; and he was mighty in his words and in his deeds.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	23	And when he was full forty years old, it came into his heart to visit his brethren, the children of Israel.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	24	And when he had seen one of them suffer wrong, he defended him; and striking the Egyptian, he avenged him who suffered the injury.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	25	And he thought that his brethren understood that God by his hand would save them; but they understood it not.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	26	And the day following, he shewed himself to them when they were at strife; and would have reconciled them in peace, saying: Men, ye are brethren; why hurt you one another?
The Acts	Acts	51	7	27	But he that did the injury to his neighbour thrust him away, saying: Who hath appointed thee prince and judge over us?
The Acts	Acts	51	7	28	What, wilt thou kill me, as thou didst yesterday kill the Egyptian?
The Acts	Acts	51	7	29	And Moses fled upon this word, and was a stranger in the land of Madian, where he begot two sons.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	30	And when forty years were expired, there appeared to him in the desert of mount Sina, an angel in a flame of fire in a bush.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	31	And Moses seeing it, wondered at the sight. And as he drew near to view it, the voice of the Lord came unto him, saying:
The Acts	Acts	51	7	32	I am the God of thy fathers; the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob. And Moses being terrified, durst not behold.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	33	And the Lord said to him: Loose the shoes from thy feet, for the place wherein thou standest, is holy ground.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	34	Seeing I have seen the affliction of my people which is in Egypt, and I have heard their groaning, and am come down to deliver them. And now come, and I will send thee into Egypt.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	35	This Moses, whom they refused, saying: Who hath appointed thee prince and judge? him God sent to be prince and redeemer by the hand of the angel who appeared to him in the bush.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	36	He brought them out, doing wonders and signs in the land of Egypt, and in the Red Sea, and in the desert forty years.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	37	This is that Moses who said to the children of Israel: A prophet shall God raise up to you of your own brethren, as myself: him shall you hear.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	38	This is he that was in the church in the wilderness, with the angel who spoke to him on mount Sina, and with our fathers; who received the words of life to give unto us.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	39	Whom our fathers would not obey; but thrust him away, and in their hearts turned back into Egypt,
The Acts	Acts	51	7	40	Saying to Aaron: Make us gods to go before us. For as for this Moses, who brought us out of the land of Egypt, we know not what is become of him.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	41	And they made a calf in those days, and offered sacrifices to the idol, and rejoiced in the works of their own hands.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	42	And God turned, and gave them up to serve the host of heaven, as it is written in the books of the prophets: Did you offer victims and sacrifices to me for forty years, in the desert, O house of Israel?
The Acts	Acts	51	7	43	And you took unto you the tabernacle of Moloch, and the star of your god Rempham, figures which you made to adore them. And I will carry you away beyond Babylon.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	44	The tabernacle of the testimony was with our fathers in the desert, as God ordained for them, speaking to Moses, that he should make it according to the form which he had seen.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	45	Which also our fathers receiving, brought in with Jesus, into the possession of the Gentiles, whom God drove out before the face of our fathers, unto the days of David.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	46	Who found grace before God, and desired to find a tabernacle for the God of Jacob.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	47	But Solomon built him a house.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	48	Yet the most High dwelleth not in houses made by hands, as the prophet saith:
The Acts	Acts	51	7	49	Heaven is my throne, and the earth my footstool. What house will you build me? saith the Lord; or what is the place of my resting?
The Acts	Acts	51	7	50	Hath not my hand made all these things?
The Acts	Acts	51	7	51	You stiffnecked and uncircumcised in heart and ears, you always resist the Holy Ghost: as your fathers did, so do you also.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	52	Which of the prophets have not your fathers persecuted? And they have slain them who foretold of the coming of the Just One; of whom you have been now the betrayers and murderers:
The Acts	Acts	51	7	53	Who have received the law by the disposition of angels, and have not kept it.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	54	Now hearing these things, they were cut to the heart, and they gnashed with their teeth at him.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	55	But he, being full of the Holy Ghost, looking up steadfastly to heaven, saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing on the right hand of God. And he said: Behold, I see the heavens opened, and the Son of man standing on the right hand of God.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	56	And they crying out with a loud voice, stopped their ears, and with one accord ran violently upon him.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	57	And casting him forth without the city, they stoned him; and the witnesses laid down their garments at the feet of a young man, whose name was Saul.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	58	And they stoned Stephen, invoking, and saying: Lord Jesus, receive my spirit.
The Acts	Acts	51	7	59	And falling on his knees, he cried with a loud voice, saying: Lord, lay not this sin to their charge. And when he had said this, he fell asleep in the Lord. And Saul was consenting to his death.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	1	And at that time there was raised a great persecution against the church which was at Jerusalem; and they were all dispersed through the countries of Judea, and Samaria, except the apostles.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	2	And devout men took order for Stephen's funeral, and made great mourning over him.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	3	But Saul made havock of the church, entering in from house to house, and dragging away men and women, committed them to prison.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	4	They therefore that were dispersed, went about preaching the word of God.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	5	And Philip going down to the city of Samaria, preached Christ unto them.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	6	And the people with one accord were attentive to those things which were said by Philip, hearing, and seeing the miracles which he did.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	7	For many of them who had unclean spirits, crying with a loud voice, went out.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	8	And many, taken with the palsy, and that were lame, were healed.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	9	There was therefore great joy in that city. Now there was a certain man named Simon, who before had been a magician in that city, seducing the people of Samaria, giving out that he was some great one:
The Acts	Acts	51	8	10	To whom they all gave ear, from the least to the greatest, saying: This man is the power of God, which is called great.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	11	And they were attentive to him, because, for a long time, he had bewitched them with his magical practices.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	12	But when they had believed Philip preaching of the kingdom of God, in the name of Jesus Christ, they were baptized, both men and women.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	13	Then Simon himself believed also; and being baptized, he adhered to Philip. And being astonished, wondered to see the signs and exceeding great miracles which were done.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	14	Now when the apostles, who were in Jerusalem, had heard that Samaria had received the word of God, they sent unto them Peter and John.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	15	Who, when they were come, prayed for them, that they might receive the Holy Ghost.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	16	For he was not as yet come upon any of them; but they were only baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	17	Then they laid their hands upon them, and they received the Holy Ghost.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	18	And when Simon saw, that by the imposition of the hands of the apostles, the Holy Ghost was given, he offered them money,
The Acts	Acts	51	8	19	Saying: Give me also this power, that on whomsoever I shall lay my hands, he may receive the Holy Ghost. But Peter said to him:
The Acts	Acts	51	8	20	Keep thy money to thyself, to perish with thee, because thou hast thought that the gift of God may be purchased with money.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	21	Thou hast no part nor lot in this matter. For thy heart is not right in the sight of God.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	22	Do penance therefore for this thy wickedness; and pray to God, that perhaps this thought of thy heart may be forgiven thee.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	23	For I see thou art in the gall of bitterness, and in the bonds of iniquity.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	24	Then Simon answering, said: Pray you for me to the Lord, that none of these things which you have spoken may come upon me.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	25	And they indeed having testified and preached the word of the Lord, returned to Jerusalem, and preached the gospel to many countries of the Samaritans.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	26	Now an angel of the Lord spoke to Philip, saying: Arise, go towards the south, to the way that goeth down from Jerusalem into Gaza: this is desert.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	27	And rising up, he went. And behold a man of Ethiopia, an eunuch, of great authority under Candace the queen of the Ethiopians, who had charge over all her treasures, had come to Jerusalem to adore.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	28	And he was returning, sitting in his chariot, and reading Isaias the prophet.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	29	And the Spirit said to Philip: Go near, and join thyself to this chariot.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	30	And Philip running thither, heard him reading the prophet Isaias. And he said: Thinkest thou that thou understandest what thou readest?
The Acts	Acts	51	8	31	Who said: And how can I, unless some man shew me? And he desired Philip that he would come up and sit with him.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	32	And the place of the scripture which he was reading was this: He was led as a sheep to the slaughter; and like a lamb without voice before his shearer, so openeth he not his mouth.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	33	In humility his judgment was taken away. His generation who shall declare, for his life shall be taken from the earth?
The Acts	Acts	51	8	34	And the eunuch answering Philip, said: I beseech thee, of whom doth the prophet speak this? of himself, or of some other man?
The Acts	Acts	51	8	35	Then Philip, opening his mouth, and beginning at this scripture, preached unto him Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	8	36	And as they went on their way, they came to a certain water; and the eunuch said: See, here is water: what doth hinder me from being baptized?
The Acts	Acts	51	8	37	And Philip said: If thou believest with all thy heart, thou mayest. And he answering, said: I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God.
