The Acts	Acts	51	17	10	But the brethren immediately sent away Paul and Silas by night unto Berea. Who, when they were come thither, went into the synagogue of the Jews.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	11	Now these were more noble than those in Thessalonica, who received the word with all eagerness, daily searching the scriptures, whether these things were so.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	12	And many indeed of them believed, and of honourable women that were Gentiles, and of men not a few.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	13	And when the Jews of Thessalonica had knowledge that the word of God was also preached by Paul at Berea, they came thither also, stirring up and troubling the multitude.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	14	And then immediately the brethren sent away Paul, to go unto the sea; but Silas and Timothy remained there.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	15	And they that conducted Paul, brought him as far as Athens; and receiving a commandment from him to Silas and Timothy, that they should come to him with all speed, they departed.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	16	Now whilst Paul waited for them at Athens, his spirit was stirred within him, seeing the city wholly given to idolatry.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	17	He disputed, therefore, in the synagogue with the Jews, and with them that served God, and in the marketplace, every day with them that were there.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	18	And certain philosophers of the Epicureans and of the Stoics disputed with him; and some said: What is it, that this word sower would say? But others: He seemeth to be a setter forth of new gods; because he preached to them Jesus and the resurrection.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	19	And taking him, they brought him to the Areopagus, saying: May we know what this new doctrine is, which thou speakest of?
The Acts	Acts	51	17	20	For thou bringest in certain new things to our ears. We would know therefore what these things mean.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	21	(Now all the Athenians, and strangers that were there, employed themselves in nothing else, but either in telling or in hearing some new thing.)
The Acts	Acts	51	17	22	But Paul standing in the midst of the Areopagus, said: Ye men of Athens, I perceive that in all things you are too superstitious.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	23	For passing by, and seeing your idols, I found an altar also, on which was written: To the unknown God. What therefore you worship, without knowing it, that I preach to you:
The Acts	Acts	51	17	24	God, who made the world, and all things therein; he, being Lord of heaven and earth, dwelleth not in temples made with hands;
The Acts	Acts	51	17	25	Neither is he served with men's hands, as though he needed any thing; seeing it is he who giveth to all life, and breath, and all things:
The Acts	Acts	51	17	26	And hath made of one, all mankind, to dwell upon the whole face of the earth, determining appointed times, and the limits of their habitation.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	27	That they should seek God, if happily they may feel after him or find him, although he be not far from every one of us:
The Acts	Acts	51	17	28	For in him we live, and move, and are; as some also of your own poets said: For we are also his offspring.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	29	Being therefore the offspring of God, we must not suppose the divinity to be like unto gold, or silver, or stone, the graving of art, and device of man.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	30	And God indeed having winked at the times of this ignorance, now declareth unto men, that all should everywhere do penance.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	31	Because he hath appointed a day wherein he will judge the world in equity, by the man whom he hath appointed; giving faith to all, by raising him up from the dead.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	32	And when they had heard of the resurrection of the dead, some indeed mocked, but others said: We will hear thee again concerning this matter.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	33	So Paul went out from among them.
The Acts	Acts	51	17	34	But certain men adhering to him, did believe; among whom was also Dionysius, the Areopagite, and a woman named Damaris, and others with them.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	1	After these things, departing from Athens, he came to Corinth.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	2	And finding a certain Jew, named Aquila, born in Pontus, lately come from Italy, with Priscilla his wife, (because that Claudius had commanded all Jews to depart from Rome,) he came to them.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	3	And because he was of the same trade, he remained with them, and wrought; (now they were tentmakers by trade.)
The Acts	Acts	51	18	4	And he reasoned in the synagogue every sabbath, bringing in the name of the Lord Jesus; and he persuaded the Jews and the Greeks.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	5	And when Silas and Timothy were come from Macedonia, Paul was earnest in preaching, testifying to the Jews, that Jesus is the Christ.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	6	But they gainsaying and blaspheming, he shook his garments, and said to them: Your blood be upon your own heads; I am clean: from henceforth I will go unto the Gentiles.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	7	And departing thence, he entered into the house of a certain man, named Titus Justus, one that worshipped God, whose house was adjoining to the synagogue.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	8	And Crispus, the ruler of the synagogue, believed in the Lord, with all his house; and many of the Corinthians hearing, believed, and were baptized.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	9	And the Lord said to Paul in the night, by a vision: Do not fear, but speak; and hold not thy peace,
The Acts	Acts	51	18	10	Because I am with thee: and no man shall set upon thee, to hurt thee; for I have much people in this city.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	11	And he stayed there a year and six months, teaching among them the word of God.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	12	But when Gallio was proconsul of Achaia, the Jews with one accord rose up against Paul, and brought him to the judgment seat,
The Acts	Acts	51	18	13	Saying: This man persuadeth men to worship God contrary to the law.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	14	And when Paul was beginning to open his mouth, Gallio said to the Jews: If it were some matter of injustice, or an heinous deed, O Jews, I should with reason bear with you.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	15	But if they be questions of word and names, and of your law, look you to it: I will not be judge of such things.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	16	And he drove them from the judgment seat.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	17	And all laying hold on Sosthenes, the ruler of the synagogue, beat him before the judgment seat; and Gallio cared for none of those things.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	18	But Paul, when he had stayed yet many days, taking his leave of the brethren, sailed thence into Syria (and with him Priscilla and Aquila), having shorn his head in Cenchrae: for he had a vow.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	19	And he came to Ephesus, and left them there. But he himself entering into the synagogue, disputed with the Jews.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	20	And when they desired him, that he would tarry a longer time, he consented not;
The Acts	Acts	51	18	21	But taking his leave, and saying: I will return to you again, God willing, he departed from Ephesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	22	And going down to Caesarea, he went up to Jerusalem, and saluted the church, and so came down to Antioch.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	23	And after he had spent some time there, he departed, and went through the country of Galatia and Phrygia, in order, confirming all the disciples.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	24	Now a certain Jew, named Apollo, born at Alexandria, an eloquent man, came to Ephesus, one mighty in the scriptures.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	25	This man was instructed in the way of the Lord; and being fervent in spirit, spoke, and taught diligently the things that are of Jesus, knowing only the baptism of John.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	26	This man therefore began to speak boldly in the synagogue. Whom when Priscilla and Aquila had heard, they took him to them, and expounded to him the way of the Lord more diligently.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	27	And whereas he was desirous to go to Achaia, the brethren exhorting, wrote to the disciples to receive him. Who, when he was come, helped them much who had believed.
The Acts	Acts	51	18	28	For with much vigour he convinced the Jews openly, shewing by the scriptures, that Jesus is the Christ.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	1	And it came to pass, while Apollo was at Corinth, that Paul having passed through the upper coasts, came to Ephesus, and found certain disciples.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	2	And he said to them: Have you received the Holy Ghost since ye believed? But they said to him: We have not so much as heard whether there be a Holy Ghost.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	3	And he said: In what then were you baptized? Who said: In John's baptism.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	4	Then Paul said: John baptized the people with the baptism of penance, saying: That they should believe in him who was to come after him, that is to say, in Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	5	Having heard these things, they were baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	6	And when Paul had imposed his hands on them, the Holy Ghost came upon them, and they spoke with tongues and prophesied.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	7	And all the men were about twelve.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	8	And entering into the synagogue, he spoke boldly for the space of three months, disputing and exhorting concerning the kingdom of God.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	9	But when some were hardened, and believed not, speaking evil of the way of the Lord, before the multitude, departing from them, he separated the disciples, disputing daily in the school of one Tyrannus.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	10	And this continued for the space of two years, so that all they who dwelt in Asia, heard the word of the Lord, both Jews and Gentiles.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	11	And God wrought by the hand of Paul more than common miracles.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	12	So that even there were brought from his body to the sick, handkerchiefs and aprons, and the diseases departed from them, and the wicked spirits went out of them.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	13	Now some also of the Jewish exorcists who went about, attempted to invoke over them that had evil spirits, the name of the Lord Jesus, saying: I conjure you by Jesus, whom Paul preacheth.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	14	And there were certain men, seven sons of Sceva, a Jew, a chief priest, that did this.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	15	But the wicked spirit, answering, said to them: Jesus I know, and Paul I know; but who are you?
The Acts	Acts	51	19	16	And the man in whom the wicked spirit was, leaping upon them, and mastering them both, prevailed against them, so that they fled out of that house naked and wounded.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	17	And this became known to all the Jews and the Gentiles that dwelt at Ephesus; and fear fell on them all, and the name of the Lord Jesus was magnified.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	18	And many of them that believed, came confessing and declaring their deeds.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	19	And many of them who had followed curious arts, brought together their books, and burnt them before all; and counting the price of them, they found the money to be fifty thousand pieces of silver.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	20	So mightily grew the word of God, and was confirmed.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	21	And when these things were ended, Paul purposed in the spirit, when he had passed through Macedonia and Achaia, to go to Jerusalem, saying: After I have been there, I must see Rome also.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	22	And sending into Macedonia two of them that ministered to him, Timothy and Erastus, he himself remained for a time in Asia.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	23	Now at that time there arose no small disturbance about the way of the Lord.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	24	For a certain man named Demetrius, a silversmith, who made silver temples for Diana, brought no small gain to the craftsmen;
The Acts	Acts	51	19	25	Whom he calling together, with the workmen of like occupation, said: Sirs, you know that our gain is by this trade;
The Acts	Acts	51	19	26	And you see and hear, that this Paul by persuasion hath drawn away a great multitude, not only of Ephesus, but almost of all Asia, saying: They are not gods which are made by hands.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	27	So that not only this our craft is in danger to be set at nought, but also the temple of great Diana shall be reputed for nothing; yea, and her majesty shall begin to be destroyed, whom all Asia and the world worshippeth.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	28	Having heard these things, they were full of anger, and cried out, saying: Great is Diana of the Ephesians.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	29	And the whole city was filled with confusion; and having caught Gaius and Aristarchus, men of Macedonia, Paul's companions, they rushed with one accord into the theatre.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	30	And when Paul would have entered in unto the people, the disciples suffered him not.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	31	And some also of the rulers of Asia, who were his friends, sent unto him, desiring that he would not venture himself into the theatre.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	32	Now some cried one thing, some another. For the assembly was confused, and the greater part knew not for what cause they were come together.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	33	And they drew forth Alexander out of the multitude, the Jews thrusting him forward. And Alexander beckoning with his hand for silence, would have given the people satisfaction.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	34	But as soon as they perceived him to be a Jew, all with one voice, for the space of about two hours, cried out: Great is Diana of the Ephesians.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	35	And when the town clerk had appeased the multitudes, he said: Ye men of Ephesus, what man is there that knoweth not that the city of the Ephesians is a worshipper of the great Diana, and of Jupiter's offspring.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	36	For as much therefore as these things cannot be contradicted, you ought to be quiet, and to do nothing rashly.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	37	For you have brought hither these men, who are neither guilty of sacrilege, nor of blasphemy against your goddess.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	38	But if Demetrius and the craftsmen that are with him, have a matter against any man, the courts of justice are open, and there are proconsuls: let them accuse one another.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	39	And if you inquire after any other matter, it may be decided in a lawful assembly.
The Acts	Acts	51	19	40	For we are even in danger to be called in question for this day's uproar, there being no man guilty (of whom we may give account) of this concourse. And when he had said these things, he dismissed the assembly.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	1	And after the tumult was ceased, Paul calling to him the disciples, and exhorting them, took his leave, and set forward to go into Macedonia.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	2	And when he had gone over those parts, and had exhorted them with many words, he came into Greece;
The Acts	Acts	51	20	3	Where, when he had spent three months, the Jews laid wait for him, as he was about to sail into Syria; so he took a resolution to return through Macedonia.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	4	And there accompanied him Sopater the son of Pyrrhus, of Berea; and of the Thessalonians, Aristarchus, and Secundus, and Gaius of Derbe, and Timothy; and of Asia, Tychicus and Trophimus.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	5	These going before, stayed for us at Troas.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	6	But we sailed from Philippi after the days of the Azymes, and came to them to Troas in five days, where we abode seven days.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	7	And on the first day of the week, when we were assembled to break bread, Paul discoursed with them, being to depart on the morrow: and he continued his speech until midnight.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	8	And there were a great number of lamps in the upper chamber where we were assembled.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	9	And a certain young man named Eutychus, sitting on the window, being oppressed with a deep sleep, (as Paul was long preaching,) by occasion of his sleep fell from the third loft down, and was taken up dead.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	10	To whom, when Paul had gone down, he laid himself upon him, and embracing him, said: Be not troubled, for his soul is in him.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	11	Then going up, and breaking bread and tasting, and having talked a long time to them, until daylight, so he departed.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	12	And they brought the youth alive, and were not a little comforted.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	13	But we, going aboard the ship, sailed to Assos, being there to take in Paul; for so he had appointed, himself purposing to travel by land.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	14	And when he had met with us at Assos, we took him in, and came to Mitylene.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	15	And sailing thence, the day following we came over against Chios; and the next day we arrived at Samos; and the day following we came to Miletus.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	16	For Paul had determined to sail by Ephesus, lest he should be stayed any time in Asia. For he hasted, if it were possible for him, to keep the day of Pentecost at Jerusalem.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	17	And sending from Miletus to Ephesus, he called the ancients of the church.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	18	And when they were come to him, and were together, he said to them: You know from the first day that I came into Asia, in what manner I have been with you, for all the time,
The Acts	Acts	51	20	19	Serving the Lord with all humility, and with tears, and temptations which befell me by the conspiracies of the Jews;
The Acts	Acts	51	20	20	How I have kept back nothing that was profitable to you, but have preached it to you, and taught you publicly, and from house to house,
The Acts	Acts	51	20	21	Testifying both to Jews and Gentiles penance towards God, and faith in our Lord Jesus Christ.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	22	And now, behold, being bound in the spirit, I go to Jerusalem: not knowing the things which shall befall me there:
The Acts	Acts	51	20	23	Save that the Holy Ghost in every city witnesseth to me, saying: That bands and afflictions wait for me at Jerusalem.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	24	But I fear none of these things, neither do I count my life more precious than myself, so that I may consummate my course and the ministry of the word which I received from the Lord Jesus, to testify the gospel of the grace of God.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	25	And now behold, I know that all you, among whom I have gone preaching the kingdom of God, shall see my face no more.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	26	Wherefore I take you to witness this day, that I am clear from the blood of all men;
The Acts	Acts	51	20	27	For I have not spared to declare unto you all the counsel of God.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	28	Take heed to yourselves, and to the whole flock, wherein the Holy Ghost hath placed you bishops, to rule the church of God, which he hath purchased with his own blood.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	29	I know that, after my departure, ravening wolves will enter in among you, not sparing the flock.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	30	And of your own selves shall arise men speaking perverse things, to draw away disciples after them.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	31	Therefore watch, keeping in memory, that for three years I ceased not, with tears to admonish every one of you night and day.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	32	And now I commend you to God, and to the word of his grace, who is able to build up, and to give an inheritance among all the sanctified.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	33	I have not coveted any man's silver, gold, or apparel, as
The Acts	Acts	51	20	34	You yourselves know: for such things as were needful for me and them that are with me, these hands have furnished.
The Acts	Acts	51	20	35	I have shewed you all things, how that so labouring you ought to support the weak, and to remember the word of the Lord Jesus, how he said: It is a more blessed thing to give, rather than to receive.
