The Acts	Acts	51	22	1	Men, brethren, and fathers, hear ye the account which I now give unto you.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	2	(And when they heard that he spoke to them in the Hebrew tongue, they kept the more silence.)
The Acts	Acts	51	22	3	And he saith: I am a Jew, born at Tarsus in Cilicia, but brought up in this city, at the feet of Gamaliel, taught according to the truth of the law of the fathers, zealous for the law, as also all you are this day:
The Acts	Acts	51	22	4	Who persecuted this way unto death, binding and delivering into prisons both men and women.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	5	As the high priest doth bear me witness, and all the ancients: from whom also receiving letters to the brethren, I went to Damascus, that I might bring them bound from thence to Jerusalem to be punished.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	6	And it came to pass, as I was going, and drawing nigh to Damascus at midday, that suddenly from heaven there shone round about me a great light:
The Acts	Acts	51	22	7	And falling on the ground, I heard a voice saying to me: Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?
The Acts	Acts	51	22	8	And I answered: Who art thou, Lord? And he said to me: I am Jesus of Nazareth, whom thou persecutest.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	9	And they that were with me, saw indeed the light, but they heard not the voice of him that spoke with me.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	10	And I said: What shall I do, Lord? And the Lord said to me: Arise, and go to Damascus; and there it shall be told thee of all things that thou must do.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	11	And whereas I did not see for the brightness of that light, being led by the hand by my companions, I came to Damascus.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	12	And one Ananias, a man according to the law, having testimony of all the Jews who dwelt there,
The Acts	Acts	51	22	13	Coming to me, and standing by me, said to me: Brother Saul, look up. And I the same hour looked upon him.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	14	But he said: The God of our fathers hath preordained thee that thou shouldst know his will, and see the Just One, and shouldst hear the voice from his mouth.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	15	For thou shalt be his witness to all men, of those things which thou hast seen and heard.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	16	And now why tarriest thou? Rise up, and be baptized, and wash away thy sins, invoking his name.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	17	And it came to pass, when I was come again to Jerusalem, and was praying in the temple, that I was in a trance,
The Acts	Acts	51	22	18	And saw him saying unto me: Make haste, and get thee quickly out of Jerusalem; because they will not receive thy testimony concerning me.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	19	And I said: Lord, they know that I cast into prison, and beat in every synagogue, them that believed in thee.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	20	And when the blood of Stephen thy witness was shed, I stood by and consented, and kept the garments of them that killed him.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	21	And he said to me: Go, for unto the Gentiles afar off, will I send thee.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	22	And they heard him until this word, and then lifted up their voice, saying: Away with such an one from the earth; for it is not fit that he should live.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	23	And as they cried out and threw off their garments, and cast dust into the air,
The Acts	Acts	51	22	24	The tribune commanded him to be brought into the castle, and that he should be scourged and tortured: to know for what cause they did so cry out against him.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	25	And when they had bound him with thongs, Paul saith to the centurion that stood by him: Is it lawful for you to scourge a man that is a Roman, and uncondemned?
The Acts	Acts	51	22	26	Which the centurion hearing, went to the tribune, and told him, saying: What art thou about to do? For this man is a Roman citizen.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	27	And the tribune coming, said to him: Tell me, art thou a Roman? But he said: Yea.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	28	And the tribune answered: I obtained the being free of this city with a great sum. And Paul said: But I was born so.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	29	Immediately therefore they departed from him that were about to torture him. The tribune also was afraid after he understood that he was a Roman citizen, and because he had bound him.
The Acts	Acts	51	22	30	But on the next day, meaning to know more diligently for what cause he was accused by the Jews, he loosed him, and commanded the priests to come together, and all the council: and bringing forth Paul, he set him before them.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	1	And Paul looking upon the council, said: Men, brethren, I have conversed with all good conscience before God until this present day.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	2	And the high priest Ananias commanded them that stood by him to strike him on the mouth.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	3	Then Paul said to him: God shall strike thee, thou whited wall. For sittest thou to judge me according to the law, and contrary to the law commandest me to be struck?
The Acts	Acts	51	23	4	And they that stood by said: Dost thou revile the high priest of God?
The Acts	Acts	51	23	5	And Paul said: I knew not, brethren, that he is the high priest. For it is written: Thou shalt not speak evil of the prince of thy people.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	6	And Paul knowing that the one part were Sadducees, and the other Pharisees, cried out in the council: Men, brethren, I am a Pharisee, the son of Pharisees: concerning the hope and resurrection of the dead I am called in question.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	7	And when he had so said, there arose a dissension between the Pharisees and the Sadducees; and the multitude was divided.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	8	For the Sadducees say that there is no resurrection, neither angel, nor spirit: but the Pharisees confess both.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	9	And there arose a great cry. And some of the Pharisees rising up, strove, saying: We find no evil in this man. What if a spirit hath spoken to him, or an angel?
The Acts	Acts	51	23	10	And when there arose a great dissension, the tribune fearing lest Paul should be pulled in pieces by them, commanded the soldiers to go down, and to take him by force from among them, and to bring him into the castle.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	11	And the night following the Lord standing by him, said: Be constant; for as thou hast testified of me in Jerusalem, so must thou bear witness also at Rome.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	12	And when day was come, some of the Jews gathered together, and bound themselves under a curse, saying, that they would neither eat, nor drink, till they killed Paul.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	13	And they were more than forty men that had made this conspiracy.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	14	Who came to the chief priests and the ancients, and said: We have bound ourselves under a great curse that we will eat nothing till we have slain Paul.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	15	Now therefore do you with the council signify to the tribune, that he bring him forth to you, as if you meant to know something more certain touching him. And we, before he come near, are ready to kill him.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	16	Which when Paul's sister's son had heard, of their lying in wait, he came and entered into the castle and told Paul.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	17	And Paul, calling to him one of the centurions, said: Bring this young man to the tribune, for he hath some thing to tell him.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	18	And he taking him, brought him to the tribune, and said: Paul, the prisoner, desired me to bring this young man unto thee, who hath some thing to say to thee.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	19	And the tribune taking him by the hand, went aside with him privately, and asked him: What is it that thou hast to tell me?
The Acts	Acts	51	23	20	And he said: The Jews have agreed to desire thee, that thou wouldst bring forth Paul tomorrow into the council, as if they meant to inquire some thing more certain touching him.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	21	But do not thou give credit to them; for there lie in wait for him more than forty men of them, who have bound themselves by oath neither to eat, nor to drink, till they have killed him: and they are now ready, looking for a promise from thee.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	22	The tribune therefore dismissed the young man, charging him that he should tell no man, that he had made known these things unto him.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	23	Then having called two centurions, he said to them: Make ready two hundred soldiers to go as far as Caesarea, and seventy horsemen, and two hundred spearmen for the third hour of the night:
The Acts	Acts	51	23	24	And provide beasts, that they may set Paul on, and bring him safe to Felix the governor.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	25	(For he feared lest perhaps the Jews might take him away by force and kill him, and he should afterwards be slandered, as if he was to take money.) And he wrote a letter after this manner:
The Acts	Acts	51	23	26	Claudius Lysias to the most excellent governor, Felix, greeting.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	27	This man being taken by the Jews, and ready to be killed by them, I rescued coming in with an army, understanding that he is a Roman:
The Acts	Acts	51	23	28	And meaning to know the cause which they objected unto him, I brought him forth into their council.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	29	Whom I found to be accused concerning questions of their law; but having nothing laid to his charge worthy of death or of bands.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	30	And when I was told of ambushes that they had prepared for him, I sent him to thee, signifying also to his accusers to plead before thee. Farewell.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	31	Then the soldiers, according as it was commanded them, taking Paul, brought him by night to Antipatris.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	32	And the next day, leaving the horsemen to go with him, they returned to the castle.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	33	Who, when they were come to Caesarea, and had delivered the letter to the governor, did also present Paul before him.
The Acts	Acts	51	23	34	And when he had read it, and had asked of what province he was, and understood that he was of Cilicia;
The Acts	Acts	51	23	35	I will hear thee, said he, when thy accusers come. And he commanded him to be kept in Herod's judgment hall.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	1	And after five days the high priest Ananias came down, with some of the ancients, and one Tertullus an orator, who went to the governor against Paul.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	2	And Paul being called for, Tertullus began to accuse him, saying: Whereas through thee we live in much peace, and many things are rectified by thy providence,
The Acts	Acts	51	24	3	We accept it always and in all places, most excellent Felix, with all thanksgiving.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	4	But that I be no further tedious to thee, I desire thee of thy clemency to hear us in few words.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	5	We have found this to be a pestilent man, and raising seditions among all the Jews throughout the world, and author of the sedition of the sect of the Nazarenes.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	6	Who also hath gone about to profane the temple: whom, we having apprehended, would also have judged according to our law.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	7	But Lysias the tribune coming upon us, with great violence took him away out of our hands;
The Acts	Acts	51	24	8	Commanding his accusers to come to thee: of whom thou mayest thyself, by examination, have knowledge of all these things, whereof we accuse him.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	9	And the Jews also added, and said that these things were so.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	10	Then Paul answered, (the governor making a sign to him to speak:) Knowing that for many years thou hast been judge over this nation, I will with good courage answer for myself.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	11	For thou mayest understand, that there are yet but twelve days, since I went up to adore in Jerusalem:
The Acts	Acts	51	24	12	And neither in the temple did they find me disputing with any man, or causing any concourse of the people, neither in the synagogues, nor in the city:
The Acts	Acts	51	24	13	Neither can they prove unto thee the things whereof they now accuse me.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	14	But this I confess to thee, that according to the way, which they call a heresy, so do I serve the Father and my God, believing all things which are written in the law and the prophets:
The Acts	Acts	51	24	15	Having hope in God, which these also themselves look for, that there shall be a resurrection of the just and unjust.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	16	And herein do I endeavour to have always a conscience without offence towards God, and towards men.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	17	Now after many years, I came to bring alms to my nation, and offerings, and vows.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	18	In which I was found purified in the temple: neither with multitude, nor with tumult.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	19	But certain Jews of Asia, who ought to be present before thee, and to accuse, if they had any thing against me:
The Acts	Acts	51	24	20	Or let these men themselves say, if they found in me any iniquity, when standing before the council,
The Acts	Acts	51	24	21	Except it be for this one voice only that I cried, standing among them, Concerning the resurrection of the dead am I judged this day by you.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	22	And Felix put them off, having most certain knowledge of this way, saying: When Lysias the tribune shall come down, I will hear you.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	23	And he commanded a centurion to keep him, and that he should be easy, and that he should not prohibit any of his friends to minister unto him.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	24	And after some days, Felix, coming with Drusilla his wife, who was a Jew, sent for Paul, and heard of him the faith, that is in Christ Jesus.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	25	And as he treated of justice, and chastity, and of the judgment to come, Felix being terrified, answered: For this time, go thy way: but when I have a convenient time, I will send for thee.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	26	Hoping also withal, that money should be given him by Paul; for which cause also oftentimes sending for him, he spoke with him.
The Acts	Acts	51	24	27	But when two years were ended, Felix had for successor Portius Festus. And Felix being willing to shew the Jews a pleasure, left Paul bound.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	1	Now when Festus was come into the province, after three days, he went up to Jerusalem from Caesarea.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	2	And the chief priests, and principal men of the Jews, went unto him against Paul: and they besought him,
The Acts	Acts	51	25	3	Requesting favour against him, that he would command him to be brought to Jerusalem, laying wait to kill him in the way.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	4	But Festus answered: That Paul was kept in Caesarea, and that he himself would very shortly depart thither.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	5	Let them, therefore, saith he, among you that are able, go down with me, and accuse him, if there be any crime in the man.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	6	And having tarried among them no more than eight or ten days, he went down to Caesarea, and the next day he sat in the judgment seat; and commanded Paul to be brought.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	7	Who being brought, the Jews stood about him, who were come down from Jerusalem, objecting many and grievous causes, which they could not prove;
The Acts	Acts	51	25	8	Paul making answer for himself: Neither against the law of the Jews, nor against the temple, nor against Caesar, have I offended in any thing.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	9	But Festus, willing to shew the Jews a pleasure, answering Paul, said: Wilt thou go up to Jerusalem, and there be judged of these things before me?
The Acts	Acts	51	25	10	Then Paul said: I stand at Caesar's judgment seat, where I ought to be judged. To the Jews I have done no injury, as thou very well knowest.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	11	For if I have injured them, or have committed any thing worthy of death, I refuse not to die. But if there be none of these things whereof they accuse me, no man may deliver me to them: I appeal to Caesar.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	12	Then Festus having conferred with the council, answered: Hast thou appealed to Caesar? To Caesar shalt thou go.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	13	And after some days, king Agrippa and Bernice came down to Caesarea to salute Festus.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	14	And as they tarried there many days, Festus told the king of Paul, saying: A certain man was left prisoner by Felix.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	15	About whom, when I was at Jerusalem, the chief priests, and the ancients of the Jews, came unto me, desiring condemnation against him.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	16	To whom I answered: It is not the custom of the Romans to condemn any man, before that he who is accused have his accusers present, and have liberty to make his answer, to clear himself of the things laid to his charge.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	17	When therefore they were come hither, without any delay, on the day following, sitting in the judgment seat, I commanded the man to be brought.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	18	Against whom, when the accusers stood up, they brought no accusation of things which I thought ill of:
The Acts	Acts	51	25	19	But had certain questions of their own superstition against him, and of one Jesus deceased, whom Paul affirmed to be alive.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	20	I therefore being in a doubt of this manner of question, asked him whether he would go to Jerusalem, and there be judged of these things.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	21	But Paul appealing to be reserved unto the hearing of Augustus, I commanded him to be kept, till I might send him to Caesar.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	22	And Agrippa said to Festus: I would also hear the man, myself. Tomorrow, said he, thou shalt hear him.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	23	And on the next day, when Agrippa and Bernice were come with great pomp, and had entered into the hall of audience, with the tribunes, and principal men of the city, at Festus' commandment, Paul was brought forth.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	24	And Festus saith: King Agrippa, and all ye men who are here present with us, you see this man, about whom all the multitude of the Jews dealt with me at Jerusalem, requesting and crying out that he ought not to live any longer.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	25	Yet have I found nothing that he hath committed worthy of death. But forasmuch as he himself hath appealed to Augustus, I have determined to send him.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	26	Of whom I have nothing certain to write to my lord. For which cause I have brought him forth before you, and especially before thee, O king Agrippa, that examination being made, I may have what to write.
The Acts	Acts	51	25	27	For it seemeth to me unreasonable to send a prisoner, and not to signify the things laid to his charge.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	1	Then Agrippa said to Paul: Thou art permitted to speak for thyself. Then Paul stretching forth his hand, began to make his answer.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	2	I think myself happy, O king Agrippa, that I am to answer for myself this day before thee, touching all the things whereof I am accused by the Jews.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	3	Especially as thou knowest all, both customs and questions that are among the Jews: Wherefore I beseech thee to hear me patiently.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	4	And my life indeed from my youth, which was from the beginning among my own nation in Jerusalem, all the Jews do know:
The Acts	Acts	51	26	5	Having known me from the beginning (if they will give testimony) that according to the most sure sect of our religion I lived a Pharisee.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	6	And now for the hope of the promise that was made by God to the fathers, do I stand subject to judgment:
The Acts	Acts	51	26	7	Unto which, our twelve tribes, serving night and day, hope to come. For which hope, O king, I am accused by the Jews.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	8	Why should it be thought a thing incredible, that God should raise the dead?
The Acts	Acts	51	26	9	And I indeed did formerly think, that I ought to do many things contrary to the name of Jesus of Nazareth.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	10	Which also I did at Jerusalem, and many of the saints did I shut up in prison, having received authority of the chief priests: and when they were put to death, I brought the sentence.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	11	And oftentimes punishing them, in every synagogue, I compelled them to blaspheme: and being yet more mad against them, I persecuted them even unto foreign cities.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	12	Whereupon when I was going to Damascus with authority and permission of the chief priest,
The Acts	Acts	51	26	13	At midday, O king, I saw in the way a light from heaven above the brightness of the sun, shining round about me, and them that were in company with me.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	14	And when we were all fallen down on the ground, I heard a voice speaking to me in the Hebrew tongue: Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me? It is hard for thee to kick against the goad.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	15	And I said: Who art thou, Lord? And the Lord answered: I am Jesus whom thou persecutest.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	16	But rise up, and stand upon thy feet: for to this end have I appeared to thee, that I may make thee a minister, and a witness of those things which thou hast seen, and of those things wherein I will appear to thee,
The Acts	Acts	51	26	17	Delivering thee from the people, and from the nations, unto which now I send thee:
The Acts	Acts	51	26	18	To open their eyes, that they may be converted from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan to God, that they may receive forgiveness of sins, and a lot among the saints, by the faith that is in me.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	19	Whereupon, O king Agrippa, I was not incredulous to the heavenly vision:
The Acts	Acts	51	26	20	But to them first that are at Damascus, and at Jerusalem, and unto all the country of Judea, and to the Gentiles did I preach, that they should do penance, and turn to God, doing works worthy of penance.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	21	For this cause the Jews, when I was in the temple, having apprehended me, went about to kill me.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	22	But being aided by the help of God, I stand unto this day, witnessing both to small and great, saying no other thing than those which the prophets, and Moses did say should come to pass:
The Acts	Acts	51	26	23	That Christ should suffer, and that he should be the first that should rise from the dead, and should shew light to the people, and to the Gentiles.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	24	As he spoke these things, and made his answer, Festus said with a loud voice: Paul, thou art beside thyself: much learning doth make thee mad.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	25	And Paul said: I am not mad, most excellent Festus, but I speak words of truth and soberness.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	26	For the king knoweth of these things, to whom also I speak with confidence. For I am persuaded that none of these things are hidden from him. For neither was any of these things done in a corner.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	27	Believest thou the prophets, O king Agrippa? I know that thou believest.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	28	And Agrippa said to Paul: In a little thou persuadest me to become a Christian.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	29	And Paul said: I would to God, that both in a little and in much, not only thou, but also all that hear me, this day, should become such as I also am, except these bands.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	30	And the king rose up, and the governor, and Bernice, and they that sat with them.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	31	And when they were gone aside, they spoke among themselves, saying: This man hath done nothing worthy of death or of bands.
The Acts	Acts	51	26	32	And Agrippa said to Festus: This man might have been set at liberty, if he had not appealed to Caesar.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	1	And when it was determined that he should sail into Italy, and that Paul, with the other prisoners, should be delivered to a centurion, named Julius, of the band Augusta,
The Acts	Acts	51	27	2	Going on board a ship of Adrumetum, we launched, meaning to sail by the coasts of Asia, Aristarchus, the Macedonian of Thessalonica, continuing with us.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	3	And the day following we came to Sidon. And Julius treating Paul courteously, permitted him to go to his friends, and to take care of himself.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	4	And when we had launched from thence, we sailed under Cyprus, because the winds were contrary.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	5	And sailing over the sea of Cilicia, and Pamphylia, we came to Lystra, which is in Lycia:
The Acts	Acts	51	27	6	And there the centurion finding a ship of Alexandria sailing into Italy, removed us into it.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	7	And when for many days we had sailed slowly, and were scarce come over against Gnidus, the wind not suffering us, we sailed near Crete by Salmone:
The Acts	Acts	51	27	8	And with much ado sailing by it, we came into a certain place, which is called Good-havens, nigh to which was the city of Thalassa.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	9	And when much time was spent, and when sailing now was dangerous, because the fast was now past, Paul comforted them,
The Acts	Acts	51	27	10	Saying to them: Ye men, I see that the voyage beginneth to be with injury and much damage, not only of the lading and ship, but also of our lives.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	11	But the centurion believed the pilot and the master of the ship, more than those things which were said by Paul.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	12	And whereas it was not a commodious haven to winter in, the greatest part gave counsel to sail thence, if by any means they might reach Phenice to winter there, which is a haven of Crete, looking towards the southwest and northwest.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	13	And the south wind gently blowing, thinking that they had obtained their purpose, when they had loosed from Asson, they sailed close by Crete.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	14	But not long after, there arose against it a tempestuous wind, called Euroaquilo.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	15	And when the ship was caught, and could not bear up against the wind, giving up the ship to the winds, we were driven.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	16	And running under a certain island, that is called Cauda, we had much work to come by the boat.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	17	Which being taken up, they used helps, undergirding the ship, and fearing lest they should fall into the quicksands, they let down the sail yard, and so were driven.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	18	And we being mightily tossed with the tempest, the next day they lightened the ship.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	19	And the third day they cast out with their own hands the tackling of the ship.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	20	And when neither sun nor stars appeared for many days, and no small storm lay on us, all hope of our being saved was now taken away.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	21	And after they had fasted a long time, Paul standing forth in the midst of them, said: You should indeed, O ye men, have hearkened unto me, and not have loosed from Crete, and have gained this harm and loss.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	22	And now I exhort you to be of good cheer. For there shall be no loss of any man's life among you, but only of the ship.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	23	For an angel of God, whose I am, and whom I serve, stood by me this night,
The Acts	Acts	51	27	24	Saying: Fear not, Paul, thou must be brought before Caesar; and behold, God hath given thee all them that sail with thee.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	25	Wherefore, sirs, be of good cheer; for I believe God that it shall so be, as it hath been told me.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	26	And we must come unto a certain island.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	27	But after the fourteenth night was come, as we were sailing in Adria, about midnight, the shipmen deemed that they discovered some country.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	28	Who also sounding, found twenty fathoms; and going on a little further, they found fifteen fathoms.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	29	Then fearing lest we should fall upon rough places, they cast four anchors out of the stern, and wished for the day.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	30	But as the shipmen sought to fly out of the ship, having let down the boat into the sea, under colour, as though they would have cast anchors out of the forepart of the ship,
The Acts	Acts	51	27	31	Paul said to the centurion, and to the soldiers: Except these stay in the ship, you cannot be saved.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	32	Then the soldiers cut off the ropes of the boat, and let her fall off.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	33	And when it began to be light, Paul besought them all to take meat, saying: This day is the fourteenth day that you have waited, and continued fasting, taking nothing.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	34	Wherefore I pray you to take some meat for your health's sake; for there shall not an hair of the head of any of you perish.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	35	And when he had said these things, taking bread, he gave thanks to God in the sight of them all; and when he had broken it, he began to eat.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	36	Then were they all of better cheer, and they also took some meat.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	37	And we were in all in the ship, two hundred threescore and sixteen souls.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	38	And when they had eaten enough, they lightened the ship, casting the wheat into the sea.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	39	And when it was day, they knew not the land; but they discovered a certain creek that had a shore, into which they minded, if they could, to thrust in the ship.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	40	And when they had taken up the anchors, they committed themselves to the sea, loosing withal the rudder bands; and hoisting up the mainsail to the wind, they made towards shore.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	41	And when we were fallen into a place where two seas met, they run the ship aground; and the forepart indeed, sticking fast, remained unmoveable: but the hinder part was broken with the violence of the sea.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	42	And the soldiers' counsel was, that they should kill the prisoners, lest any of them, swimming out, should escape.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	43	But the centurion, willing to save Paul, forbade it to be done; and he commanded that they who could swim, should cast themselves first into the sea, and save themselves, and get to land.
The Acts	Acts	51	27	44	And the rest, some they carried on boards, and some on those things that belonged to the ship. And so it came to pass, that every soul got safe to land.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	1	And when we had escaped, then we knew that the island was called Melita. But the barbarians shewed us no small courtesy.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	2	For kindling a fire, they refreshed us all, because of the present rain, and of the cold.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	3	And when Paul had gathered together a bundle of sticks, and had laid them on the fire, a viper coming out of the heat, fastened on his hand.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	4	And when the barbarians saw the beast hanging on his hand, they said one to another: Undoubtedly this man is a murderer, who though he hath escaped the sea, yet vengeance doth not suffer him to live.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	5	And he indeed shaking off the beast into the fire, suffered no harm.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	6	But they supposed that he would begin to swell up, and that he would suddenly fall down and die. But expecting long, and seeing that there came no harm to him, changing their minds, they said, that he was a god.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	7	Now in these places were possessions of the chief man of the island, named Publius, who receiving us, for three days entertained us courteously.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	8	And it happened that the father of Publius lay sick of a fever, and of a bloody flux. To whom Paul entered in; and when he had prayed, and laid his hands on him, he healed him.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	9	Which being done, all that had diseases in the island, came and were healed:
The Acts	Acts	51	28	10	Who also honoured us with many honours, and when we were to set sail, they laded us with such things as were necessary.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	11	And after three months, we sailed in a ship of Alexandria, that had wintered in the island, whose sign was the Castors.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	12	And when we were come to Syracusa, we tarried there three days.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	13	From thence, compassing by the shore, we came to Rhegium: and after one day, the south wind blowing, we came the second day to Puteoli;
The Acts	Acts	51	28	14	Where, finding brethren, we were desired to tarry with them seven days: and so we went to Rome.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	15	And from thence, when the brethren had heard of us, they came to meet us as far as Appii Forum, and the Three Taverns: whom when Paul saw, he gave thanks to God, and took courage.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	16	And when we were come to Rome, Paul was suffered to dwell by himself, with a soldier that kept him.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	17	And after the third day, he called together the chief of the Jews. And when they were assembled, he said to them: Men, brethren, I, having done nothing against the people, or the custom of our fathers, was delivered prisoner from Jerusalem into the hands of the Romans;
The Acts	Acts	51	28	18	Who, when they had examined me, would have released me, for that there was no cause of death in me;
The Acts	Acts	51	28	19	But the Jews contradicting it, I was constrained to appeal unto Caesar; not that I had any thing to accuse my nation of.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	20	For this cause therefore I desired to see you, and to speak to you. Because that for the hope of Israel, I am bound with this chain.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	21	But they said to him: We neither received letters concerning thee from Judea, neither did any of the brethren that came hither, relate or speak any evil of thee.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	22	But we desire to hear of thee what thou thinkest; for as concerning this sect, we know that it is everywhere contradicted.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	23	And when they had appointed him a day, there came very many to him unto his lodgings; to whom he expounded, testifying the kingdom of God, and persuading them concerning Jesus, out of the law of Moses and the prophets, from morning until evening.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	24	And some believed the things that were said; but some believed not.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	25	And when they agreed not among themselves, they departed, Paul speaking this one word: Well did the Holy Ghost speak to our fathers by Isaias the prophet,
The Acts	Acts	51	28	26	Saying: Go to this people, and say to them: With the ear you shall hear, and shall not understand; and seeing you shall see, and shall not perceive.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	27	For the heart of this people is grown gross, and with their ears have they heard heavily, and their eyes they have shut; lest perhaps they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and should be converted, and I should heal them.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	28	Be it known therefore to you, that this salvation of God is sent to the Gentiles, and they will hear it.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	29	And when he had said these things, the Jews went out from him, having much reasoning among themselves.
The Acts	Acts	51	28	30	And he remained two whole years in his own hired lodging; and he received all that came in to him,
The Acts	Acts	51	28	31	Preaching the kingdom of God, and teaching the things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, without prohibition.
Romans	Rom	52	1	1	Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ, called to be an apostle, separated unto the gospel of God,
Romans	Rom	52	1	2	Which he had promised before, by his prophets, in the holy scriptures,
Romans	Rom	52	1	3	Concerning his Son, who was made to him of the seed of David, according to the flesh,
Romans	Rom	52	1	4	Who was predestinated the Son of God in power, according to the spirit of sanctification, by the resurrection of our Lord Jesus Christ from the dead;
Romans	Rom	52	1	5	By whom we have received grace and apostleship for obedience to the faith, in all nations, for his name;
Romans	Rom	52	1	6	Among whom are you also the called of Jesus Christ:
Romans	Rom	52	1	7	To all that are at Rome, the beloved of God, called to be saints. Grace to you, and peace from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	1	8	First I give thanks to my God, through Jesus Christ, for you all, because your faith is spoken of in the whole world.
Romans	Rom	52	1	9	For God is my witness, whom I serve in my spirit in the gospel of his Son, that without ceasing I make a commemoration of you;
Romans	Rom	52	1	10	Always in my prayers making request, if by any means now at length I may have a prosperous journey, by the will of God, to come unto you.
Romans	Rom	52	1	11	For I long to see you, that I may impart unto you some spiritual grace, to strengthen you:
Romans	Rom	52	1	12	That is to say, that I may be comforted together in you, by that which is common to us both, your faith and mine.
Romans	Rom	52	1	13	And I would not have you ignorant, brethren, that I have often purposed to come unto you, (and have been hindered hitherto,) that I might have some fruit among you also, even as among other Gentiles.
Romans	Rom	52	1	14	To the Greeks and to the barbarians, to the wise and to the unwise, I am a debtor;
Romans	Rom	52	1	15	So (as much as is in me) I am ready to preach the gospel to you also that are at Rome.
Romans	Rom	52	1	16	For I am not ashamed of the gospel. For it is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth, to the Jew first, and to the Greek.
Romans	Rom	52	1	17	For the justice of God is revealed therein, from faith unto faith, as it is written: The just man liveth by faith.
Romans	Rom	52	1	18	For the wrath of God is revealed from heaven against all ungodliness and injustice of those men that detain the truth of God in injustice:
Romans	Rom	52	1	19	Because that which is known of God is manifest in them. For God hath manifested it unto them.
Romans	Rom	52	1	20	For the invisible things of him, from the creation of the world, are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made; his eternal power also, and divinity: so that they are inexcusable.
Romans	Rom	52	1	21	Because that, when they knew God, they have not glorified him as God, or given thanks; but became vain in their thoughts, and their foolish heart was darkened.
Romans	Rom	52	1	22	For professing themselves to be wise, they became fools.
Romans	Rom	52	1	23	And they changed the glory of the incorruptible God into the likeness of the image of a corruptible man, and of birds, and of fourfooted beasts, and of creeping things.
Romans	Rom	52	1	24	Wherefore God gave them up to the desires of their heart, unto uncleanness, to dishonour their own bodies among themselves.
Romans	Rom	52	1	25	Who changed the truth of God into a lie; and worshipped and served the creature rather than the Creator, who is blessed for ever. Amen.
Romans	Rom	52	1	26	For this cause God delivered them up to shameful affections. For their women have changed the natural use into that use which is against nature.
Romans	Rom	52	1	27	And, in like manner, the men also, leaving the natural use of the women, have burned in their lusts one towards another, men with men working that which is filthy, and receiving in themselves the recompense which was due to their error.
Romans	Rom	52	1	28	And as they liked not to have God in their knowledge, God delivered them up to a reprobate sense, to do those things which are not convenient;
Romans	Rom	52	1	29	Being filled with all iniquity, malice, fornication, avarice, wickedness, full of envy, murder, contention, deceit, malignity, whisperers,
Romans	Rom	52	1	30	Detractors, hateful to God, contumelious, proud, haughty, inventors of evil things, disobedient to parents,
Romans	Rom	52	1	31	Foolish, dissolute, without affection, without fidelity, without mercy.
Romans	Rom	52	1	32	Who, having known the justice of God, did not understand that they who do such things, are worthy of death; and not only they that do them, but they also that consent to them that do them.
Romans	Rom	52	2	1	Wherefore thou art inexcusable, O man, whosoever thou art that judgest. For wherein thou judgest another, thou condemnest thyself. For thou dost the same things which thou judgest.
Romans	Rom	52	2	2	For we know that the judgment of God is, according to truth, against them that do such things.
Romans	Rom	52	2	3	And thinkest thou this, O man, that judgest them who do such things, and dost the same, that thou shalt escape the judgment of God?
Romans	Rom	52	2	4	Or despisest thou the riches of his goodness, and patience, and longsuffering? Knowest thou not, that the benignity of God leadeth thee to penance?
Romans	Rom	52	2	5	But according to thy hardness and impenitent heart, thou treasurest up to thyself wrath, against the day of wrath, and revelation of the just judgment of God.
Romans	Rom	52	2	6	Who will render to every man according to his works.
Romans	Rom	52	2	7	To them indeed, who according to patience in good work, seek glory and honour and incorruption, eternal life:
Romans	Rom	52	2	8	But to them that are contentious, and who obey not the truth, but give credit to iniquity, wrath and indignation.
Romans	Rom	52	2	9	Tribulation and anguish upon every soul of man that worketh evil, of the Jew first, and also of the Greek.
Romans	Rom	52	2	10	But glory, and honour, and peace to every one that worketh good, to the Jew first, and also to the Greek.
Romans	Rom	52	2	11	For there is no respect of persons with God.
Romans	Rom	52	2	12	For whosoever have sinned without the law, shall perish without the law; and whosoever have sinned in the law, shall be judged by the law.
Romans	Rom	52	2	13	For not the hearers of the law are just before God, but the doers of the law shall be justified.
Romans	Rom	52	2	14	For when the Gentiles, who have not the law, do by nature those things that are of the law; these having not the law are a law to themselves:
Romans	Rom	52	2	15	Who shew the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience bearing witness to them, and their thoughts between themselves accusing, or also defending one another,
Romans	Rom	52	2	16	In the day when God shall judge the secrets of men by Jesus Christ, according to my gospel.
Romans	Rom	52	2	17	But if thou art called a Jew and restest in the law, and makest thy boast of God,
Romans	Rom	52	2	18	And knowest his will, and approvest the more profitable things, being instructed by the law,
Romans	Rom	52	2	19	Art confident that thou thyself art a guide of the blind, a light of them that are in darkness,
Romans	Rom	52	2	20	An instructor of the foolish, a teacher of infants, having the form of knowledge and of truth in the law.
Romans	Rom	52	2	21	Thou therefore that teachest another, teachest not thyself: thou that preachest that men should not steal, stealest:
Romans	Rom	52	2	22	Thou that sayest, men should not commit adultery, committest adultery: thou that abhorrest idols, committest sacrilege:
Romans	Rom	52	2	23	Thou that makest thy boast of the law, by transgression of the law dishonourest God.
Romans	Rom	52	2	24	(For the name of God through you is blasphemed among the Gentiles, as it is written.)
Romans	Rom	52	2	25	Circumcision profiteth indeed, if thou keep the law; but if thou be a transgressor of the law, thy circumcision is made uncircumcision.
Romans	Rom	52	2	26	If, then, the uncircumcised keep the justices of the law, shall not this uncircumcision be counted for circumcision?
Romans	Rom	52	2	27	And shall not that which by nature is uncircumcision, if it fulfill the law, judge thee, who by the letter and circumcision art a transgressor of the law?
Romans	Rom	52	2	28	For it is not he is a Jew, who is so outwardly; nor is that circumcision which is outwardly in the flesh:
Romans	Rom	52	2	29	But he is a Jew, that is one inwardly; and the circumcision is that of the heart, in the spirit, not in the letter; whose praise is not of men, but of God.
Romans	Rom	52	3	1	What advantage then hath the Jew, or what is the profit of circumcision?
Romans	Rom	52	3	2	Much every way. First indeed, because the words of God were committed to them.
Romans	Rom	52	3	3	For what if some of them have not believed? shall their unbelief make the faith of God without effect? God forbid.
Romans	Rom	52	3	4	But God is true; and every man a liar, as it is written, That thou mayest be justified in thy words, and mayest overcome when thou art judged.
Romans	Rom	52	3	5	But if our injustice commend the justice of God, what shall we say? Is God unjust, who executeth wrath?
Romans	Rom	52	3	6	(I speak according to man.) God forbid: otherwise how shall God judge this world?
Romans	Rom	52	3	7	For if the truth of God hath more abounded through my lie, unto his glory, why am I also yet judged as a sinner?
Romans	Rom	52	3	8	And not rather (as we are slandered, and as some affirm that we say) let us do evil, that there may come good? whose damnation is just.
Romans	Rom	52	3	9	What then? Do we excel them? No, not so. For we have charged both Jews, and Greeks, that they are all under sin.
Romans	Rom	52	3	10	As it is written: There is not any man just.
Romans	Rom	52	3	11	There is none that understandeth, there is none that seeketh after God.
Romans	Rom	52	3	12	All have turned out of the way; they are become unprofitable together: there is none that doth good, there is not so much as one.
Romans	Rom	52	3	13	Their throat is an open sepulchre; with their tongues they have dealt deceitfully. The venom of asps is under their lips.
Romans	Rom	52	3	14	Whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness:
Romans	Rom	52	3	15	Their feet swift to shed blood:
Romans	Rom	52	3	16	Destruction and misery in their ways:
Romans	Rom	52	3	17	And the way of peace they have not known:
Romans	Rom	52	3	18	There is no fear of God before their eyes.
Romans	Rom	52	3	19	Now we know, that what things soever the law speaketh, it speaketh to them that are in the law; that every mouth may be stopped, and all the world may be made subject to God.
Romans	Rom	52	3	20	Because by the works of the law no flesh shall be justified before him. For by the law is the knowledge of sin.
Romans	Rom	52	3	21	But now without the law the justice of God is made manifest, being witnessed by the law and the prophets.
Romans	Rom	52	3	22	Even the justice of God, by faith of Jesus Christ, unto all and upon all them that believe in him: for there is no distinction:
Romans	Rom	52	3	23	For all have sinned, and do need the glory of God.
Romans	Rom	52	3	24	Being justified freely by his grace, through the redemption, that is in Christ Jesus,
Romans	Rom	52	3	25	Whom God hath proposed to be a propitiation, through faith in his blood, to the shewing of his justice, for the remission of former sins,
Romans	Rom	52	3	26	Through the forbearance of God, for the shewing of his justice in this time; that he himself may be just, and the justifier of him, who is of the faith of Jesus Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	3	27	Where is then thy boasting? It is excluded. By what law? Of works? No, but by the law of faith.
Romans	Rom	52	3	28	For we account a man to be justified by faith, without the works of the law.
Romans	Rom	52	3	29	Is he the God of the Jews only? Is he not also of the Gentiles? Yes, of the Gentiles also.
Romans	Rom	52	3	30	For it is one God, that justifieth circumcision by faith, and uncircumcision through faith.
Romans	Rom	52	3	31	Do we, then, destroy the law through faith? God forbid: but we establish the law.
Romans	Rom	52	4	1	What shall we say then that Abraham hath found, who is our father according to the flesh.
Romans	Rom	52	4	2	For if Abraham were justified by works, he hath whereof to glory, but not before God.
Romans	Rom	52	4	3	For what saith the scripture? Abraham believed God, and it was reputed to him unto justice.
Romans	Rom	52	4	4	Now to him that worketh, the reward is not reckoned according to grace, but according to debt.
Romans	Rom	52	4	5	But to him that worketh not, yet believeth in him that justifieth the ungodly, his faith is reputed to justice, according to the purpose of the grace of God.
Romans	Rom	52	4	6	As David also termeth the blessedness of a man, to whom God reputeth justice without works:
Romans	Rom	52	4	7	Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven, and whose sins are covered.
Romans	Rom	52	4	8	Blessed is the man to whom the Lord hath not imputed sin.
Romans	Rom	52	4	9	This blessedness then, doth it remain in the circumcision only, or in the uncircumcision also? For we say that unto Abraham faith was reputed to justice.
Romans	Rom	52	4	10	How then was it reputed? When he was in circumcision, or in uncircumcision? Not in circumcision, but in uncircumcision.
Romans	Rom	52	4	11	And he received the sign of circumcision, a seal of the justice of the faith, which he had, being uncircumcised; that he might be the father of all them that believe, being uncircumcised, that unto them also it may be reputed to justice:
Romans	Rom	52	4	12	And might be the father of circumcision; not to them only, that are of the circumcision, but to them also that follow the steps of the faithful, that is in the uncircumcision of our father Abraham.
Romans	Rom	52	4	13	For not through the law was the promise to Abraham, or to his seed, that he should be heir of the world; but through the justice of faith.
Romans	Rom	52	4	14	For if they who are of the law be heirs, faith is made void, the promise is made of no effect.
Romans	Rom	52	4	15	For the law worketh wrath. For where there is no law, neither is there transgression.
Romans	Rom	52	4	16	Therefore is it of faith, that according to grace the promise might be firm to all the seed; not to that only which is of the law, but to that also which is of the faith of Abraham, who is the father of us all,
Romans	Rom	52	4	17	(As it is written: I have made thee a father of many nations,) before God, whom he believed, who quickeneth the dead; and calleth those things that are not, as those that are.
Romans	Rom	52	4	18	Who against hope believed in hope; that he might be made the father of many nations, according to that which was said to him: So shall thy seed be.
Romans	Rom	52	4	19	And he was not weak in faith; neither did he consider his own body now dead, whereas he was almost an hundred years old, nor the dead womb of Sara.
Romans	Rom	52	4	20	In the promise also of God he staggered not by distrust; but was strengthened in faith, giving glory to God:
Romans	Rom	52	4	21	Most fully knowing, that whatsoever he has promised, he is able also to perform.
Romans	Rom	52	4	22	And therefore it was reputed to him unto justice.
Romans	Rom	52	4	23	Now it is not written only for him, that it was reputed to him unto justice,
Romans	Rom	52	4	24	But also for us, to whom it shall be reputed, if we believe in him, that raised up Jesus Christ, our Lord, from the dead,
Romans	Rom	52	4	25	Who was delivered up for our sins, and rose again for our justification.
Romans	Rom	52	5	1	Being justified therefore by faith, let us have peace with God, through our Lord Jesus Christ:
Romans	Rom	52	5	2	By whom also we have access through faith into this grace, wherein we stand, and glory in the hope of the glory of the sons of God.
Romans	Rom	52	5	3	And not only so; but we glory also in tribulations, knowing that tribulation worketh patience;
Romans	Rom	52	5	4	And patience trial; and trial hope;
Romans	Rom	52	5	5	And hope confoundeth not: because the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts, by the Holy Ghost, who is given to us.
Romans	Rom	52	5	6	For why did Christ, when as yet we were weak, according to the time, die for the ungodly?
Romans	Rom	52	5	7	For scarce for a just man will one die; yet perhaps for a good man some one would dare to die.
Romans	Rom	52	5	8	But God commendeth his charity towards us; because when as yet we were sinners, according to the time,
Romans	Rom	52	5	9	Christ died for us; much more therefore, being now justified by his blood, shall we be saved from wrath through him.
Romans	Rom	52	5	10	For if, when we were enemies, we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son; much more, being reconciled, shall we be saved by his life.
Romans	Rom	52	5	11	And not only so; but also we glory in God, through our Lord Jesus Christ, by whom we have now received reconciliation.
Romans	Rom	52	5	12	Wherefore as by one man sin entered into this world, and by sin death; and so death passed upon all men, in whom all have sinned.
Romans	Rom	52	5	13	For until the law sin was in the world; but sin was not imputed, when the law was not.
Romans	Rom	52	5	14	But death reigned from Adam unto Moses, even over them also who have not sinned after the similitude of the transgression of Adam, who is a figure of him who was to come.
Romans	Rom	52	5	15	But not as the offence, so also the gift. For if by the offence of one, many died; much more the grace of God, and the gift, by the grace of one man, Jesus Christ, hath abounded unto many.
Romans	Rom	52	5	16	And not as it was by one sin, so also is the gift. For judgment indeed was by one unto condemnation; but grace is of many offences, unto justification.
Romans	Rom	52	5	17	For if by one man's offence death reigned through one; much more they who receive abundance of grace, and of the gift, and of justice, shall reign in life through one, Jesus Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	5	18	Therefore, as by the offence of one, unto all men to condemnation; so also by the justice of one, unto all men to justification of life.
Romans	Rom	52	5	19	For as by the disobedience of one man, many were made sinners; so also by the obedience of one, many shall be made just.
Romans	Rom	52	5	20	Now the law entered in, that sin might abound. And where sin abounded, grace did more abound.
Romans	Rom	52	5	21	That as sin hath reigned to death; so also grace might reign by justice unto life everlasting, through Jesus Christ our Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	6	1	What shall we say, then? shall we continue in sin, that grace may abound?
Romans	Rom	52	6	2	God forbid. For we that are dead to sin, how shall we live any longer therein?
Romans	Rom	52	6	3	Know you not that all we, who are baptized in Christ Jesus, are baptized in his death?
Romans	Rom	52	6	4	For we are buried together with him by baptism into death; that as Christ is risen from the dead by the glory of the Father, so we also may walk in newness of life.
Romans	Rom	52	6	5	For if we have been planted together in the likeness of his death, we shall be also in the likeness of his resurrection.
Romans	Rom	52	6	6	Knowing this, that our old man is crucified with him, that the body of sin may be destroyed, to the end that we may serve sin no longer.
Romans	Rom	52	6	7	For he that is dead is justified from sin.
Romans	Rom	52	6	8	Now if we be dead with Christ, we believe that we shall live also together with Christ:
Romans	Rom	52	6	9	Knowing that Christ rising again from the dead, dieth now no more, death shall no more have dominion over him.
Romans	Rom	52	6	10	For in that he died to sin, he died once; but in that he liveth, he liveth unto God:
Romans	Rom	52	6	11	So do you also reckon, that you are dead to sin, but alive unto God, in Christ Jesus our Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	6	12	Let no sin therefore reign in your mortal body, so as to obey the lusts thereof.
Romans	Rom	52	6	13	Neither yield ye your members as instruments of iniquity unto sin; but present yourselves to God, as those that are alive from the dead, and your members as instruments of justice unto God.
Romans	Rom	52	6	14	For sin shall not have dominion over you; for you are not under the law, but under grace.
Romans	Rom	52	6	15	What then? Shall we sin, because we are not under the law, but under grace? God forbid.
Romans	Rom	52	6	16	Know you not, that to whom you yield yourselves servants to obey, his servants you are whom you obey, whether it be of sin unto death, or of obedience unto justice.
Romans	Rom	52	6	17	But thanks be to God, that you were the servants of sin, but have obeyed from the heart, unto that form of doctrine, into which you have been delivered.
Romans	Rom	52	6	18	Being then freed from sin, we have been made servants of justice.
Romans	Rom	52	6	19	I speak an human thing, because of the infirmity of your flesh. For as you have yielded your members to serve uncleanness and iniquity, unto iniquity; so now yield your members to serve justice, unto sanctification.
Romans	Rom	52	6	20	For when you were the servants of sin, you were free men to justice.
Romans	Rom	52	6	21	What fruit therefore had you then in those things, of which you are now ashamed? For the end of them is death.
Romans	Rom	52	6	22	But now being made free from sin, and become servants to God, you have your fruit unto sanctification, and the end life everlasting.
Romans	Rom	52	6	23	For the wages of sin is death. But the grace of God, life everlasting, in Christ Jesus our Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	7	1	Know you not, brethren, (for I speak to them that know the law,) that the law hath dominion over a man, as long as it liveth?
Romans	Rom	52	7	2	For the woman that hath an husband, whilst her husband liveth is bound to the law. But if her husband be dead, she is loosed from the law of her husband.
Romans	Rom	52	7	3	Therefore, whilst her husband liveth, she shall be called an adulteress, if she be with another man: but if her husband be dead, she is delivered from the law of her husband; so that she is not an adulteress, if she be with another man.
Romans	Rom	52	7	4	Therefore, my brethren, you also are become dead to the law, by the body of Christ; that you may belong to another, who is risen again from the dead, that we may bring forth fruit to God.
Romans	Rom	52	7	5	For when we were in the flesh, the passions of sins, which were by the law, did work in our members, to bring forth fruit unto death.
Romans	Rom	52	7	6	But now we are loosed from the law of death, wherein we were detained; so that we should serve in newness of spirit, and not in the oldness of the letter.
Romans	Rom	52	7	7	What shall we say, then? Is the law sin? God forbid. But I do not know sin, but by the law; for I had not known concupiscence, if the law did not say: Thou shalt not covet.
Romans	Rom	52	7	8	But sin taking occasion by the commandment, wrought in me all manner of concupiscence. For without the law sin was dead.
Romans	Rom	52	7	9	And I lived some time without the law. But when the commandment came, sin revived,
Romans	Rom	52	7	10	And I died. And the commandment that was ordained to life, the same was found to be unto death to me.
Romans	Rom	52	7	11	For sin, taking occasion by the commandment, seduced me, and by it killed me.
Romans	Rom	52	7	12	Wherefore the law indeed is holy, and the commandment holy, and just, and good.
Romans	Rom	52	7	13	Was that then which is good, made death unto me? God forbid. But sin, that it may appear sin, by that which is good, wrought death in me; that sin, by the commandment, might become sinful above measure.
Romans	Rom	52	7	14	For we know that the law is spiritual; but I am carnal, sold under sin.
Romans	Rom	52	7	15	For that which I work, I understand not. For I do not that good which I will; but the evil which I hate, that I do.
Romans	Rom	52	7	16	If then I do that which I will not, I consent to the law, that it is good.
Romans	Rom	52	7	17	Now then it is no more I that do it, but sin that dwelleth in me.
Romans	Rom	52	7	18	For I know that there dwelleth not in me, that is to say, in my flesh, that which is good. For to will, is present with me; but to accomplish that which is good, I find not.
Romans	Rom	52	7	19	For the good which I will, I do not; but the evil which I will not, that I do.
Romans	Rom	52	7	20	Now if I do that which I will not, it is no more I that do it, but sin that dwelleth in me.
Romans	Rom	52	7	21	I find then a law, that when I have a will to do good, evil is present with me.
Romans	Rom	52	7	22	For I am delighted with the law of God, according to the inward man:
Romans	Rom	52	7	23	But I see another law in my members, fighting against the law of my mind, and captivating me in the law of sin, that is in my members.
Romans	Rom	52	7	24	Unhappy man that I am, who shall deliver me from the body of this death?
Romans	Rom	52	7	25	The grace of God, by Jesus Christ our Lord. Therefore, I myself, with the mind serve the law of God; but with the flesh, the law of sin.
Romans	Rom	52	8	1	There is now therefore no condemnation to them that are in Christ Jesus, who walk not according to the flesh.
Romans	Rom	52	8	2	For the law of the spirit of life, in Christ Jesus, hath delivered me from the law of sin and of death.
Romans	Rom	52	8	3	For what the law could not do, in that it was weak through the flesh; God sending his own Son, in the likeness of sinful flesh and of sin, hath condemned sin in the flesh;
Romans	Rom	52	8	4	That the justification of the law might be fulfilled in us, who walk not according to the flesh, but according to the spirit.
Romans	Rom	52	8	5	For they that are according to the flesh, mind the things that are of the flesh; but they that are according to the spirit, mind the things that are of the spirit.
Romans	Rom	52	8	6	For the wisdom of the flesh is death; but the wisdom of the spirit is life and peace.
Romans	Rom	52	8	7	Because the wisdom of the flesh is an enemy to God; for it is not subject to the law of God, neither can it be.
Romans	Rom	52	8	8	And they who are in the flesh, cannot please God.
Romans	Rom	52	8	9	But you are not in the flesh, but in the spirit, if so be that the Spirit of God dwell in you. Now if any man have not the Spirit of Christ, he is none of his.
Romans	Rom	52	8	10	And if Christ be in you, the body indeed is dead, because of sin; but the spirit liveth, because of justification.
Romans	Rom	52	8	11	And if the Spirit of him that raised up Jesus from the dead, dwell in you; he that raised up Jesus Christ from the dead, shall quicken also your mortal bodies, because of his Spirit that dwelleth in you.
Romans	Rom	52	8	12	Therefore, brethren, we are debtors, not to the flesh, to live according to the flesh.
Romans	Rom	52	8	13	For if you live according to the flesh, you shall die: but if by the Spirit you mortify the deeds of the flesh, you shall live.
Romans	Rom	52	8	14	For whosoever are led by the Spirit of God, they are the sons of God.
Romans	Rom	52	8	15	For you have not received the spirit of bondage again in fear; but you have received the spirit of adoption of sons, whereby we cry: Abba (Father).
Romans	Rom	52	8	16	For the Spirit himself giveth testimony to our spirit, that we are the sons of God.
Romans	Rom	52	8	17	And if sons, heirs also; heirs indeed of God, and joint heirs with Christ: yet so, if we suffer with him, that we may be also glorified with him.
Romans	Rom	52	8	18	For I reckon that the sufferings of this time are not worthy to be compared with the glory to come, that shall be revealed in us.
Romans	Rom	52	8	19	For the expectation of the creature waiteth for the revelation of the sons of God.
Romans	Rom	52	8	20	For the creature was made subject to vanity, not willingly, but by reason of him that made it subject, in hope:
Romans	Rom	52	8	21	Because the creature also itself shall be delivered from the servitude of corruption, into the liberty of the glory of the children of God.
Romans	Rom	52	8	22	For we know that every creature groaneth and travaileth in pain, even till now.
Romans	Rom	52	8	23	And not only it, but ourselves also, who have the firstfruits of the Spirit, even we ourselves groan within ourselves, waiting for the adoption of the sons of God, the redemption of our body.
Romans	Rom	52	8	24	For we are saved by hope. But hope that is seen, is not hope. For what a man seeth, why doth he hope for?
Romans	Rom	52	8	25	But if we hope for that which we see not, we wait for it with patience.
Romans	Rom	52	8	26	Likewise the Spirit also helpeth our infirmity. For we know not what we should pray for as we ought; but the Spirit himself asketh for us with unspeakable groanings.
Romans	Rom	52	8	27	And he that searcheth the hearts, knoweth what the Spirit desireth; because he asketh for the saints according to God.
Romans	Rom	52	8	28	And we know that to them that love God, all things work together unto good, to such as, according to his purpose, are called to be saints.
Romans	Rom	52	8	29	For whom he foreknew, he also predestinated to be made conformable to the image of his Son; that he might be the firstborn amongst many brethren.
Romans	Rom	52	8	30	And whom he predestinated, them he also called. And whom he called, them he also justified. And whom he justified, them he also glorified.
Romans	Rom	52	8	31	What shall we then say to these things? If God be for us, who is against us?
Romans	Rom	52	8	32	He that spared not even his own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how hath he not also, with him, given us all things?
Romans	Rom	52	8	33	Who shall accuse against the elect of God? God that justifieth.
Romans	Rom	52	8	34	Who is he that shall condemn? Christ Jesus that died, yea that is risen also again; who is at the right hand of God, who also maketh intercession for us.
Romans	Rom	52	8	35	Who then shall separate us from the love of Christ? Shall tribulation? or distress? or famine? or nakedness? or danger? or persecution? or the sword?
Romans	Rom	52	8	36	(As it is written: For thy sake we are put to death all the day long. We are accounted as sheep for the slaughter.)
Romans	Rom	52	8	37	But in all these things we overcome, because of him that hath loved us.
Romans	Rom	52	8	38	For I am sure that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor powers, nor things present, nor things to come, nor might,
Romans	Rom	52	8	39	Nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, shall be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	9	1	I speak the truth in Christ, I lie not, my conscience bearing me witness in the Holy Ghost:
Romans	Rom	52	9	2	That I have great sadness, and continual sorrow in my heart.
Romans	Rom	52	9	3	For I wished myself to be an anathema from Christ, for my brethren, who are my kinsmen according to the flesh,
Romans	Rom	52	9	4	Who are Israelites, to whom belongeth the adoption as of children, and the glory, and the testament, and the giving of the law, and the service of God, and the promises:
Romans	Rom	52	9	5	Whose are the fathers, and of whom is Christ, according to the flesh, who is over all things, God blessed for ever. Amen.
Romans	Rom	52	9	6	Not as though the word of God hath miscarried. For all are not Israelites that are of Israel:
Romans	Rom	52	9	7	Neither are all they that are the seed of Abraham, children; but in Isaac shall thy seed be called:
Romans	Rom	52	9	8	That is to say, not they that are the children of the flesh, are the children of God; but they, that are the children of the promise, are accounted for the seed.
Romans	Rom	52	9	9	For this is the word of promise: According to this time will I come; and Sara shall have a son.
Romans	Rom	52	9	10	And not only she. But when Rebecca also had conceived at once, of Isaac our father.
Romans	Rom	52	9	11	For when the children were not yet born, nor had done any good or evil (that the purpose of God, according to election, might stand,)
Romans	Rom	52	9	12	Not of works, but of him that calleth, it was said to her: The elder shall serve the younger.
Romans	Rom	52	9	13	As it is written: Jacob I have loved, but Esau I have hated.
Romans	Rom	52	9	14	What shall we say then? Is there injustice with God? God forbid.
Romans	Rom	52	9	15	For he saith to Moses: I will have mercy on whom I will have mercy; and I will shew mercy to whom I will shew mercy.
Romans	Rom	52	9	16	So then it is not of him that willeth, nor of him that runneth, but of God that sheweth mercy.
Romans	Rom	52	9	17	For the scripture saith to Pharao: To this purpose have I raised thee, that I may shew my power in thee, and that my name may be declared throughout all the earth.
Romans	Rom	52	9	18	Therefore he hath mercy on whom he will; and whom he will, he hardeneth.
Romans	Rom	52	9	19	Thou wilt say therefore to me: Why doth he then find fault? for who resisteth his will?
Romans	Rom	52	9	20	O man, who art thou that repliest against God? Shall the thing formed say to him that formed it: Why hast thou made me thus?
Romans	Rom	52	9	21	Or hath not the potter power over the clay, of the same lump, to make one vessel unto honour, and another unto dishonour?
Romans	Rom	52	9	22	What if God, willing to shew his wrath, and to make his power known, endured with much patience vessels of wrath, fitted for destruction,
Romans	Rom	52	9	23	That he might shew the riches of his glory on the vessels of mercy, which he hath prepared unto glory?
Romans	Rom	52	9	24	Even us, whom also he hath called, nor only of the Jews, but also of the Gentiles.
Romans	Rom	52	9	25	As in Osee he saith: I will call that which was not my people, my people; and her that was not beloved, beloved; and her that had not obtained mercy, one that hath obtained mercy.
Romans	Rom	52	9	26	And it shall be, in the place where it was said unto them, You are not my people; there they shall be called the sons of the living God.
Romans	Rom	52	9	27	And Isaias crieth out concerning Israel: If the number of the children of Israel be as the sand of the sea, a remnant shall be saved.
Romans	Rom	52	9	28	For he shall finish his word, and cut it short in justice; because a short word shall the Lord make upon the earth.
Romans	Rom	52	9	29	And as Isaias foretold: Unless the Lord of Sabaoth had left us a seed, we had been made as Sodom, and we had been like unto Gomorrha.
Romans	Rom	52	9	30	What then shall we say? That the Gentiles, who followed not after justice, have attained to justice, even the justice that is of faith.
Romans	Rom	52	9	31	But Israel, by following after the law of justice, is not come unto the law of justice.
Romans	Rom	52	9	32	Why so? Because they sought it not by faith, but as it were of works. For they stumbled at the stumblingstone.
Romans	Rom	52	9	33	As it is written: Behold I lay in Sion a stumblingstone and a rock of scandal; and whosoever believeth in him shall not be confounded.
Romans	Rom	52	10	1	Brethren, the will of my heart, indeed, and my prayer to God, is for them unto salvation.
Romans	Rom	52	10	2	For I bear them witness, that they have a zeal of God, but not according to knowledge.
Romans	Rom	52	10	3	For they, not knowing the justice of God, and seeking to establish their own, have not submitted themselves to the justice of God.
Romans	Rom	52	10	4	For the end of the law is Christ, unto justice to every one that believeth.
Romans	Rom	52	10	5	For Moses wrote, that the justice which is of the law, the man that shall do it, shall live by it.
Romans	Rom	52	10	6	But the justice which is of faith, speaketh thus: Say not in thy heart, Who shall ascend into heaven? that is, to bring Christ down;
Romans	Rom	52	10	7	Or who shall descend into the deep? that is, to bring up Christ again from the dead.
Romans	Rom	52	10	8	But what saith the scripture? The word is nigh thee, even in thy mouth, and in thy heart. This is the word of faith, which we preach.
Romans	Rom	52	10	9	For if thou confess with thy mouth the Lord Jesus, and believe in thy heart that God hath raised him up from the dead, thou shalt be saved.
Romans	Rom	52	10	10	For, with the heart, we believe unto justice; but, with the mouth, confession is made unto salvation.
Romans	Rom	52	10	11	For the scripture saith: Whosoever believeth in him, shall not be confounded.
Romans	Rom	52	10	12	For there is no distinction of the Jew and the Greek: for the same is Lord over all, rich unto all that call upon him.
Romans	Rom	52	10	13	For whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord, shall be saved.
Romans	Rom	52	10	14	How then shall they call on him, in whom they have not believed? Or how shall they believe him, of whom they have not heard? And how shall they hear, without a preacher?
Romans	Rom	52	10	15	And how shall they preach unless they be sent, as it is written: How beautiful are the feet of them that preach the gospel of peace, of them that bring glad tidings of good things!
Romans	Rom	52	10	16	But all do not obey the gospel. For Isaias saith: Lord, who hath believed our report?
Romans	Rom	52	10	17	Faith then cometh by hearing; and hearing by the word of Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	10	18	But I say: Have they not heard? Yes, verily, their sound hath gone forth into all the earth, and their words unto the ends of the whole world.
Romans	Rom	52	10	19	But I say: Hath not Israel known? First, Moses saith: I will provoke you to jealousy by that which is not a nation; by a foolish nation I will anger you.
Romans	Rom	52	10	20	But Isaias is bold, and saith: I was found by them that did not seek me: I appeared openly to them that asked not after me.
Romans	Rom	52	10	21	But to Israel he saith: All the day long have I spread my hands to a people that believeth not, and contradicteth me.
Romans	Rom	52	11	1	I say then: Hath God cast away his people? God forbid. For I also am an Israelite of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin.
Romans	Rom	52	11	2	God hath not cast away his people, which he foreknew. Know you not what the scripture saith of Elias; how he calleth on God against Israel?
Romans	Rom	52	11	3	Lord, they have slain thy prophets, they have dug down thy altars; and I am left alone, and they seek my life.
Romans	Rom	52	11	4	But what saith the divine answer to him? I have left me seven thousand men, that have not bowed their knees to Baal.
Romans	Rom	52	11	5	Even so then at this present time also, there is a remnant saved according to the election of grace.
Romans	Rom	52	11	6	And if by grace, it is not now by works: otherwise grace is no more grace.
Romans	Rom	52	11	7	What then? That which Israel sought, he hath not obtained: but the election hath obtained it; and the rest have been blinded.
Romans	Rom	52	11	8	As it is written: God hath given them the spirit of insensibility; eyes that they should not see; and ears that they should not hear, until this present day.
Romans	Rom	52	11	9	And David saith: Let their table be made a snare, and a trap, and a stumbling block, and a recompense unto them.
Romans	Rom	52	11	10	Let their eyes be darkened, that they may not see: and bow down their back always.
Romans	Rom	52	11	11	I say then, have they so stumbled, that they should fall? God forbid. But by their offence, salvation is come to the Gentiles, that they may be emulous of them.
Romans	Rom	52	11	12	Now if the offence of them be the riches of the world, and the diminution of them, the riches of the Gentiles; how much more the fulness of them?
Romans	Rom	52	11	13	For I say to you, Gentiles: as long indeed as I am the apostle of the Gentiles, I will honour my ministry,
Romans	Rom	52	11	14	If, by any means, I may provoke to emulation them who are my flesh, and may save some of them.
Romans	Rom	52	11	15	For if the loss of them be the reconciliation of the world, what shall the receiving of them be, but life from the dead?
Romans	Rom	52	11	16	For if the firstfruit be holy, so is the lump also: and if the root be holy, so are the branches.
Romans	Rom	52	11	17	And if some of the branches be broken, and thou, being a wild olive, art ingrafted in them, and art made partaker of the root, and of the fatness of the olive tree,
Romans	Rom	52	11	18	Boast not against the branches. But if thou boast, thou bearest not the root, but the root thee.
Romans	Rom	52	11	19	Thou wilt say then: The branches were broken off, that I might be grafted in.
Romans	Rom	52	11	20	Well: because of unbelief they were broken off. But thou standest by faith: be not highminded, but fear.
Romans	Rom	52	11	21	For if God hath not spared the natural branches, fear lest perhaps he also spare not thee.
Romans	Rom	52	11	22	See then the goodness and the severity of God: towards them indeed that are fallen, the severity; but towards thee, the goodness of God, if thou abide in goodness, otherwise thou also shalt be cut off.
Romans	Rom	52	11	23	And they also, if they abide not still in unbelief, shall be grafted in: for God is able to graft them in again.
Romans	Rom	52	11	24	For if thou wert cut out of the wild olive tree, which is natural to thee; and, contrary to nature, were grafted into the good olive tree; how much more shall they that are the natural branches, be grafted into their own olive tree?
Romans	Rom	52	11	25	For I would not have you ignorant, brethren, of this mystery, (lest you should be wise in your own conceits), that blindness in part has happened in Israel, until the fulness of the Gentiles should come in.
Romans	Rom	52	11	26	And so all Israel should be saved, as it is written: There shall come out of Sion, he that shall deliver, and shall turn away ungodliness from Jacob.
Romans	Rom	52	11	27	And this is to them my covenant: when I shall take away their sins.
Romans	Rom	52	11	28	As concerning the gospel, indeed, they are enemies for your sake: but as touching the election, they are most dear for the sake of the fathers.
Romans	Rom	52	11	29	For the gifts and the calling of God are without repentance.
Romans	Rom	52	11	30	For as you also in times past did not believe God, but now have obtained mercy, through their unbelief;
Romans	Rom	52	11	31	So these also now have not believed, for your mercy, that they also may obtain mercy.
Romans	Rom	52	11	32	For God hath concluded all in unbelief, that he may have mercy on all.
Romans	Rom	52	11	33	O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and of the knowledge of God! How incomprehensible are his judgments, and how unsearchable his ways!
Romans	Rom	52	11	34	For who hath known the mind of the Lord? Or who hath been his counsellor?
Romans	Rom	52	11	35	Or who hath first given to him, and recompense shall be made him?
Romans	Rom	52	11	36	For of him, and by him, and in him, are all things: to him be glory for ever. Amen.
Romans	Rom	52	12	1	I BESEECH you therefore, brethren, by the mercy of God, that you present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, pleasing unto God, your reasonable service.
Romans	Rom	52	12	2	And be not conformed to this world; but be reformed in the newness of your mind, that you may prove what is the good, and the acceptable, and the perfect will of God.
Romans	Rom	52	12	3	For I say, by the grace that is given me, to all that are among you, not to be more wise than it behoveth to be wise, but to be wise unto sobriety, and according as God hath divided to every one the measure of faith.
Romans	Rom	52	12	4	For as in one body we have many members, but all the members have not the same office:
Romans	Rom	52	12	5	So we being many, are one body in Christ, and every one members one of another.
Romans	Rom	52	12	6	And having different gifts, according to the grace that is given us, either prophecy, to be used according to the rule of faith;
Romans	Rom	52	12	7	Or ministry, in ministering; or he that teacheth, in doctrine;
Romans	Rom	52	12	8	He that exhorteth, in exhorting; he that giveth, with simplicity; he that ruleth, with carefulness; he that sheweth mercy, with cheerfulness.
Romans	Rom	52	12	9	Let love be without dissimulation. Hating that which is evil, cleaving to that which is good.
Romans	Rom	52	12	10	Loving one another with the charity of brotherhood, with honour preventing one another.
Romans	Rom	52	12	11	In carefulness not slothful. In spirit fervent. Serving the Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	12	12	Rejoicing in hope. Patient in tribulation. Instant in prayer.
Romans	Rom	52	12	13	Communicating to the necessities of the saints. Pursuing hospitality.
Romans	Rom	52	12	14	Bless them that persecute you: bless, and curse not.
Romans	Rom	52	12	15	Rejoice with them that rejoice; weep with them that weep.
Romans	Rom	52	12	16	Being of one mind one towards another. Not minding high things, but consenting to the humble. Be not wise in your own conceits.
Romans	Rom	52	12	17	To no man rendering evil for evil. Providing good things, not only in the sight of God, but also in the sight of all men.
Romans	Rom	52	12	18	If it be possible, as much as is in you, have peace with all men.
Romans	Rom	52	12	19	Revenge not yourselves, my dearly beloved; but give place unto wrath, for it is written: Revenge is mine, I will repay, saith the Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	12	20	But if thy enemy be hungry, give him to eat; if he thirst, give him to drink. For, doing this, thou shalt heap coals of fire upon his head.
Romans	Rom	52	12	21	Be not overcome by evil, but overcome evil by good.
Romans	Rom	52	13	1	Let every soul be subject to higher powers: for there is no power but from God: and those that are, are ordained of God.
Romans	Rom	52	13	2	Therefore he that resisteth the power, resisteth the ordinance of God. And they that resist, purchase to themselves damnation.
Romans	Rom	52	13	3	For princes are not a terror to the good work, but to the evil. Wilt thou then not be afraid of the power? Do that which is good: and thou shalt have praise from the same.
Romans	Rom	52	13	4	For he is God's minister to thee, for good. But if thou do that which is evil, fear: for he beareth not the sword in vain. For he is God's minister: an avenger to execute wrath upon him that doth evil.
Romans	Rom	52	13	5	Wherefore be subject of necessity, not only for wrath, but also for conscience' sake.
Romans	Rom	52	13	6	For therefore also you pay tribute. For they are the ministers of God, serving unto this purpose.
Romans	Rom	52	13	7	Render therefore to all men their dues. Tribute, to whom tribute is due: custom, to whom custom: fear, to whom fear: honour, to whom honour.
Romans	Rom	52	13	8	Owe no man any thing, but to love one another. For he that loveth his neighbour, hath fulfilled the law.
Romans	Rom	52	13	9	For Thou shalt not commit adultery: Thou shalt not kill: Thou shalt not steal, Thou shalt not bear false witness: Thou shalt not covet: and if there be any other commandment, it is comprised in this word, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.
Romans	Rom	52	13	10	The love of our neighbour worketh no evil. Love therefore is the fulfilling of the law.
Romans	Rom	52	13	11	And that knowing the season; that it is now the hour for us to rise from sleep. For now our salvation is nearer than when we believed.
Romans	Rom	52	13	12	The night is passed, and the day is at hand. Let us therefore cast off the works of darkness, and put on the armour of light.
Romans	Rom	52	13	13	Let us walk honestly, as in the day: not in rioting and drunkenness, not in chambering and impurities, not in contention and envy:
Romans	Rom	52	13	14	But put ye on the Lord Jesus Christ, and make not provision for the flesh in its concupiscences.
Romans	Rom	52	14	1	Now him that is weak in faith, take unto you: not in disputes about thoughts.
Romans	Rom	52	14	2	For one believeth that he may eat all things: but he that is weak, let him eat herbs.
Romans	Rom	52	14	3	Let not him that eateth, despise him that eateth not: and he that eateth not, let him not judge him that eateth. For God hath taken him to him.
Romans	Rom	52	14	4	Who art thou that judgest another man's servant? To his own lord he standeth or falleth. And he shall stand: for God is able to make him stand.
Romans	Rom	52	14	5	For one judgeth between day and day: and another judgeth every day: let every man abound in his own sense.
Romans	Rom	52	14	6	He that regardeth the day, regardeth it unto the Lord. And he that eateth, eateth to the Lord: for he giveth thanks to God. And he that eateth not, to the Lord he eateth not, and giveth thanks to God.
Romans	Rom	52	14	7	For none of us liveth to himself; and no man dieth to himself.
Romans	Rom	52	14	8	For whether we live, we live unto the Lord; or whether we die, we die unto the Lord. Therefore, whether we live, or whether we die, we are the Lord's.
Romans	Rom	52	14	9	For to this end Christ died and rose again; that he might be Lord both of the dead and of the living.
Romans	Rom	52	14	10	But thou, why judgest thou thy brother? or thou, why dost thou despise thy brother? For we shall all stand before the judgment seat of Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	14	11	For it is written: As I live, saith the Lord, every knee shall bow to me, and every tongue shall confess to God.
Romans	Rom	52	14	12	Therefore every one of us shall render account to God for himself.
Romans	Rom	52	14	13	Let us not therefore judge one another any more. But judge this rather, that you put not a stumblingblock or a scandal in your brother's way.
Romans	Rom	52	14	14	I know, and am confident in the Lord Jesus, that nothing is unclean of itself; but to him that esteemeth any thing to be unclean, to him it is unclean.
Romans	Rom	52	14	15	For if, because of thy meat, thy brother be grieved, thou walkest not now according to charity. Destroy not him with thy meat, for whom Christ died.
Romans	Rom	52	14	16	Let not then our good be evil spoken of.
Romans	Rom	52	14	17	For the kingdom of God is not meat and drink; but justice, and peace, and joy in the Holy Ghost.
Romans	Rom	52	14	18	For he that in this serveth Christ, pleaseth God, and is approved of men.
Romans	Rom	52	14	19	Therefore let us follow after the things that are of peace; and keep the things that are of edification one towards another.
Romans	Rom	52	14	20	Destroy not the work of God for meat. All things indeed are clean: but it is evil for that man who eateth with offence.
Romans	Rom	52	14	21	It is good not to eat flesh, and not to drink wine, nor any thing whereby thy brother is offended, or scandalized, or made weak.
Romans	Rom	52	14	22	Hast thou faith? Have it to thyself before God. Blessed is he that condemneth not himself in that which he alloweth.
Romans	Rom	52	14	23	But he that discerneth, if he eat, is condemned; because not of faith. For all that is not of faith is sin.
Romans	Rom	52	15	1	Now we that are stronger, ought to bear the infirmities of the weak, and not to please ourselves.
Romans	Rom	52	15	2	Let every one of you please his neighbour unto good, to edification.
Romans	Rom	52	15	3	For Christ did not please himself, but as it is written: The reproaches of them that reproached thee, fell upon me.
Romans	Rom	52	15	4	For what things soever were written, were written for our learning: that through patience and the comfort of the scriptures, we might have hope.
Romans	Rom	52	15	5	Now the God of patience and of comfort grant you to be of one mind one towards another, according to Jesus Christ:
Romans	Rom	52	15	6	That with one mind, and with one mouth, you may glorify God and the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	15	7	Wherefore receive one another, as Christ also hath received you unto the honour of God.
Romans	Rom	52	15	8	For I say that Christ Jesus was minister of the circumcision for the truth of God, to confirm the promises made unto the fathers.
Romans	Rom	52	15	9	But that the Gentiles are to glorify God for his mercy, as it is written: Therefore will I confess to thee, O Lord, among the Gentiles, and will sing to thy name.
Romans	Rom	52	15	10	And again he saith: Rejoice, ye Gentiles, with his people.
Romans	Rom	52	15	11	And again: Praise the Lord, all ye Gentiles; and magnify him, all ye people.
Romans	Rom	52	15	12	And again Isaias saith: There shall be a root of Jesse; and he that shall rise up to rule the Gentiles, in him the Gentiles shall hope.
Romans	Rom	52	15	13	Now the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing; that you may abound in hope, and in the power of the Holy Ghost.
Romans	Rom	52	15	14	And I myself also, my brethren, am assured of you, that you also are full of love, replenished with all knowledge, so that you are able to admonish one another.
Romans	Rom	52	15	15	But I have written to you, brethren, more boldly in some sort, as it were putting you in mind: because of the grace which is given me from God.
Romans	Rom	52	15	16	That I should be the minister of Christ Jesus among the Gentiles; sanctifying the gospel of God, that the oblation of the Gentiles may be made acceptable and sanctified in the Holy Ghost.
Romans	Rom	52	15	17	I have therefore glory in Christ Jesus towards God.
Romans	Rom	52	15	18	For I dare not to speak of any of those things which Christ worketh not by me, for the obedience of the Gentiles, by word and deed,
Romans	Rom	52	15	19	By the virtue of signs and wonders, in the power of the Holy Ghost, so that from Jerusalem round about as far as unto Illyricum, I have replenished the gospel of Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	15	20	And I have so preached this gospel, not where Christ was named, lest I should build upon another man's foundation.
Romans	Rom	52	15	21	But as it is written: They to whom he was not spoken of, shall see, and they that have not heard shall understand.
Romans	Rom	52	15	22	For which cause also I was hindered very much from coming to you, and have been kept away till now.
Romans	Rom	52	15	23	But now having no more place in these countries, and having a great desire these many years past to come unto you,
Romans	Rom	52	15	24	When I shall begin to take my journey into Spain, I hope that as I pass, I shall see you, and be brought on my way thither by you, if first, in part, I shall have enjoyed you:
Romans	Rom	52	15	25	But now I shall go to Jerusalem, to minister unto the saints.
Romans	Rom	52	15	26	For it hath pleased them of Macedonia and Achaia to make a contribution for the poor of the saints that are in Jerusalem.
Romans	Rom	52	15	27	For it hath pleased them; and they are their debtors. For if the Gentiles have been made partakers of their spiritual things, they ought also in carnal things to minister to them.
Romans	Rom	52	15	28	When therefore I shall have accomplished this, and consigned to them this fruit, I will come by you into Spain.
Romans	Rom	52	15	29	And I know, that when I come to you, I shall come in the abundance of the blessing of the gospel of Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	15	30	I beseech you therefore, brethren, through our Lord Jesus Christ, and by the charity of the Holy Ghost, that you help me in your prayers for me to God,
Romans	Rom	52	15	31	That I may be delivered from the unbelievers that are in Judea, and that the oblation of my service may be acceptable in Jerusalem to the saints.
Romans	Rom	52	15	32	That I may come to you with joy, by the will of God, and may be refreshed with you.
Romans	Rom	52	15	33	Now the God of peace be with you all. Amen.
Romans	Rom	52	16	1	And I commend to you Phebe, our sister, who is in the ministry of the church, that is in Cenchrae:
Romans	Rom	52	16	2	That you receive her in the Lord as becometh saints; and that you assist her in whatsoever business she shall have need of you. For she also hath assisted many, and myself also.
Romans	Rom	52	16	3	Salute Prisca and Aquila, my helpers in Christ Jesus,
Romans	Rom	52	16	4	(Who have for my life laid down their own necks: to whom not I only give thanks, but also all the churches of the Gentiles,)
Romans	Rom	52	16	5	And the church which is in their house. Salute Epenetus, my beloved: who is the firstfruits of Asia in Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	16	6	Salute Mary, who hath laboured much among you.
Romans	Rom	52	16	7	Salute Andronicus and Junias, my kinsmen and fellow prisoners: who are of note among the apostles, who also were in Christ before me.
Romans	Rom	52	16	8	Salute Ampliatus, most beloved to me in the Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	16	9	Salute Urbanus, our helper in Christ Jesus, and Stachys, my beloved.
Romans	Rom	52	16	10	Salute Apelles, approved in Christ.
Romans	Rom	52	16	11	Salute them that are of Aristobulus' household. Salute Herodian, my kinsman. Salute them that are of Narcissus' household, who are in the Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	16	12	Salute Tryphaena and Tryphosa, who labour in the Lord. Salute Persis, the dearly beloved, who hath much laboured in the Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	16	13	Salute Rufus, elect in the Lord, and his mother and mine.
Romans	Rom	52	16	14	Salute Asyncritus, Phlegon, Hermas, Patrobas, Hermes, and the brethren that are with them.
Romans	Rom	52	16	15	Salute Philologus and Julia, Nereus and his sister, and Olympias; and all the saints that are with them.
Romans	Rom	52	16	16	Salute one another with an holy kiss. All the churches of Christ salute you.
Romans	Rom	52	16	17	Now I beseech you, brethren, to mark them who make dissensions and offences contrary to the doctrine which you have learned, and avoid them.
Romans	Rom	52	16	18	For they that are such, serve not Christ our Lord, but their own belly; and by pleasing speeches and good words, seduce the hearts of the innocent.
Romans	Rom	52	16	19	For your obedience is published in every place. I rejoice therefore in you. But I would have you to be wise in good, and simple in evil.
Romans	Rom	52	16	20	And the God of peace crush Satan under your feet speedily. The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you.
Romans	Rom	52	16	21	Timothy, my fellow labourer, saluteth you, and Lucius, and Jason, and Sosipater, my kinsmen.
Romans	Rom	52	16	22	I Tertius, who wrote this epistle, salute you in the Lord.
Romans	Rom	52	16	23	Caius, my host, and the whole church, saluteth you. Erastus, the treasurer of the city, saluteth you, and Quartus, a brother.
Romans	Rom	52	16	24	The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen.
Romans	Rom	52	16	25	Now to him that is able to establish you, according to my gospel, and the preaching of Jesus Christ, according to the revelation of the mystery, which was kept secret from eternity,
Romans	Rom	52	16	26	(Which now is made manifest by the scriptures of the prophets, according to the precept of the eternal God, for the obedience of faith,) known among all nations;
Romans	Rom	52	16	27	To God the only wise, through Jesus Christ, to whom be honour and glory for ever and ever. Amen.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	1	Paul, called to be an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Sosthenes a brother,
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	2	To the church of God that is at Corinth, to them that are sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be saints, with all that invoke the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, in every place of theirs and ours.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	3	Grace to you, and peace from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	4	I give thanks to my God always for you, for the grace of God that is given you in Christ Jesus,
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	5	That in all things you are made rich in him, in all utterance, and in all knowledge;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	6	As the testimony of Christ was confirmed in you,
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	7	So that nothing is wanting to you in any grace, waiting for the manifestation of our Lord Jesus Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	8	Who also will confirm you unto the end without crime, in the day of the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	9	God is faithful: by whom you are called unto the fellowship of his Son Jesus Christ our Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	10	Now I beseech you, brethren, by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that you all speak the same thing, and that there be no schisms among you; but that you be perfect in the same mind, and in the same judgment.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	11	For it hath been signified unto me, my brethren, of you, by them that are of the house of Chloe, that there are contentions among you.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	12	Now this I say, that every one of you saith: I indeed am of Paul; and I am of Apollo; and I am of Cephas; and I of Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	13	Is Christ divided? Was Paul then crucified for you? or were you baptized in the name of Paul?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	14	I give God thanks, that I baptized none of you but Crispus and Caius;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	15	Lest any should say that you were baptized in my name.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	16	And I baptized also the household of Stephanus; besides, I know not whether I baptized any other.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	17	For Christ sent me not to baptize, but to preach the gospel: not in wisdom of speech, lest the cross of Christ should be made void.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	18	For the word of the cross, to them indeed that perish, is foolishness; but to them that are saved, that is, to us, it is the power of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	19	For it is written: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the prudence of the prudent I will reject.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	20	Where is the wise? Where is the scribe? Where is the disputer of this world? Hath not God made foolish the wisdom of this world?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	21	For seeing that in the wisdom of God the world, by wisdom, knew not God, it pleased God, by the foolishness of our preaching, to save them that believe.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	22	For both the Jews require signs, and the Greeks seek after wisdom:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	23	But we preach Christ crucified, unto the Jews indeed a stumblingblock, and unto the Gentiles foolishness:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	24	But unto them that are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God, and the wisdom of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	25	For the foolishness of God is wiser than men; and the weakness of God is stronger than men.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	26	For see your vocation, brethren, that there are not many wise according to the flesh, not many mighty, not many noble:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	27	But the foolish things of the world hath God chosen, that he may confound the wise; and the weak things of the world hath God chosen, that he may confound the strong.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	28	And the base things of the world, and the things that are contemptible, hath God chosen, and things that are not, that he might bring to nought things that are:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	29	That no flesh should glory in his sight.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	30	But of him are you in Christ Jesus, who of God is made unto us wisdom, and justice, and sanctification, and redemption:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	1	31	That, as it is written: He that glorieth, may glory in the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	1	And I, brethren, when I came to you, came not in loftiness of speech or of wisdom, declaring unto you the testimony of Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	2	For I judged not myself to know anything among you, but Jesus Christ, and him crucified.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	3	And I was with you in weakness, and in fear, and in much trembling.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	4	And my speech and my preaching was not in the persuasive words of human wisdom, but in shewing of the Spirit and power;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	5	That your faith might not stand on the wisdom of men, but on the power of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	6	Howbeit we speak wisdom among the perfect: yet not the wisdom of this world, neither of the princes of this world that come to nought;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	7	But we speak the wisdom of God in a mystery, a wisdom which is hidden, which God ordained before the world, unto our glory:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	8	Which none of the princes of this world knew; for if they had known it, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	9	But, as it is written: That eye hath not seen, nor ear heard, neither hath it entered into the heart of man, what things God hath prepared for them that love him.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	10	But to us God hath revealed them, by his Spirit. For the Spirit searcheth all things, yea, the deep things of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	11	For what man knoweth the things of a man, but the spirit of a man that is in him? So the things also that are of God no man knoweth, but the Spirit of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	12	Now we have received not the spirit of this world, but the Spirit that is of God; that we may know the things that are given us from God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	13	Which things also we speak, not in the learned words of human wisdom; but in the doctrine of the Spirit, comparing spiritual things with spiritual.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	14	But the sensual man perceiveth not these things that are of the Spirit of God; for it is foolishness to him, and he cannot understand, because it is spiritually examined.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	15	But the spiritual man judgeth all things; and he himself is judged of no man.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	2	16	For who hath known the mind of the Lord, that we may instruct him? But we have the mind of Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	1	And I, brethren, could not speak to you as unto spiritual, but as unto carnal. As unto little ones in Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	2	I gave you milk to drink, not meat; for you were not able as yet. But neither indeed are you now able; for you are yet carnal.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	3	For, whereas there is among you envying and contention, are you not carnal, and walk according to man?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	4	For while one saith, I indeed am of Paul; and another, I am of Apollo; are you not men? What then is Apollo, and what is Paul?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	5	The ministers of him whom you have believed; and to every one as the Lord hath given.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	6	I have planted, Apollo watered, but God gave the increase.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	7	Therefore, neither he that planteth is any thing, nor he that watereth; but God that giveth the increase.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	8	Now he that planteth, and he that watereth, are one. And every man shall receive his own reward, according to his own labour.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	9	For we are God's coadjutors: you are God's husbandry; you are God's building.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	10	According to the grace of God that is given to me, as a wise architect, I have laid the foundation; and another buildeth thereon. But let every man take heed how he buildeth thereupon.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	11	For other foundation no man can lay, but that which is laid; which is Christ Jesus.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	12	Now if any man build upon this foundation, gold, silver, precious stones, wood, hay, stubble:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	13	Every man's work shall be manifest; for the day of the Lord shall declare it, because it shall be revealed in fire; and the fire shall try every man's work, of what sort it is.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	14	If any man's work abide, which he hath built thereupon, he shall receive a reward.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	15	If any man's work burn, he shall suffer loss; but he himself shall be saved, yet so as by fire.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	16	Know you not, that you are the temple of God, and that the Spirit of God dwelleth in you?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	17	But if any man violate the temple of God, him shall God destroy. For the temple of God is holy, which you are.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	18	Let no man deceive himself: if any man among you seem to be wise in this world, let him become a fool, that he may be wise.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	19	For the wisdom of this world is foolishness with God. For it is written: I will catch the wise in their own craftiness.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	20	And again: The Lord knoweth the thoughts of the wise, that they are vain.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	21	Let no man therefore glory in men.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	22	For all things are yours, whether it be Paul, or Apollo, or Cephas, or the world, or life, or death, or things present, or things to come; for all are yours;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	3	23	And you are Christ's; and Christ is God's.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	1	Let a man so account of us as of the ministers of Christ, and the dispensers of the mysteries of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	2	Here now it is required among the dispensers, that a man be found faithful.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	3	But to me it is a very small thing to be judged by you, or by man's day; but neither do I judge my own self.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	4	For I am not conscious to myself of any thing, yet am I not hereby justified; but he that judgeth me, is the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	5	Therefore judge not before the time; until the Lord come, who both will bring to light the hidden things of darkness, and will make manifest the counsels of the hearts; and then shall every man have praise from God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	6	But these things, brethren, I have in a figure transferred to myself and to Apollo, for your sakes; that in us you may learn, that one be not puffed up against the other for another, above that which is written.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	7	For who distinguisheth thee? Or what hast thou that thou hast not received? And if thou hast received, why dost thou glory, as if thou hadst not received it?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	8	You are now full; you are now become rich; you reign without us; and I would to God you did reign, that we also might reign with you.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	9	For I think that God hath set forth us apostles, the last, as it were men appointed to death: we are made a spectacle to the world, and to angels, and to men.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	10	We are fools for Christ's sake, but you are wise in Christ; we are weak, but you are strong; you are honourable, but we without honour.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	11	Even unto this hour we both hunger and thirst, and are naked, and are buffeted, and have no fixed abode;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	12	And we labour, working with our own hands: we are reviled, and we bless; we are persecuted, and we suffer it.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	13	We are blasphemed, and we entreat; we are made as the refuse of this world, the offscouring of all even until now.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	14	I write not these things to confound you; but I admonish you as my dearest children.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	15	For if you have ten thousand instructors in Christ, yet not many fathers. For in Christ Jesus, by the gospel, I have begotten you.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	16	Wherefore I beseech you, be ye followers of me, as I also am of Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	17	For this cause have I sent to you Timothy, who is my dearest son and faithful in the Lord; who will put you in mind of my ways, which are in Christ Jesus; as I teach everywhere in every church.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	18	As if I would not come to you, so some are puffed up.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	19	But I will come to you shortly, if the Lord will: and will know, not the speech of them that are puffed up, but the power.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	20	For the kingdom of God is not in speech, but in power.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	4	21	What will you? shall I come to you with a rod; or in charity, and in the spirit of meekness?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	1	It is absolutely heard, that there is fornication among you, and such fornication as the like is not among the heathens; that one should have his father's wife.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	2	And you are puffed up; and have not rather mourned, that he might be taken away from among you, that hath done this deed.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	3	I indeed, absent in body, but present in spirit, have already judged, as though I were present, him that hath so done,
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	4	In the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, you being gathered together, and my spirit, with the power of our Lord Jesus;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	5	To deliver such a one to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	6	Your glorying is not good. Know you not that a little leaven corrupteth the whole lump?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	7	Purge out the old leaven, that you may be a new paste, as you are unleavened. For Christ our pasch is sacrificed.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	8	Therefore let us feast, not with the old leaven, nor with the leaven of malice and wickedness; but with the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	9	I wrote to you in an epistle, not to keep company with fornicators.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	10	I mean not with the fornicators of this world, or with the covetous, or the extortioners, or the servers of idols; otherwise you must needs go out of this world.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	11	But now I have written to you, not to keep company, if any man that is named a brother, be a fornicator, or covetous, or a server of idols, or a railer, or a drunkard, or an extortioner: with such a one, not so much as to eat.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	12	For what have I to do to judge them that are without? Do not you judge them that are within?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	5	13	For them that are without, God will judge. Put away the evil one from among yourselves.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	1	Dare any of you, having a matter against another, go to be judged before the unjust, and not before the saints?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	2	Know you not that the saints shall judge this world? And if the world shall be judged by you, are you unworthy to judge the smallest matters?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	3	Know you not that we shall judge angels? how much more things of this world?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	4	If therefore you have judgments of things pertaining to this world, set them to judge, who are the most despised in the church.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	5	I speak to your shame. Is it so that there is not among you any one wise man, that is able to judge between his brethren?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	6	But brother goeth to law with brother, and that before unbelievers.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	7	Already indeed there is plainly a fault among you, that you have lawsuits one with another. Why do you not rather take wrong? Why do you not rather suffer yourselves to be defrauded?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	8	But you do wrong and defraud, and that to your brethren.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	9	Know you not that the unjust shall not possess the kingdom of God? Do not err: neither fornicators, nor idolaters, nor adulterers,
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	10	Nor the effeminate, nor liers with mankind, nor thieves, nor covetous, nor drunkards, nor railers, nor extortioners, shall possess the kingdom of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	11	And such some of you were; but you are washed, but you are sanctified, but you are justified in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, and the Spirit of our God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	12	All things are lawful to me, but all things are not expedient. All things are lawful to me, but I will not be brought under the power of any.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	13	Meat for the belly, and the belly for the meats; but God shall destroy both it and them: but the body is not for fornication, but for the Lord, and the Lord for the body.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	14	Now God hath both raised up the Lord, and will raise us up also by his power.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	15	Know you not that your bodies are the members of Christ? Shall I then take the members of Christ, and make them the members of an harlot? God forbid.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	16	Or know you not, that he who is joined to a harlot, is made one body? For they shall be, saith he, two in one flesh.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	17	But he who is joined to the Lord, is one spirit.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	18	Fly fornication. Every sin that a man doth, is without the body; but he that committeth fornication, sinneth against his own body.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	19	Or know you not, that your members are the temple of the Holy Ghost, who is in you, whom you have from God; and you are not your own?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	6	20	For you are bought with a great price. Glorify and bear God in your body.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	1	Now concerning the thing whereof you wrote to me: It is good for a man not to touch a woman.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	2	But for fear of fornication, let every man have his own wife, and let every woman have her own husband.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	3	Let the husband render the debt to his wife, and the wife also in like manner to the husband.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	4	The wife hath not power of her own body, but the husband. And in like manner the husband also hath not power of his own body, but the wife.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	5	Defraud not one another, except, perhaps, by consent, for a time, that you may give yourselves to prayer; and return together again, lest Satan tempt you for your incontinency.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	6	But I speak this by indulgence, not by commandment.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	7	For I would that all men were even as myself: but every one hath his proper gift from God; one after this manner, and another after that.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	8	But I say to the unmarried, and to the widows: It is good for them if they so continue, even as I.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	9	But if they do not contain themselves, let them marry. For it is better to marry than to be burnt.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	10	But to them that are married, not I but the Lord commandeth, that the wife depart not from her husband.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	11	And if she depart, that she remain unmarried, or be reconciled to her husband. And let not the husband put away his wife.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	12	For to the rest I speak, not the Lord. If any brother hath a wife that believeth not, and she consent to dwell with him, let him not put her away.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	13	And if any woman hath a husband that believeth not, and he consent to dwell with her, let her not put away her husband.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	14	For the unbelieving husband is sanctified by the believing wife; and the unbelieving wife is sanctified by the believing husband: otherwise your children should be unclean; but now they are holy.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	15	But if the unbeliever depart, let him depart. For a brother or sister is not under servitude in such cases. But God hath called us in peace.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	16	For how knowest thou, O wife, whether thou shalt save thy husband? Or how knowest thou, O man, whether thou shalt save thy wife?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	17	But as the Lord hath distributed to every one, as God hath called every one, so let him walk: and so in all churches I teach.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	18	Is any man called, being circumcised? let him not procure uncircumcision. Is any man called in uncircumcision? let him not be circumcised.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	19	Circumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing: but the observance of the commandments of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	20	Let every man abide in the same calling in which he was called.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	21	Wast thou called, being a bondman? care not for it; but if thou mayest be made free, use it rather.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	22	For he that is called in the Lord, being a bondman, is the freeman of the Lord. Likewise he that is called, being free, is the bondman of Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	23	You are bought with a price; be not made the bondslaves of men.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	24	Brethren, let every man, wherein he was called, therein abide with God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	25	Now concerning virgins, I have no commandment of the Lord; but I give counsel, as having obtained mercy of the Lord, to be faithful.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	26	I think therefore that this is good for the present necessity, that it is good for a man so to be.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	27	Art thou bound to a wife? seek not to be loosed. Art thou loosed from a wife? seek not a wife.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	28	But if thou take a wife, thou hast not sinned. And if a virgin marry, she hath not sinned: nevertheless, such shall have tribulation of the flesh. But I spare you.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	29	This therefore I say, brethren; the time is short; it remaineth, that they also who have wives, be as if they had none;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	30	And they that weep, as though they wept not; and they that rejoice, as if they rejoiced not; and they that buy, as though they possessed not;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	31	And they that use this world, as if they used it not: for the fashion of this world passeth away.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	32	But I would have you to be without solicitude. He that is without a wife, is solicitous for the things that belong to the Lord, how he may please God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	33	But he that is with a wife, is solicitous for the things of the world, how he may please his wife: and he is divided.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	34	And the unmarried woman and the virgin thinketh on the things of the Lord, that she may be holy both in body and in spirit. But she that is married thinketh on the things of the world, how she may please her husband.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	35	And this I speak for your profit: not to cast a snare upon you; but for that which is decent, and which may give you power to attend upon the Lord, without impediment.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	36	But if any man think that he seemeth dishonoured, with regard to his virgin, for that she is above the age, and it must so be: let him do what he will; he sinneth not, if she marry.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	37	For he that hath determined being steadfast in his heart, having no necessity, but having power of his own will; and hath judged this in his heart, to keep his virgin, doth well.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	38	Therefore, both he that giveth his virgin in marriage, doth well; and he that giveth her not, doth better.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	39	A woman is bound by the law as long as her husband liveth; but if her husband die, she is at liberty: let her marry to whom she will; only in the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	7	40	But more blessed shall she be, if she so remain, according to my counsel; and I think that I also have the spirit of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	1	Now concerning those things that are sacrificed to idols, we know that we all have knowledge. Knowledge puffeth up; but charity edifieth.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	2	And if any man think that he knoweth any thing, he hath not yet known as he ought to know.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	3	But if any man love God, the same is known by him.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	4	But as for the meats that are sacrificed to idols, we know that an idol is nothing in the world, and that there is no God but one.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	5	For although there be that are called gods, either in heaven or on earth (for there be gods many, and lords many).
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	6	Yet to us there is but one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we unto him; and one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	7	But there is not knowledge in every one. For some until this present, with conscience of the idol: eat as a thing sacrificed to an idol, and their conscience, being weak, is defiled.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	8	But meat doth not commend us to God. For neither, if we eat, shall we have the more; nor, if we eat not, shall we have the less.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	9	But take heed lest perhaps this your liberty become a stumblingblock to the weak.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	10	For if a man see him that hath knowledge sit at meat in the idol's temple, shall not his conscience, being weak, be emboldened to eat those things which are sacrificed to idols?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	11	And through thy knowledge shall the weak brother perish, for whom Christ hath died?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	12	Now when you sin thus against the brethren, and wound their weak conscience, you sin against Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	8	13	Wherefore, if meat scandalize my brother, I will never eat flesh, lest I should scandalize my brother.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	1	Am not I free? Am not I an apostle? Have not I seen Christ Jesus our Lord? Are not you my work in the Lord?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	2	And if unto others I be not an apostle, but yet to you I am. For you are the seal of my apostleship in the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	3	My defence with them that do examine me is this.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	4	Have not we power to eat and to drink?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	5	Have we not power to carry about a woman, a sister, as well as the rest of the apostles, and the brethren of the Lord, and Cephas?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	6	Or I only and Barnabas, have not we power to do this?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	7	Who serveth as a soldier at any time, at his own charges? Who planteth a vineyard, and eateth not of the fruit thereof? Who feedeth the flock, and eateth not of the milk of the flock?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	8	Speak I these things according to man? Or doth not the law also say these things?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	9	For it is written in the law of Moses: Thou shalt not muzzle the mouth of the ox that treadeth out the corn. Doth God take care for oxen?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	10	Or doth he say this indeed for our sakes? For these things are written for our sakes: that he that plougheth, should plough in hope; and he that thrasheth, in hope to receive fruit.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	11	If we have sown unto you spiritual things, is it a great matter if we reap your carnal things?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	12	If others be partakers of this power over you, why not we rather? Nevertheless, we have not used this power: but we bear all things, lest we should give any hindrance to the gospel of Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	13	Know you not, that they who work in the holy place, eat the things that are of the holy place; and they that serve the altar, partake with the altar?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	14	So also the Lord ordained that they who preach the gospel, should live by the gospel.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	15	But I have used none of these things. Neither have I written these things, that they should be so done unto me: for it is good for me to die, rather than that any man should make my glory void.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	16	For if I preach the gospel, it is no glory to me, for a necessity lieth upon me: for woe is unto me if I preach not the gospel.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	17	For if I do this thing willingly, I have a reward: but if against my will, a dispensation is committed to me:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	18	What is my reward then? That preaching the gospel, I may deliver the gospel without charge, that I abuse not my power in the gospel.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	19	For whereas I was free as to all, I made myself the servant of all, that I might gain the more.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	20	And I became to the Jews, a Jew, that I might gain the Jews:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	21	To them that are under the law, as if I were under the law, (whereas myself was not under the law,) that I might gain them that were under the law. To them that were without the law, as if I were without the law, (whereas I was not without the law of God, but was in the law of Christ,) that I might gain them that were without the law.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	22	To the weak I became weak, that I might gain the weak. I became all things to all men, that I might save all.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	23	And I do all things for the gospel's sake: that I may be made partaker thereof.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	24	Know you not that they that run in the race, all run indeed, but one receiveth the prize? So run that you may obtain.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	25	And every one that striveth for the mastery, refraineth himself from all things: and they indeed that they may receive a corruptible crown; but we an incorruptible one.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	26	I therefore so run, not as at an uncertainty: I so fight, not as one beating the air:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	9	27	But I chastise my body, and bring it into subjection: lest perhaps, when I have preached to others, I myself should become a castaway.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	1	For I would not have you ignorant, brethren, that our fathers were all under the cloud, and all passed through the sea.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	2	And all in Moses were baptized, in the cloud, and in the sea:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	3	And did all eat the same spiritual food,
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	4	And all drank the same spiritual drink; (and they drank of the spiritual rock that followed them, and the rock was Christ.)
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	5	But with most of them God was not well pleased: for they were overthrown in the desert.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	6	Now these things were done in a figure of us, that we should not covet evil things as they also coveted.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	7	Neither become ye idolaters, as some of them, as it is written: The people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to play.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	8	Neither let us commit fornication, as some of them committed fornication, and there fell in one day three and twenty thousand.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	9	Neither let us tempt Christ: as some of them tempted, and perished by the serpents.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	10	Neither do you murmur: as some of them murmured, and were destroyed by the destroyer.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	11	Now all these things happened to them in figure: and they are written for our correction, upon whom the ends of the world are come.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	12	Wherefore he that thinketh himself to stand, let him take heed lest he fall.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	13	Let no temptation take hold on you, but such as is human. And God is faithful, who will not suffer you to be tempted above that which you are able: but will make also with temptation issue, that you may be able to bear it.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	14	Wherefore, my dearly beloved, fly from the service of idols.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	15	I speak as to wise men: judge ye yourselves what I say.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	16	The chalice of benediction, which we bless, is it not the communion of the blood of Christ? And the bread, which we break, is it not the partaking of the body of the Lord?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	17	For we, being many, are one bread, one body, all that partake of one bread.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	18	Behold Israel according to the flesh: are not they, that eat of the sacrifices, partakers of the altar?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	19	What then? Do I say, that what is offered in sacrifice to idols, is any thing? Or, that the idol is any thing?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	20	But the things which the heathens sacrifice, they sacrifice to devils, and not to God. And I would not that you should be made partakers with devils.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	21	You cannot drink the chalice of the Lord, and the chalice of devils: you cannot be partakers of the table of the Lord, and of the table of devils.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	22	Do we provoke the Lord to jealousy? Are we stronger than he? All things are lawful for me, but all things are not expedient.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	23	All things are lawful for me, but all things do not edify.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	24	Let no man seek his own, but that which is another's.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	25	Whatsoever is sold in the shambles, eat; asking no question for conscience' sake.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	26	The earth is the Lord's, and the fulness thereof.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	27	If any of them that believe not, invite you, and you will be willing to go; eat of any thing that is set before you, asking no question for conscience' sake.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	28	But if any man say: This has been sacrificed to idols, do not eat of it for his sake that told it, and for conscience' sake.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	29	Conscience, I say, not thy own, but the other's. For why is my liberty judged by another man's conscience?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	30	If I partake with thanksgiving, why am I evil spoken of, for that for which I give thanks?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	31	Therefore, whether you eat or drink, or whatsoever else you do, do all to the glory of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	32	Be without offence to the Jews, and to the Gentiles, and to the church of God:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	10	33	As I also in all things please all men, not seeking that which is profitable to myself, but to many, that may be saved.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	1	Be ye followers of me, as I also am of Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	2	Now I praise you, brethren, that in all things you are mindful of me: and keep my ordinances as I have delivered them to you.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	3	But I would have you know, that the head of every man is Christ; and the head of the woman is the man; and the head of Christ is God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	4	Every man praying or prophesying with his head covered, disgraceth his head.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	5	But every woman praying or prophesying with her head not covered, disgraceth her head: for it is all one as if she were shaven.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	6	For if a woman be not covered, let her be shorn. But if it be a shame to a woman to be shorn or made bald, let her cover her head.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	7	The man indeed ought not to cover his head, because he is the image and glory of God; but the woman is the glory of the man.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	8	For the man is not of the woman, but the woman of the man.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	9	For the man was not created for the woman, but the woman for the man.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	10	Therefore ought the woman to have a power over her head, because of the angels.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	11	But yet neither is the man without the woman, nor the woman without the man, in the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	12	For as the woman is of the man, so also is the man by the woman: but all things of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	13	You yourselves judge: doth it become a woman, to pray unto God uncovered?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	14	Doth not even nature itself teach you, that a man indeed, if he nourish his hair, it is a shame unto him?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	15	But if a woman nourish her hair, it is a glory to her; for her hair is given to her for a covering.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	16	But if any man seem to be contentious, we have no such custom, nor the church of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	17	Now this I ordain: not praising you, that you come together not for the better, but for the worse.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	18	For first of all I hear that when you come together in the church, there are schisms among you; and in part I believe it.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	19	For there must be also heresies: that they also, who are approved, may be made manifest among you.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	20	When you come therefore together into one place, it is not now to eat the Lord's supper.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	21	For every one taketh before his own supper to eat. And one indeed is hungry and another is drunk.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	22	What, have you not houses to eat and to drink in? Or despise ye the church of God; and put them to shame that have not? What shall I say to you? Do I praise you? In this I praise you not.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	23	For I have received of the Lord that which also I delivered unto you, that the Lord Jesus, the same night in which he was betrayed, took bread.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	24	And giving thanks, broke, and said: Take ye, and eat: this is my body, which shall be delivered for you: this do for the commemoration of me.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	25	In like manner also the chalice, after he had supped, saying: This chalice is the new testament in my blood: this do ye, as often as you shall drink, for the commemoration of me.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	26	For as often as you shall eat this bread, and drink the chalice, you shall shew the death of the Lord, until he come.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	27	Therefore whosoever shall eat this bread, or drink the chalice of the Lord unworthily, shall be guilty of the body and of the blood of the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	28	But let a man prove himself: and so let him eat of that bread, and drink of the chalice.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	29	For he that eateth and drinketh unworthily, eateth and drinketh judgment to himself, not discerning the body of the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	30	Therefore are there many infirm and weak among you, and many sleep.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	31	But if we would judge ourselves, we should not be judged.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	32	But whilst we are judged, we are chastised by the Lord, that we be not condemned with this world.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	33	Wherefore, my brethren, when you come together to eat, wait for one another.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	11	34	If any man be hungry, let him eat at home; that you come not together unto judgment. And the rest I will set in order, when I come.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	1	Now concerning spiritual things, my brethren, I would not have you ignorant.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	2	You know that when you were heathens, you went to dumb idols, according as you were led.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	3	Wherefore I give you to understand, that no man, speaking by the Spirit of God, saith Anathema to Jesus. And no man can say the Lord Jesus, but by the Holy Ghost.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	4	Now there are diversities of graces, but the same Spirit;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	5	And there are diversities of ministries, but the same Lord;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	6	And there are diversities of operations, but the same God, who worketh all in all.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	7	And the manifestation of the Spirit is given to every man unto profit.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	8	To one indeed, by the Spirit, is given the word of wisdom: and to another, the word of knowledge, according to the same Spirit;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	9	To another, faith in the same spirit; to another, the grace of healing in one Spirit;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	10	To another, the working of miracles; to another, prophecy; to another, the discerning of spirits; to another, diverse kinds of tongues; to another, interpretation of speeches.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	11	But all these things one and the same Spirit worketh, dividing to every one according as he will.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	12	For as the body is one, and hath many members; and all the members of the body, whereas they are many, yet are one body, so also is Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	13	For in one Spirit were we all baptized into one body, whether Jews or Gentiles, whether bond or free; and in one Spirit we have all been made to drink.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	14	For the body also is not one member, but many.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	15	If the foot should say, because I am not the hand, I am not of the body; is it therefore not of the body?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	16	And if the ear should say, because I am not the eye, I am not of the body; is it therefore not of the body?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	17	If the whole body were the eye, where would be the hearing? If the whole were hearing, where would be the smelling?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	18	But now God hath set the members every one of them in the body as it hath pleased him.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	19	And if they all were one member, where would be the body?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	20	But now there are many members indeed, yet one body.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	21	And the eye cannot say to the hand: I need not thy help; nor again the head to the feet: I have no need of you.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	22	Yea, much more those that seem to be the more feeble members of the body, are more necessary.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	23	And such as we think to be the less honourable members of the body, about these we put more abundant honour; and those that are our uncomely parts, have more abundant comeliness.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	24	But our comely parts have no need: but God hath tempered the body together, giving to that which wanted the more abundant honour,
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	25	That there might be no schism in the body; but the members might be mutually careful one for another.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	26	And if one member suffer any thing, all the members suffer with it; or if one member glory, all the members rejoice with it.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	27	Now you are the body of Christ, and members of member.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	28	And God indeed hath set some in the church; first apostles, secondly prophets, thirdly doctors; after that miracles; then the graces of healing, helps, governments, kinds of tongues, interpretations of speeches.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	29	Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all doctors?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	30	Are all workers of miracles? Have all the grace of healing? Do all speak with tongues? Do all interpret?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	12	31	But be zealous for the better gifts. And I shew unto you yet a more excellent way.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	1	If I speak with the tongues of men, and of angels, and have not charity, I am become as sounding brass, or a tinkling cymbal.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	2	And if I should have prophecy and should know all mysteries, and all knowledge, and if I should have all faith, so that I could remove mountains, and have not charity, I am nothing.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	3	And if I should distribute all my goods to feed the poor, and if I should deliver my body to be burned, and have not charity, it profiteth me nothing.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	4	Charity is patient, is kind: charity envieth not, dealeth not perversely; is not puffed up;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	5	Is not ambitious, seeketh not her own, is not provoked to anger, thinketh no evil;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	6	Rejoiceth not in iniquity, but rejoiceth with the truth;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	7	Beareth all things, believeth all things, hopeth all things, endureth all things.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	8	Charity never falleth away: whether prophecies shall be made void, or tongues shall cease, or knowledge shall be destroyed.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	9	For we know in part, and we prophesy in part.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	10	But when that which is perfect is come, that which is in part shall be done away.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	11	When I was a child, I spoke as a child, I understood as a child, I thought as a child. But, when I became a man, I put away the things of a child.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	12	We see now through a glass in a dark manner; but then face to face. Now I know in part; but then I shall know even as I am known.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	13	13	And now there remain faith, hope, and charity, these three: but the greatest of these is charity.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	1	Follow after charity, be zealous for spiritual gifts; but rather that you may prophesy.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	2	For he that speaketh in a tongue, speaketh not unto men, but unto God: for no man heareth. Yet by the Spirit he speaketh mysteries.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	3	But he that prophesieth, speaketh to men unto edification, and exhortation, and comfort.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	4	He that speaketh in a tongue, edifieth himself: but he that prophesieth, edifieth the church.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	5	And I would have you all to speak with tongues, but rather to prophesy. For greater is he that prophesieth, than he that speaketh with tongues: unless perhaps he interpret, that the church may receive edification.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	6	But now, brethren, if I come to you speaking with tongues, what shall I profit you, unless I speak to you either in revelation, or in knowledge, or in prophecy, or in doctrine?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	7	Even things without life that give sound, whether pipe or harp, except they give a distinction of sounds, how shall it be known what is piped or harped?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	8	For if the trumpet give an uncertain sound, who shall prepare himself to the battle?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	9	So likewise you, except you utter by the tongue plain speech, how shall it be known what is said? For you shall be speaking into the air.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	10	There are, for example, so many kinds of tongues in this world; and none is without voice.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	11	If then I know not the power of the voice, I shall be to him to whom I speak a barbarian; and he that speaketh, a barbarian to me.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	12	So you also, forasmuch as you are zealous of spirits, seek to abound unto the edifying of the church.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	13	And therefore he that speaketh by a tongue, let him pray that he may interpret.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	14	For if I pray in a tongue, my spirit prayeth, but my understanding is without fruit.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	15	What is it then? I will pray with the spirit, I will pray also with the understanding; I will sing with the spirit, I will sing also with the understanding.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	16	Else if thou shalt bless with the spirit, how shall he that holdeth the place of the unlearned say, Amen, to thy blessing? because he knoweth not what thou sayest.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	17	For thou indeed givest thanks well, but the other is not edified.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	18	I thank my God I speak with all your tongues.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	19	But in the church I had rather speak five words with my understanding, that I may instruct others also; than ten thousand words in a tongue.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	20	Brethren, do not become children in sense: but in malice be children, and in sense be perfect.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	21	In the law it is written: In other tongues and other lips I will speak to this people; and neither so will they hear me, saith the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	22	Wherefore tongues are for a sign, not to believers, but to unbelievers; but prophecies not to unbelievers, but to believers.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	23	If therefore the whole church come together into one place, and all speak with tongues, and there come in unlearned persons or infidels, will they not say that you are mad?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	24	But if all prophesy, and there come in one that believeth not, or an unlearned person, he is convinced of all, he is judged of all.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	25	The secrets of his heart are made manifest; and so, falling down on his face, he will adore God, affirming that God is among you indeed.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	26	How is it then, brethren? When you come together, every one of you hath a psalm, hath a doctrine, hath a revelation, hath a tongue, hath an interpretation: let all things be done to edification.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	27	If any speak with a tongue, let it be by two, or at the most by three, and in course, and let one interpret.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	28	But if there be no interpreter, let him hold his peace in the church, and speak to himself and to God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	29	And let the prophets speak, two or three; and let the rest judge.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	30	But if any thing be revealed to another sitting, let the first hold his peace.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	31	For you may all prophesy one by one; that all may learn, and all may be exhorted:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	32	And the spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	33	For God is not the God of dissension, but of peace: as also I teach in all the churches of the saints.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	34	Let women keep silence in the churches: for it is not permitted them to speak, but to be subject, as also the law saith.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	35	But if they would learn any thing, let them ask their husbands at home. For it is a shame for a woman to speak in the church.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	36	Or did the word of God come out from you? Or came it only unto you?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	37	If any seem to be a prophet, or spiritual, let him know the things that I write to you, that they are the commandments of the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	38	But if any man know not, he shall not be known.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	39	Wherefore, brethren, be zealous to prophesy; and forbid not to speak with tongues.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	14	40	But let all things be done decently, and according to order.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	1	Now I make known unto you, brethren, the gospel which I preached to you, which also you have received, and wherein you stand;
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	2	By which also you are saved, if you hold fast after what manner I preached unto you, unless you have believed in vain.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	3	For I delivered unto you first of all, which I also received: how that Christ died for our sins, according to the scriptures:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	4	And that he was buried, and that he rose again the third day, according to the scriptures:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	5	And that he was seen by Cephas; and after that by the eleven.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	6	Then he was seen by more than five hundred brethren at once: of whom many remain until this present, and some are fallen asleep.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	7	After that, he was seen by James, then by all the apostles.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	8	And last of all, he was seen also by me, as by one born out of due time.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	9	For I am the least of the apostles, who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	10	But by the grace of God, I am what I am; and his grace in me hath not been void, but I have laboured more abundantly than all they: yet not I, but the grace of God with me.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	11	For whether I, or they, so we preach, and so you have believed.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	12	Now if Christ be preached, that he arose again from the dead, how do some among you say, that there is no resurrection of the dead?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	13	But if there be no resurrection of the dead, then Christ is not risen again.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	14	And if Christ be not risen again, then is our preaching vain, and your faith is also vain.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	15	Yea, and we are found false witnesses of God: because we have given testimony against God, that he hath raised up Christ; whom he hath not raised up, if the dead rise not again.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	16	For if the dead rise not again, neither is Christ risen again.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	17	And if Christ be not risen again, your faith is vain, for you are yet in your sins.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	18	Then they also that are fallen asleep in Christ, are perished.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	19	If in this life only we have hope in Christ, we are of all men most miserable.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	20	But now Christ is risen from the dead, the firstfruits of them that sleep:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	21	For by a man came death, and by a man the resurrection of the dead.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	22	And as in Adam all die, so also in Christ all shall be made alive.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	23	But every one in his own order: the firstfruits Christ, then they that are of Christ, who have believed in his coming.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	24	Afterwards the end, when he shall have delivered up the kingdom to God and the Father, when he shall have brought to nought all principality, and power, and virtue.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	25	For he must reign, until he hath put all his enemies under his feet.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	26	And the enemy death shall be destroyed last: For he hath put all things under his feet. And whereas he saith,
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	27	All things are put under him; undoubtedly, he is excepted, who put all things under him.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	28	And when all things shall be subdued unto him, then the Son also himself shall be subject unto him that put all things under him, that God may be all in all.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	29	Otherwise what shall they do that are baptized for the dead, if the dead rise not again at all? why are they then baptized for them?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	30	Why also are we in danger every hour?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	31	I die daily, I protest by your glory, brethren, which I have in Christ Jesus our Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	32	If (according to man) I fought with beasts at Ephesus, what doth it profit me, if the dead rise not again? Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	33	Be not seduced: Evil communications corrupt good manners.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	34	Awake, ye just, and sin not. For some have not the knowledge of God, I speak it to your shame.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	35	But some man will say: How do the dead rise again? or with what manner of body shall they come?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	36	Senseless man, that which thou sowest is not quickened, except it die first.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	37	And that which thou sowest, thou sowest not the body that shall be; but bare grain, as of wheat, or of some of the rest.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	38	But God giveth it a body as he will: and to every seed its proper body.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	39	All flesh is not the same flesh: but one is the flesh of men, another of beasts, another of birds, another of fishes.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	40	And there are bodies celestial, and bodies terrestrial: but, one is the glory of the celestial, and another of the terrestrial.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	41	One is the glory of the sun, another the glory of the moon, and another the glory of the stars. For star differeth from star in glory.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	42	So also is the resurrection of the dead. It is sown in corruption, it shall rise in incorruption.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	43	It is sown in dishonour, it shall rise in glory. It is sown in weakness, it shall rise in power.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	44	It is sown a natural body, it shall rise a spiritual body. If there be a natural body, there is also a spiritual body, as it is written:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	45	The first man Adam was made into a living soul; the last Adam into a quickening spirit.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	46	Yet that was not first which is spiritual, but that which is natural; afterwards that which is spiritual.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	47	The first man was of the earth, earthly: the second man, from heaven, heavenly.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	48	Such as is the earthly, such also are the earthly: and such as is the heavenly, such also are they that are heavenly.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	49	Therefore as we have borne the image of the earthly, let us bear also the image of the heavenly.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	50	Now this I say, brethren, that flesh and blood cannot possess the kingdom of God: neither shall corruption possess incorruption.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	51	Behold, I tell you a mystery. We shall all indeed rise again: but we shall not all be changed.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	52	In a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet: for the trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall rise again incorruptible: and we shall be changed.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	53	For this corruptible must put on incorruption; and this mortal must put on immortality.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	54	And when this mortal hath put on immortality, then shall come to pass the saying that is written: Death is swallowed up in victory.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	55	O death, where is thy victory? O death, where is thy sting?
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	56	Now the sting of death is sin: and the power of sin is the law.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	57	But thanks be to God, who hath given us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	15	58	Therefore, my beloved brethren, be ye steadfast and unmoveable; always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	1	Now concerning the collections that are made for the saints, as I have given order to the churches of Galatia, so do ye also.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	2	On the first day of the week let every one of you put apart with himself, laying up what it shall well please him; that when I come, the collections be not then to be made.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	3	And when I shall be with you, whomsoever you shall approve by letters, them will I send to carry your grace to Jerusalem.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	4	And if it be meet that I also go, they shall go with me.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	5	Now I will come to you, when I shall have passed through Macedonia. For I shall pass through Macedonia.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	6	And with you perhaps I shall abide, or even spend the winter: that you may bring me on my way whithersoever I shall go.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	7	For I will not see you now by the way, for I trust that I shall abide with you some time, if the Lord permit.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	8	But I will tarry at Ephesus until Pentecost.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	9	For a great door and evident is opened unto me: and many adversaries.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	10	Now if Timothy come, see that he be with you without fear, for he worketh the work of the Lord, as I also do.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	11	Let no man therefore despise him, but conduct ye him on his way in peace: that he may come to me. For I look for him with the brethren.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	12	And touching our brother Apollo, I give you to understand, that I much entreated him to come unto you with the brethren: and indeed it was not his will at all to come at this time. But he will come when he shall have leisure.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	13	Watch ye, stand fast in the faith, do manfully, and be strengthened.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	14	Let all your things be done in charity.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	15	And I beseech you, brethren, you know the house of Stephanas, and of Fortunatus, and of Achaicus, that they are the firstfruits of Achaia, and have dedicated themselves to the ministry of the saints:
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	16	That you also be subject to such, and to every one that worketh with us, and laboureth.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	17	And I rejoice in the presence of Stephanas, and Fortunatus, and Achaicus, because that which was wanting on your part, they have supplied.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	18	For they have refreshed both my spirit and yours. Know them, therefore, that are such.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	19	The churches of Asia salute you. Aquila and Priscilla salute you much in the Lord, with the church that is in their house, with whom I also lodge.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	20	All the brethren salute you. Salute one another with a holy kiss.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	21	The salutation of me Paul, with my own hand.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	22	If any man love not our Lord Jesus Christ, let him be anathema, maranatha.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	23	The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you.
1 Corinthians	1Cor	53	16	24	My charity be with you all in Christ Jesus. Amen.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	1	Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Timothy our brother: to the church of God that is at Corinth, with all the saints that are in all Achaia:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	2	Grace unto you and peace from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	3	Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies, and the God of all comfort.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	4	Who comforteth us in all our tribulation; that we also may be able to comfort them who are in all distress, by the exhortation wherewith we also are exhorted by God.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	5	For as the sufferings of Christ abound in us: so also by Christ doth our comfort abound.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	6	Now whether we be in tribulation, it is for your exhortation and salvation: or whether we be comforted, it is for your consolation: or whether we be exhorted, it is for your exhortation and salvation, which worketh the enduring of the same sufferings which we also suffer.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	7	That our hope for you may be steadfast: knowing that as you are partakers of the sufferings, so shall you be also of the consolation.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	8	For we would not have you ignorant, brethren, of our tribulation, which came to us in Asia, that we were pressed out of measure above our strength, so that we were weary even of life.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	9	But we had in ourselves the answer of death, that we should not trust in ourselves, but in God who raiseth the dead.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	10	Who hath delivered and doth deliver us out of so great dangers: in whom we trust that he will yet also deliver us.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	11	You helping withal in prayer for us: that for this gift obtained for us, by the means of many persons, thanks may be given by many in our behalf.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	12	For our glory is this, the testimony of our conscience, that in simplicity of heart and sincerity of God, and not in carnal wisdom, but in the grace of God, we have conversed in this world: and more abundantly towards you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	13	For we write no other things to you than what you have read and known. And I hope that you shall know unto the end:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	14	As also you have known us in part, that we are your glory, as you also are ours, in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	15	And in this confidence I had a mind to come to you before, that you might have a second grace:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	16	And to pass by you into Macedonia, and again from Macedonia to come to you, and by you to be brought on my way towards Judea.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	17	Whereas then I was thus minded, did I use lightness? Or, the things that I purpose, do I purpose according to the flesh, that there should be with me, It is, and It is not?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	18	But God is faithful, for our preaching which was to you, was not, It is, and It is not.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	19	For the Son of God, Jesus Christ who was preached among you by us, by me, and Sylvanus, and Timothy, was not, It is and It is not, but, It is, was in him.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	20	For all the promises of God are in him, It is; therefore also by him, amen to God, unto our glory.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	21	Now he that confirmeth us with you in Christ, and that hath anointed us, is God:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	22	Who also hath sealed us, and given the pledge of the Spirit in our hearts.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	1	23	But I call God to witness upon my soul, that to spare you, I came not any more to Corinth: not because we exercise dominion over your faith: but we are helpers of your joy: for in faith you stand.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	1	But I determined this with myself, not to come to you again in sorrow.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	2	For if I make you sorrowful, who is he then that can make me glad, but the same who is made sorrowful by me?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	3	And I wrote this same to you; that I may not, when I come, have sorrow upon sorrow, from them of whom I ought to rejoice: having confidence in you all, that my joy is the joy of you all.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	4	For out of much affliction and anguish of heart, I wrote to you with many tears: not that you should be made sorrowful: but that you might know the charity I have more abundantly towards you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	5	And if any one have caused grief, he hath not grieved me; but in part, that I may not burden you all.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	6	To him who is such a one, this rebuke is sufficient, which is given by many:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	7	So that on the contrary, you should rather forgive him and comfort him, lest perhaps such a one be swallowed up with overmuch sorrow.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	8	Wherefore, I beseech you, that you would confirm your charity towards him.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	9	For to this end also did I write, that I may know the experiment of you, whether you be obedient in all things.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	10	And to whom you have pardoned any thing, I also. For, what I have pardoned, if I have pardoned any thing, for your sakes have I done it in the person of Christ.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	11	That we be not overreached by Satan. For we are not ignorant of his devices.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	12	And when I was come to Troas for the gospel of Christ, and a door was opened unto me in the Lord,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	13	I had no rest in my spirit, because I found not Titus my brother; but bidding them farewell, I went into Macedonia.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	14	Now thanks be to God, who always maketh us to triumph in Christ Jesus, and manifesteth the odour of his knowledge by us in every place.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	15	For we are the good odour of Christ unto God, in them that are saved, and in them that perish.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	16	To the one indeed the odour of death unto death: but to the others the odour of life unto life. And for these things who is so sufficient?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	2	17	For we are not as many, adulterating the word of God; but with sincerity, but as from God, before God, in Christ we speak.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	1	Do we begin again to commend ourselves? Or do we need (as some do) epistles of commendation to you, or from you?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	2	You are our epistle, written in our hearts, which is known and read by all men:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	3	Being manifested, that you are the epistle of Christ, ministered by us, and written not with ink, but with the Spirit of the living God; not in tables of stone, but in the fleshly tables of the heart.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	4	And such confidence we have, through Christ, towards God.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	5	Not that we are sufficient to think any thing of ourselves, as of ourselves: but our sufficiency is from God.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	6	Who also hath made us fit ministers of the new testament, not in the letter, but in the spirit. For the letter killeth, but the spirit quickeneth.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	7	Now if the ministration of death, engraven with letters upon stones, was glorious; so that the children of Israel could not steadfastly behold the face of Moses, for the glory of his countenance, which is made void:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	8	How shall not the ministration of the spirit be rather in glory?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	9	For if the ministration of condemnation be glory, much more the ministration of justice aboundeth in glory.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	10	For even that which was glorious in this part was not glorified, by reason of the glory that excelleth.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	11	For if that which is done away was glorious, much more that which remaineth is in glory.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	12	Having therefore such hope, we use much confidence:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	13	And not as Moses put a veil upon his face, that the children of Israel might not steadfastly look on the face of that which is made void.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	14	But their senses were made dull. For, until this present day, the selfsame veil, in the reading of the old testament, remaineth not taken away (because in Christ it is made void).
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	15	But even until this day, when Moses is read, the veil is upon their heart.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	16	But when they shall be converted to the Lord, the veil shall be taken away.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	17	Now the Lord is a Spirit. And where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	3	18	But we all beholding the glory of the Lord with open face, are transformed into the same image from glory to glory, as by the Spirit of the Lord.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	1	Therefore, seeing we have this ministration, according as we have obtained mercy, we faint not;
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	2	But we renounce the hidden things of dishonesty, not walking in craftiness, nor adulterating the word of God; but by manifestation of the truth commending ourselves to every man's conscience, in the sight of God.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	3	And if our gospel be also hid, it is hid to them that are lost,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	4	In whom the god of this world hath blinded the minds of unbelievers, that the light of the gospel of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God, should not shine unto them.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	5	For we preach not ourselves, but Jesus Christ our Lord; and ourselves your servants through Jesus.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	6	For God, who commanded the light to shine out of darkness, hath shined in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God, in the face of Christ Jesus.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	7	But we have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellency may be of the power of God, and not of us.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	8	In all things we suffer tribulation, but are not distressed; we are straitened, but are not destitute;
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	9	We suffer persecution, but are not forsaken; we are cast down, but we perish not:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	10	Always bearing about in our body the mortification of Jesus, that the life also of Jesus may be made manifest in our bodies.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	11	For we who live are always delivered unto death for Jesus' sake; that the life also of Jesus may be made manifest in our mortal flesh.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	12	So then death worketh in us, but life in you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	13	But having the same spirit of faith, as it is written: I believed, for which cause I have spoken; we also believe, for which cause we speak also:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	14	Knowing that he who raised up Jesus, will raise us up also with Jesus, and place us with you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	15	For all things are for your sakes; that the grace abounding through many, may abound in thanksgiving unto the glory of God.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	16	For which cause we faint not; but though our outward man is corrupted, yet the inward man is renewed day by day.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	17	For that which is at present momentary and light of our tribulation, worketh for us above measure exceedingly an eternal weight of glory.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	4	18	While we look not at the things which are seen, but at the things which are not seen. For the things which are seen, are temporal; but the things which are not seen, are eternal.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	1	For we know, if our earthly house of this habitation be dissolved, that we have a building of God, a house not made with hands, eternal in heaven.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	2	For in this also we groan, desiring to be clothed upon with our habitation that is from heaven.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	3	Yet so that we be found clothed, not naked.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	4	For we also, who are in this tabernacle, do groan, being burthened; because we would not be unclothed, but clothed upon, that that which is mortal may be swallowed up by life.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	5	Now he that maketh us for this very thing, is God, who hath given us the pledge of the Spirit.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	6	Therefore having always confidence, knowing that, while we are in the body, we are absent from the Lord.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	7	(For we walk by faith, and not by sight.)
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	8	But we are confident, and have a good will to be absent rather from the body, and to be present with the Lord.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	9	And therefore we labour, whether absent or present, to please him.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	10	For we must all be manifested before the judgment seat of Christ, that every one may receive the proper things of the body, according as he hath done, whether it be good or evil.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	11	Knowing therefore the fear of the Lord, we use persuasion to men; but to God we are manifest. And I trust also that in your consciences we are manifest.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	12	We commend not ourselves again to you, but give you occasion to glory in our behalf; that you may have somewhat to answer them who glory in face, and not in heart.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	13	For whether we be transported in mind, it is to God; or whether we be sober, it is for you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	14	For the charity of Christ presseth us: judging this, that if one died for all, then all were dead.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	15	And Christ died for all; that they also who live, may not now live to themselves, but unto him who died for them, and rose again.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	16	Wherefore henceforth, we know no man according to the flesh. And if we have known Christ according to the flesh; but now we know him so no longer.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	17	If then any be in Christ a new creature, the old things are passed away, behold all things are made new.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	18	But all things are of God, who hath reconciled us to himself by Christ; and hath given to us the ministry of reconciliation.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	19	For God indeed was in Christ, reconciling the world to himself, not imputing to them their sins; and he hath placed in us the word of reconciliation.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	20	For Christ therefore we are ambassadors, God as it were exhorting by us. For Christ, we beseech you, be reconciled to God.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	5	21	Him, who knew no sin, he hath made sin for us, that we might be made the justice of God in him.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	1	And we helping do exhort you, that you receive not the grace of God in vain.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	2	For he saith: In an accepted time have I heard thee; and in the day of salvation have I helped thee. Behold, now is the acceptable time; behold, now is the day of salvation.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	3	Giving no offence to any man, that our ministry be not blamed:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	4	But in all things let us exhibit ourselves as the ministers of God, in much patience, in tribulation, in necessities, in distresses,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	5	In stripes, in prisons, in seditions, in labours, in watchings, in fastings,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	6	In chastity, in knowledge, in longsuffering, in sweetness, in the Holy Ghost, in charity unfeigned,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	7	In the word of truth, in the power of God; by the armour of justice on the right hand and on the left;
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	8	By honour and dishonour, by evil report and good report; as deceivers, and yet true; as unknown, and yet known;
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	9	As dying, and behold we live; as chastised, and not killed;
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	10	As sorrowful, yet always rejoicing; as needy, yet enriching many; as having nothing, and possessing all things.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	11	Our mouth is open to you, O ye Corinthians, our heart is enlarged.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	12	You are not straitened in us, but in your own bowels you are straitened.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	13	But having the same recompense, (I speak as to my children,) be you also enlarged.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	14	Bear not the yoke with unbelievers. For what participation hath justice with injustice? Or what fellowship hath light with darkness?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	15	And what concord hath Christ with Belial? Or what part hath the faithful with the unbeliever?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	16	And what agreement hath the temple of God with idols? For you are the temple of the living God; as God saith: I will dwell in them, and walk among them; and I will be their God, and they shall be my people.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	17	Wherefore, Go out from among them, and be ye separate, saith the Lord, and touch not the unclean thing:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	6	18	And I will receive you; and I will be a Father to you; and you shall be my sons and daughters, saith the Lord Almighty.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	1	Having therefore these promises, dearly beloved, let us cleanse ourselves from all defilement of the flesh and of the spirit, perfecting sanctification in the fear of God.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	2	Receive us. We have injured no man, we have corrupted no man, we have overreached no man.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	3	I speak not this to your condemnation. For we have said before, that you are in our hearts, to die together, and to live together.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	4	Great is my confidence for you, great is my glorying for you. I am filled with comfort: I exceedingly abound with joy in all our tribulation.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	5	For also when we were come into Macedonia, our flesh had no rest, but we suffered all tribulation; combats without, fears within.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	6	But God, who comforteth the humble, comforted us by the coming of Titus.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	7	And not by his coming only, but also by the consolation, wherewith he was comforted in you, relating to us your desire, your mourning, your zeal for me, so that I rejoiced the more.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	8	For although I made you sorrowful by my epistle, I do not repent; and if I did repent, seeing that the same epistle (although but for a time) did make you sorrowful;
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	9	Now I am glad: not because you were made sorrowful; but because you were made sorrowful unto penance. For you were made sorrowful according to God, that you might suffer damage by us in nothing.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	10	For the sorrow that is according to God worketh penance, steadfast unto salvation; but the sorrow of the world worketh death.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	11	For behold this selfsame thing, that you were made sorrowful according to God, how great carefulness it worketh in you; yea defence, yea indignation, yea fear, yea desire, yea zeal, yea revenge: in all things you have shewed yourselves to be undefiled in the matter.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	12	Wherefore although I wrote to you, it was not for his sake that did the wrong, nor for him that suffered it; but to manifest our carefulness that we have for you
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	13	Before God: therefore we were comforted. But in our consolation, we did the more abundantly rejoice for the joy of Titus, because his spirit was refreshed by you all.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	14	And if I have boasted any thing to him of you, I have not been put to shame; but as we have spoken all things to you in truth, so also our boasting that was made to Titus is found a truth.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	15	And his bowels are more abundantly towards you; remembering the obedience of you all, how with fear and trembling you have received him.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	7	16	I rejoice that in all things I have confidence in you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	1	Now we make known unto you, brethren, the grace of God, that hath been given in the churches of Macedonia.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	2	That in much experience of tribulation, they have had abundance of joy; and their very deep poverty hath abounded unto the riches of their simplicity.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	3	For according to their power (I bear them witness), and beyond their power, they were willing.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	4	With much entreaty begging of us the grace and communication of the ministry that is done toward the saints.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	5	And not as we hoped, but they gave their own selves first to the Lord, then to us by the will of God:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	6	Insomuch, that we desired Titus, that as he had begun, so also he would finish among you this same grace.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	7	That as in all things you abound in faith, and word, and knowledge, and all carefulness; moreover also in your charity towards us, so in this grace also you may abound.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	8	I speak not as commanding; but by the carefulness of others, approving also the good disposition of your charity.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	9	For you know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that being rich he became poor, for your sakes; that through his poverty you might be rich.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	10	And herein I give my advice; for this is profitable for you, who have begun not only to do, but also to be willing, a year ago.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	11	Now therefore perform ye it also in deed; that as your mind is forward to be willing, so it may be also to perform, out of that which you have.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	12	For if the will be forward, it is accepted according to that which a man hath, not according to that which he hath not.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	13	For I mean not that others should be eased, and you burthened, but by an equality.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	14	In this present time let your abundance supply their want, that their abundance also may supply your want, that there may be an equality,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	15	As it is written: He that had much, had nothing over; and he that had little, had no want.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	16	And thanks be to God, who hath given the same carefulness for you in the heart of Titus.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	17	For indeed he accepted the exhortation; but being more careful, of his own will he went unto you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	18	We have sent also with him the brother, whose praise is in the gospel through all the churches.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	19	And not that only, but he was also ordained by the churches companion of our travels, for this grace, which is administered by us, to the glory of the Lord, and our determined will:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	20	Avoiding this, lest any man should blame us in this abundance which is administered by us.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	21	For we forecast what may be good not only before God, but also before men.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	22	And we have sent with them our brother also, whom we have often proved diligent in many things; but now much more diligent, with much confidence in you,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	23	Either for Titus, who is my companion and fellow labourer towards you, or our brethren, the apostles of the churches, the glory of Christ.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	8	24	Wherefore shew ye to them, in the sight of the churches, the evidence of your charity, and of our boasting on your behalf.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	1	For concerning the ministry that is done towards the saints, it is superfluous for me to write unto you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	2	For I know your forward mind: for which I boast of you to the Macedonians. That Achaia also is ready from the year past, and your emulation hath provoked very many.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	3	Now I have sent the brethren, that the thing which we boast of concerning you, be not made void in this behalf, that (as I have said) you may be ready:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	4	Lest, when the Macedonians shall come with me, and find you unprepared, we (not to say ye) should be ashamed in this matter.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	5	Therefore I thought it necessary to desire the brethren that they would go to you before, and prepare this blessing before promised, to be ready, so as a blessing, not as covetousness.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	6	Now this I say: He who soweth sparingly, shall also reap sparingly: and he who soweth in blessings, shall also reap blessings.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	7	Every one as he hath determined in his heart, not with sadness, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	8	And God is able to make all grace abound in you; that ye always, having all sufficiency in all things, may abound to every good work,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	9	As it is written: He hath dispersed abroad, he hath given to the poor: his justice remaineth for ever.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	10	And he that ministereth seed to the sower, will both give you bread to eat, and will multiply your seed, and increase the growth of the fruits of your justice:
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	11	That being enriched in all things, you may abound unto all simplicity, which worketh through us thanksgiving to God.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	12	Because the administration of this office doth not only supply the want of the saints, but aboundeth also by many thanksgivings in the Lord,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	13	By the proof of this ministry, glorifying God for the obedience of your confession unto the gospel of Christ, and for the simplicity of your communicating unto them, and unto all.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	14	And in their praying for you, being desirous of you, because of the excellent grace of God in you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	9	15	Thanks be to God for his unspeakable gift.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	1	Now I Paul myself beseech you, by the mildness and modesty of Christ, who in presence indeed am lowly among you, but being absent, am bold toward you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	2	But I beseech you, that I may not be bold when I am present, with that confidence wherewith I am thought to be bold, against some, who reckon us as if we walked according to the flesh.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	3	For though we walk in the flesh, we do not war according to the flesh.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	4	For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty to God unto the pulling down of fortifications, destroying counsels,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	5	And every height that exhalteth itself against the knowledge of God, and bringing into captivity every understanding unto the obedience of Christ;
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	6	And having in readiness to revenge all disobedience, when your obedience shall be fulfilled.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	7	See the things that are according to outward appearance. If any man trust to himself, that he is Christ's, let him think this again with himself, that as he is Christ's, so are we also.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	8	For if also I should boast somewhat more of our power, which the Lord hath given us unto edification, and not for your destruction, I should not be ashamed.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	9	But that I may not be thought as it were to terrify you by epistles,
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	10	(For his epistles indeed, say they, are weighty and strong; but his bodily presence is weak, and his speech contemptible,)
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	11	Let such a one think this, that such as we are in word by epistles, when absent, such also we will be indeed when present.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	12	For we dare not match, or compare ourselves with some, that commend themselves; but we measure ourselves by ourselves, and compare ourselves with ourselves.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	13	But we will not glory beyond our measure; but according to the measure of the rule, which God hath measured to us, a measure to reach even unto you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	14	For we stretch not ourselves beyond our measure, as if we reached not unto you. For we are come as far as to you in the gospel of Christ.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	15	Not glorying beyond measure in other men's labours; but having hope of your increasing faith, to be magnified in you according to our rule abundantly;
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	16	Yea, unto those places that are beyond you, to preach the gospel, not to glory in another man's rule, in those things that are made ready to our hand.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	17	But he that glorieth, let him glory in the Lord.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	10	18	For not he who commendeth himself, is approved, but he, whom God commendeth.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	1	Would to God you could bear with some little of my folly: but do bear with me.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	2	For I am jealous of you with the jealousy of God. For I have espoused you to one husband that I may present you as a chaste virgin to Christ.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	3	But I fear lest, as the serpent seduced Eve by his subtilty, so your minds should be corrupted, and fall from the simplicity that is in Christ.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	4	For if he that cometh preacheth another Christ, whom we have not preached; or if you receive another Spirit, whom you have not received; or another gospel which you have not received; you might well bear with him.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	5	For I suppose that I have done nothing less than the great apostles.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	6	For although I be rude in speech, yet not in knowledge; but in all things we have been made manifest to you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	7	Or did I commit a fault, humbling myself, that you might be exalted? Because I preached unto you the gospel of God freely?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	8	I have taken from other churches, receiving wages of them for your ministry.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	9	And, when I was present with you, and wanted, I was chargeable to no man: for that which was wanting to me, the brethren supplied who came from Macedonia; and in all things I have kept myself from being burthensome to you, and so I will keep myself.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	10	The truth of Christ is in me, that this glorying shall not be broken off in me in the regions of Achaia.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	11	Wherefore? Because I love you not? God knoweth it.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	12	But what I do, that I will do, that I may cut off the occasion from them that desire occasion, that wherein they glory, they may be found even as we.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	13	For such false apostles are deceitful workmen, transforming themselves into the apostles of Christ.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	14	And no wonder: for Satan himself transformeth himself into an angel of light.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	15	Therefore it is no great thing if his ministers be transformed as the ministers of justice, whose end shall be according to their works.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	16	I say again, (let no man think me to be foolish, otherwise take me as one foolish, that I also may glory a little.)
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	17	That which I speak, I speak not according to God, but as it were in foolishness, in this matter of glorying.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	18	Seeing that many glory according to the flesh, I will glory also.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	19	For you gladly suffer the foolish; whereas yourselves are wise.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	20	For you suffer if a man bring you into bondage, if a man devour you, if a man take from you, if a man be lifted up, if a man strike you on the face.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	21	I speak according to dishonour, as if we had been weak in this part. Wherein if any man dare (I speak foolishly), I dare also.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	22	They are Hebrews: so am I. They are Israelites: so am I. They are the seed of Abraham: so am I.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	23	They are the ministers of Christ (I speak as one less wise). I am more; in many more labours, in prisons more frequently, in stripes above measure, in deaths often.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	24	Of the Jews five times did I receive forty stripes, save one.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	25	Thrice was I beaten with rods, once I was stoned, thrice I suffered shipwreck, a night and a day I was in the depth of the sea.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	26	In journeying often, in perils of waters, in perils of robbers, in perils from my own nation, in perils from the Gentiles, in perils in the city, in perils in the wilderness, in perils in the sea, in perils from false brethren.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	27	In labour and painfulness, in much watchings, in hunger and thirst, in fastings often, in cold and nakedness.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	28	Besides those things which are without: my daily instance, the solicitude for all the churches.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	29	Who is weak, and I am not weak? Who is scandalized, and I am not on fire?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	30	If I must needs glory, I will glory of the things that concern my infirmity.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	31	The God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who is blessed for ever, knoweth that I lie not.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	32	At Damascus, the governor of the nation under Aretas the king, guarded the city of the Damascenes, to apprehend me.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	11	33	And through a window in a basket was I let down by the wall, and so escaped his hands.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	1	If I must glory (it is not expedient indeed), but I will come to visions and revelations of the Lord.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	2	I know a man in Christ above fourteen years ago (whether in the body, I know not, or out of the body, I know not; God knoweth), such a one caught up to the third heaven.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	3	And I know such a man (whether in the body, or out of the body, I know not: God knoweth),
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	4	That he was caught up into paradise, and heard secret words, which it is not granted to man to utter.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	5	For such an one I will glory; but for myself I will glory nothing, but in my infirmities.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	6	For though I should have a mind to glory, I shall not be foolish; for I will say the truth. But I forbear, lest any man should think of me above that which he seeth in me, or any thing he heareth from me.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	7	And lest the greatness of the revelations should exalt me, there was given me a sting of my flesh, an angel of Satan, to buffet me.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	8	For which thing thrice I besought the Lord, that it might depart from me.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	9	And he said to me: My grace is sufficient for thee; for power is made perfect in infirmity. Gladly therefore will I glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may dwell in me.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	10	For which cause I please myself in my infirmities, in reproaches, in necessities, in persecutions, in distresses, for Christ. For when I am weak, then am I powerful.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	11	I am become foolish: you have compelled me. For I ought to have been commended by you: for I have no way come short of them that are above measure apostles, although I be nothing.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	12	Yet the signs of my apostleship have been wrought on you, in all patience, in signs, and wonders, and mighty deeds.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	13	For what is there that you have had less than the other churches, but that I myself was not burthensome to you? Pardon me this injury.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	14	Behold now the third time I am ready to come to you; and I will not be burthensome unto you. For I seek not the things that are yours, but you. For neither ought the children to lay up for the parents, but the parents for the children.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	15	But I most gladly will spend and be spent myself for your souls; although loving you more, I be loved less.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	16	But be it so: I did not burthen you: but being crafty, I caught you by guile.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	17	Did I overreach you by any of them whom I sent to you?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	18	I desired Titus, and I sent with him a brother. Did Titus overreach you? Did we not walk with the same spirit? did we not in the same steps?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	19	Of old, think you that we excuse ourselves to you? We speak before God in Christ; but all things, my dearly beloved, for your edification.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	20	For I fear lest perhaps when I come I shall not find you such as I would, and that I shall be found by you such as you would not. Lest perhaps contentions, envyings, animosities, dissensions, detractions, whisperings, swellings, seditions, be among you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	12	21	Lest again, when I come, God humble me among you: and I mourn many of them that sinned before, and have not done penance for the uncleanness, and fornication, and lasciviousness, that they have committed.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	1	Behold, this is the third time I am coming to you: In the mouth of two or three witnesses shall every word stand.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	2	I have told before, and foretell, as present, and now absent, to them that sinned before, and to all the rest, that if I come again, I will not spare.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	3	Do you seek a proof of Christ that speaketh in me, who towards you is not weak, but is mighty in you?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	4	For although he was crucified through weakness, yet he liveth by the power of God. For we also are weak in him: but we shall live with him by the power of God towards you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	5	Try your own selves if you be in the faith; prove ye yourselves. Know you not your own selves, that Christ Jesus is in you, unless perhaps you be reprobates?
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	6	But I trust that you shall know that we are not reprobates.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	7	Now we pray God, that you may do no evil, not that we may appear approved, but that you may do that which is good, and that we may be as reprobates.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	8	For we can do nothing against the truth; but for the truth.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	9	For we rejoice that we are weak, and you are strong. This also we pray for, your perfection.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	10	Therefore I write these things, being absent, that, being present, I may not deal more severely, according to the power which the Lord hath given me unto edification, and not unto destruction.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	11	For the rest, brethren, rejoice, be perfect, take exhortation, be of one mind, have peace; and the God of peace and of love shall be with you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	12	Salute one another with a holy kiss. All the saints salute you.
2 Corinthians	2Cor	54	13	13	The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, and the charity of God, and the communication of the Holy Ghost be with you all. Amen.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	1	Paul, an apostle, not of men, neither by man, but by Jesus Christ, and God the Father, who raised him from the dead,
Galatians	Gal	55	1	2	And all the brethren who are with me, to the churches of Galatia.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	3	Grace be to you, and peace from God the Father, and from our Lord Jesus Christ,
Galatians	Gal	55	1	4	Who gave himself for our sins, that he might deliver us from this present wicked world, according to the will of God and our Father:
Galatians	Gal	55	1	5	To whom is glory for ever and ever. Amen.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	6	I wonder that you are so soon removed from him that called you into the grace of Christ, unto another gospel.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	7	Which is not another, only there are some that trouble you, and would pervert the gospel of Christ.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	8	But though we, or an angel from heaven, preach a gospel to you besides that which we have preached to you, let him be anathema.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	9	As we said before, so now I say again: If any one preach to you a gospel, besides that which you have received, let him be anathema.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	10	For do I now persuade men, or God? Or do I seek to please men? If I yet pleased men, I should not be the servant of Christ.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	11	For I give you to understand, brethren, that the gospel which was preached by me is not according to man.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	12	For neither did I receive it of man, nor did I learn it; but by the revelation of Jesus Christ.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	13	For you have heard of my conversation in time past in the Jews' religion: how that, beyond measure, I persecuted the church of God, and wasted it.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	14	And I made progress in the Jews' religion above many of my equals in my own nation, being more abundantly zealous for the traditions of my fathers.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	15	But when it pleased him, who separated me from my mother's womb, and called me by his grace,
Galatians	Gal	55	1	16	To reveal his Son in me, that I might preach him among the Gentiles, immediately I condescended not to flesh and blood.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	17	Neither went I to Jerusalem, to the apostles who were before me: but I went into Arabia, and again I returned to Damascus.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	18	Then, after three years, I went to Jerusalem, to see Peter, and I tarried with him fifteen days.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	19	But other of the apostles I saw none, saving James the brother of the Lord.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	20	Now the things which I write to you, behold, before God, I lie not.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	21	Afterwards I came into the regions of Syria and Cilicia.
Galatians	Gal	55	1	22	And I was unknown by face to the churches of Judea, which were in Christ:
Galatians	Gal	55	1	23	But they had heard only: He, who persecuted us in times past, doth now preach the faith which once he impugned:
Galatians	Gal	55	1	24	And they glorified God in me.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	1	Then, after fourteen years, I went up again to Jerusalem with Barnabas, taking Titus also with me.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	2	And I went up according to revelation; and communicated to them the gospel, which I preach among the Gentiles, but apart to them who seemed to be some thing: lest perhaps I should run, or had run in vain.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	3	But neither Titus, who was with me, being a Gentile, was compelled to be circumcised.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	4	But because of false brethren unawares brought in, who came in privately to spy our liberty, which we have in Christ Jesus, that they might bring us into servitude.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	5	To whom we yielded not by subjection, no not for an hour, that the truth of the gospel might continue with you.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	6	But of them who seemed to be some thing, (what they were some time, it is nothing to me, God accepteth not the person of man,) for to me they that seemed to be some thing added nothing.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	7	But contrariwise, when they had seen that to me was committed the gospel of the uncircumcision, as to Peter was that of the circumcision.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	8	(For he who wrought in Peter to the apostleship of the circumcision, wrought in me also among the Gentiles.)
Galatians	Gal	55	2	9	And when they had known the grace that was given to me, James and Cephas and John, who seemed to be pillars, gave to me and Barnabas the right hands of fellowship: that we should go unto the Gentiles, and they unto the circumcision:
Galatians	Gal	55	2	10	Only that we should be mindful of the poor: which same thing also I was careful to do.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	11	But when Cephas was come to Antioch, I withstood him to the face, because he was to be blamed.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	12	For before that some came from James, he did eat with the Gentiles: but when they were come, he withdrew and separated himself, fearing them who were of the circumcision.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	13	And to his dissimulation the rest of the Jews consented, so that Barnabas also was led by them into that dissimulation.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	14	But when I saw that they walked not uprightly unto the truth of the gospel, I said to Cephas before them all: If thou, being a Jew, livest after the manner of the Gentiles, and not as the Jews do, how dost thou compel the Gentiles to live as do the Jews?
Galatians	Gal	55	2	15	We by nature are Jews, and not of the Gentiles sinners.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	16	But knowing that man is not justified by the works of the law, but by the faith of Jesus Christ; we also believe in Christ Jesus, that we may be justified by the faith of Christ, and not by the works of the law: because by the works of the law no flesh shall be justified.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	17	But if while we seek to be justified in Christ, we ourselves also are found sinners; is Christ then the minister of sin? God forbid.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	18	For if I build up again the things which I have destroyed, I make myself a prevaricator.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	19	For I, through the law, am dead to the law, that I may live to God: with Christ I am nailed to the cross.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	20	And I live, now not I; but Christ liveth in me. And that I live now in the flesh: I live in the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and delivered himself for me.
Galatians	Gal	55	2	21	I cast not away the grace of God. For if justice be by the law, then Christ died in vain.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	1	O senseless Galatians, who hath bewitched you that you should not obey the truth, before whose eyes Jesus Christ hath been set forth, crucified among you?
Galatians	Gal	55	3	2	This only would I learn of you: Did you receive the Spirit by the works of the law, or by the hearing of faith?
Galatians	Gal	55	3	3	Are you so foolish, that, whereas you began in the Spirit, you would now be made perfect by the flesh?
Galatians	Gal	55	3	4	Have you suffered so great things in vain? If it be yet in vain.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	5	He therefore who giveth to you the Spirit, and worketh miracles among you; doth he do it by the works of the law, or by the hearing of the faith?
Galatians	Gal	55	3	6	As it is written: Abraham believed God, and it was reputed to him unto justice.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	7	Know ye therefore, that they who are of faith, the same are the children of Abraham.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	8	And the scripture, foreseeing, that God justifieth the Gentiles by faith, told unto Abraham before: In thee shall all nations be blessed.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	9	Therefore they that are of faith, shall be blessed with faithful Abraham.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	10	For as many as are of the works of the law, are under a curse. For it is written: Cursed is every one, that abideth not in all things, which are written in the book of the law to do them.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	11	But that in the law no man is justified with God, it is manifest: because the just man liveth by faith.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	12	But the law is not of faith: but, He that doth those things, shall live in them.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	13	Christ hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us: for it is written: Cursed is every one that hangeth on a tree:
Galatians	Gal	55	3	14	That the blessing of Abraham might come on the Gentiles through Christ Jesus: that we may receive the promise of the Spirit by faith.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	15	Brethren (I speak after the manner of man,) yet a man's testament, if it be confirmed, no man despiseth, nor addeth to it.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	16	To Abraham were the promises made and to his seed. He saith not, And to his seeds, as of many: but as of one, And to thy seed, which is Christ.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	17	Now this I say, that the testament which was confirmed by God, the law which was made after four hundred and thirty years, doth not disannul, to make the promise of no effect.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	18	For if the inheritance be of the law, it is no more of promise. But God gave it to Abraham by promise.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	19	Why then was the law? It was set because of transgressions, until the seed should come, to whom he made the promise, being ordained by angels in the hand of a mediator.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	20	Now a mediator is not of one: but God is one.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	21	Was the law then against the promises of God? God forbid. For if there had been a law given which could give life, verily justice should have been by the law.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	22	But the scripture hath concluded all under sin, that the promise, by the faith of Jesus Christ, might be given to them that believe.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	23	But before the faith came, we were kept under the law shut up, unto that faith which was to be revealed.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	24	Wherefore the law was our pedagogue in Christ, that we might be justified by faith.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	25	But after the faith is come, we are no longer under a pedagogue.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	26	For you are all the children of God by faith, in Christ Jesus.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	27	For as many of you as have been baptized in Christ, have put on Christ.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	28	There is neither Jew nor Greek: there is neither bond nor free: there is neither male nor female. For you are all one in Christ Jesus.
Galatians	Gal	55	3	29	And if you be Christ's, then are you the seed of Abraham, heirs according to the promise.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	1	Now I say, as long as the heir is a child, he differeth nothing from a servant, though he be lord of all;
Galatians	Gal	55	4	2	But is under tutors and governors until the time appointed by the father:
Galatians	Gal	55	4	3	So we also, when we were children, were serving under the elements of the world.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	4	But when the fulness of the time was come, God sent his Son, made of a woman, made under the law:
Galatians	Gal	55	4	5	That he might redeem them who were under the law: that we might receive the adoption of sons.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	6	And because you are sons, God hath sent the Spirit of his Son into your hearts, crying: Abba, Father.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	7	Therefore now he is not a servant, but a son. And if a son, an heir also through God.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	8	But then indeed, not knowing God, you served them, who, by nature, are not gods.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	9	But now, after that you have known God, or rather are known by God: how turn you again to the weak and needy elements, which you desire to serve again?
Galatians	Gal	55	4	10	You observe days, and months, and times, and years.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	11	I am afraid of you, lest perhaps I have laboured in vain among you.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	12	Be ye as I, because I also am as you: brethren, I beseech you: you have not injured me at all.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	13	And you know, how through infirmity of the flesh, I preached the gospel to you heretofore: and your temptation in my flesh,
Galatians	Gal	55	4	14	You despised not, nor rejected: but received me as an angel of God, even as Christ Jesus.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	15	Where is then your blessedness? For I bear you witness, that, if it could be done, you would have plucked out your own eyes, and would have given them to me.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	16	Am I then become your enemy, because I tell you the truth?
Galatians	Gal	55	4	17	They are zealous in your regard not well: but they would exclude you, that you might be zealous for them.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	18	But be zealous for that which is good in a good thing always: and not only when I am present with you.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	19	My little children, of whom I am in labour again, until Christ be formed in you.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	20	And I would willingly be present with you now, and change my voice: because I am ashamed for you.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	21	Tell me, you that desire to be under the law, have you not read the law?
Galatians	Gal	55	4	22	For it is written that Abraham had two sons: the one by a bondwoman, and the other by a free woman.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	23	But he who was of the bondwoman, was born according to the flesh: but he of the free woman, was by promise.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	24	Which things are said by an allegory. For these are the two testaments. The one from mount Sina, engendering unto bondage; which is Agar:
Galatians	Gal	55	4	25	For Sina is a mountain in Arabia, which hath affinity to that Jerusalem which now is, and is in bondage with her children.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	26	But that Jerusalem, which is above, is free: which is our mother.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	27	For it is written: Rejoice, thou barren, that bearest not: break forth and cry, thou that travailest not: for many are the children of the desolate, more than of her that hath a husband.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	28	Now we, brethren, as Isaac was, are the children of promise.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	29	But as then he, that was born according to the flesh, persecuted him that was after the spirit; so also it is now.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	30	But what saith the scripture? Cast out the bondwoman and her son; for the son of the bondwoman shall not be heir with the son of the free woman.
Galatians	Gal	55	4	31	So then, brethren, we are not the children of the bondwoman, but of the free: by the freedom wherewith Christ has made us free.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	1	Stand fast, and be not held again under the yoke of bondage.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	2	Behold, I Paul tell you, that if you be circumcised, Christ shall profit you nothing.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	3	And I testify again to every man circumcising himself, that he is a debtor to the whole law.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	4	You are made void of Christ, you who are justified in the law: you are fallen from grace.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	5	For we in spirit, by faith, wait for the hope of justice.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	6	For in Christ Jesus neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision: but faith that worketh by charity.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	7	You did run well, who hath hindered you, that you should not obey the truth?
Galatians	Gal	55	5	8	This persuasion is not from him that calleth you.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	9	A little leaven corrupteth the whole lump.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	10	I have confidence in you in the Lord: that you will not be of another mind: but he that troubleth you, shall bear the judgment, whosoever he be.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	11	And I, brethren, if I yet preach circumcision, why do I yet suffer persecution? Then is the scandal of the cross made void.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	12	I would they were even cut off, who trouble you.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	13	For you, brethren, have been called unto liberty: only make not liberty an occasion to the flesh, but by charity of the spirit serve one another.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	14	For all the law is fulfilled in one word: Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	15	But if you bite and devour one another; take heed you be not consumed one of another.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	16	I say then, walk in the spirit, and you shall not fulfill the lusts of the flesh.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	17	For the flesh lusteth against the spirit: and the spirit against the flesh; for these are contrary one to another: so that you do not the things that you would.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	18	But if you are led by the spirit, you are not under the law.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	19	Now the works of the flesh are manifest, which are fornication, uncleanness, immodesty, luxury,
Galatians	Gal	55	5	20	Idolatry, witchcrafts, enmities, contentions, emulations, wraths, quarrels, dissensions, sects,
Galatians	Gal	55	5	21	Envies, murders, drunkenness, revellings, and such like. Of the which I foretell you, as I have foretold to you, that they who do such things shall not obtain the kingdom of God.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	22	But the fruit of the Spirit is, charity, joy, peace, patience, benignity, goodness, longanimity,
Galatians	Gal	55	5	23	Mildness, faith, modesty, continency, chastity. Against such there is no law.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	24	And they that are Christ's, have crucified their flesh, with the vices and concupiscences.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	25	If we live in the Spirit, let us also walk in the Spirit.
Galatians	Gal	55	5	26	Let us not be made desirous of vain glory, provoking one another, envying one another.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	1	Brethren, and if a man be overtaken in any fault, you, who are spiritual, instruct such a one in the spirit of meekness, considering thyself, lest thou also be tempted.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	2	Bear ye one another's burdens; and so you shall fulfill the law of Christ.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	3	For if any man think himself to be some thing, whereas he is nothing, he deceiveth himself.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	4	But let every one prove his own work, and so he shall have glory in himself only, and not in another.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	5	For every one shall bear his own burden.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	6	And let him that is instructed in the word, communicate to him that instructeth him, in all good things.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	7	Be not deceived, God is not mocked.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	8	For what things a man shall sow, those also shall he reap. For he that soweth in his flesh, of the flesh also shall reap corruption. But he that soweth in the spirit, of the spirit shall reap life everlasting.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	9	And in doing good, let us not fail. For in due time we shall reap, not failing.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	10	Therefore, whilst we have time, let us work good to all men, but especially to those who are of the household of the faith.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	11	See what a letter I have written to you with my own hand.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	12	For as many as desire to please in the flesh, they constrain you to be circumcised, only that they may not suffer the persecution of the cross of Christ.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	13	For neither they themselves who are circumcised, keep the law; but they will have you to be circumcised, that they may glory in your flesh.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	14	But God forbid that I should glory, save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ; by whom the world is crucified to me, and I to the world.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	15	For in Christ Jesus neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision, but a new creature.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	16	And whosoever shall follow this rule, peace on them, and mercy, and upon the Israel of God.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	17	From henceforth let no man be troublesome to me; for I bear the marks of the Lord Jesus in my body.
Galatians	Gal	55	6	18	The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit, brethren. Amen.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	1	Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, by the will of God, to all the saints who are at Ephesus, and to the faithful in Christ Jesus.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	2	Grace be to you, and peace from God the Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	3	Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who hath blessed us with spiritual blessings in heavenly places, in Christ:
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	4	As he chose us in him before the foundation of the world, that we should be holy and unspotted in his sight in charity.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	5	Who hath predestinated us unto the adoption of children through Jesus Christ unto himself: according to the purpose of his will:
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	6	Unto the praise of the glory of his grace, in which he hath graced us in his beloved son.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	7	In whom we have redemption through his blood, the remission of sins, according to the riches of his grace,
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	8	Which hath superabounded in us in all wisdom and prudence,
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	9	That he might make known unto us the mystery of his will, according to his good pleasure, which he hath purposed in him,
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	10	In the dispensation of the fulness of times, to re-establish all things in Christ, that are in heaven and on earth, in him.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	11	In whom we also are called by lot, being predestinated according to the purpose of him who worketh all things according to the counsel of his will.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	12	That we may be unto the praise of his glory, we who before hoped in Christ:
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	13	In whom you also, after you had heard the word of truth, (the gospel of your salvation;) in whom also believing, you were signed with the holy Spirit of promise,
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	14	Who is the pledge of our inheritance, unto the redemption of acquisition, unto the praise of his glory.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	15	Wherefore I also, hearing of your faith that is in the Lord Jesus, and of your love towards all the saints,
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	16	Cease not to give thanks for you, making commemoration of you in my prayers,
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	17	That the God of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of glory, may give unto you the spirit of wisdom and of revelation, in the knowledge of him:
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	18	The eyes of your heart enlightened, that you may know what the hope is of the glory of his inheritance in the saints.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	19	And what is the exceeding greatness of his power towards us, who believe according to the operation of the might of his power,
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	20	Which he wrought in Christ, raising him up from the dead, and setting him on his right hand in the heavenly places.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	21	Above all principality, and power, and virtue, and dominion, and every name that is named, not only in this world, but also in that which is to come.
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	22	And he hath subjected all things under his feet, and hath made him head over all the church,
Ephesians	Eph	56	1	23	Which is his body, and the fulness of him who is filled all in all.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	1	And you, when you were dead in your offences, and sins,
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	2	Wherein in time past you walked according to the course of this world, according to the prince of the power of this air, of the spirit that now worketh on the children of unbelief:
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	3	In which also we all conversed in time past, in the desires of our flesh, fulfilling the will of the flesh and of our thoughts, and were by nature children of wrath, even as the rest:
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	4	But God, (who is rich in mercy,) for his exceeding charity wherewith he loved us,
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	5	Even when we were dead in sins, hath quickened us together in Christ, (by whose grace you are saved,)
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	6	And hath raised us up together, and hath made us sit together in the heavenly places, through Christ Jesus.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	7	That he might shew in the ages to come the abundant riches of his grace, in his bounty towards us in Christ Jesus.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	8	For by grace you are saved through faith, and that not of yourselves, for it is the gift of God;
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	9	Not of works, that no man may glory.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	10	For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus in good works, which God hath prepared that we should walk in them.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	11	For which cause be mindful that you, being heretofore Gentiles in the flesh, who are called uncircumcision by that which is called circumcision in the flesh, made by hands;
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	12	That you were at that time without Christ, being aliens from the conversation of Israel, and strangers to the testament, having no hope of the promise, and without God in this world.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	13	But now in Christ Jesus, you, who some time were afar off, are made nigh by the blood of Christ.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	14	For he is our peace, who hath made both one, and breaking down the middle wall of partition, the enmities in his flesh:
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	15	Making void the law of commandments contained in decrees; that he might make the two in himself into one new man, making peace;
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	16	And might reconcile both to God in one body by the cross, killing the enmities in himself.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	17	And coming, he preached peace to you that were afar off, and peace to them that were nigh.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	18	For by him we have access both in one Spirit to the Father.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	19	Now therefore you are no more strangers and foreigners; but you are fellow citizens with the saints, and the domestics of God,
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	20	Built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone:
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	21	In whom all the building, being framed together, groweth up into an holy temple in the Lord.
Ephesians	Eph	56	2	22	In whom you also are built together into an habitation of God in the Spirit.
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	1	For this cause, I Paul, the prisoner of Jesus Christ, for you Gentiles;
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	2	If yet you have heard of the dispensation of the grace of God which is given me towards you:
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	3	How that, according to revelation, the mystery has been made known to me, as I have written above in a few words;
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	4	As you reading, may understand my knowledge in the mystery of Christ,
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	5	Which in other generations was not known to the sons of men, as it is now revealed to his holy apostles and prophets in the Spirit:
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	6	That the Gentiles should be fellow heirs, and of the same body, and co-partners of his promise in Christ Jesus, by the gospel:
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	7	Of which I am made a minister, according to the gift of the grace of God, which is given to me according to the operation of his power:
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	8	To me, the least of all the saints, is given this grace, to preach among the Gentiles, the unsearchable riches of Christ,
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	9	And to enlighten all men, that they may see what is the dispensation of the mystery which hath been hidden from eternity in God, who created all things:
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	10	That the manifold wisdom of God may be made known to the principalities and powers in heavenly places through the church,
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	11	According to the eternal purpose, which he made, in Christ Jesus our Lord:
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	12	In whom we have boldness and access with confidence by the faith of him.
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	13	Wherefore I pray you not to faint at my tribulations for you, which is your glory.
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	14	For this cause I bow my knees to the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ,
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	15	Of whom all paternity in heaven and earth is named,
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	16	That he would grant you, according to the riches of his glory, to be strengthened by his Spirit with might unto the inward man,
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	17	That Christ may dwell by faith in your hearts; that being rooted and founded in charity,
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	18	You may be able to comprehend, with all the saints, what is the breadth, and length, and height, and depth:
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	19	To know also the charity of Christ, which surpasseth all knowledge, that you may be filled unto all the fulness of God.
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	20	Now to him who is able to do all things more abundantly than we desire or understand, according to the power that worketh in us;
Ephesians	Eph	56	3	21	To him be glory in the church, and in Christ Jesus unto all generations, world without end. Amen.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	1	I therefore, a prisoner in the Lord, beseech you that you walk worthy of the vocation in which you are called,
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	2	With all humility and mildness, with patience, supporting one another in charity.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	3	Careful to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	4	One body and one Spirit; as you are called in one hope of your calling.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	5	One Lord, one faith, one baptism.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	6	One God and Father of all, who is above all, and through all, and in us all.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	7	But to every one of us is given grace, according to the measure of the giving of Christ.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	8	Wherefore he saith: Ascending on high, he led captivity captive; he gave gifts to men.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	9	Now that he ascended, what is it, but because he also descended first into the lower parts of the earth?
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	10	He that descended is the same also that ascended above all the heavens, that he might fill all things.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	11	And he gave some apostles, and some prophets, and other some evangelists, and other some pastors and doctors,
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	12	For the perfecting of the saints, for the work of the ministry, for the edifying of the body of Christ:
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	13	Until we all meet into the unity of faith, and of the knowledge of the Son of God, unto a perfect man, unto the measure of the age of the fulness of Christ;
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	14	That henceforth we be no more children tossed to and fro, and carried about with every wind of doctrine by the wickedness of men, by cunning craftiness, by which they lie in wait to deceive.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	15	But doing the truth in charity, we may in all things grow up in him who is the head, even Christ:
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	16	From whom the whole body, being compacted and fitly joined together, by what every joint supplieth, according to the operation in the measure of every part, maketh increase of the body, unto the edifying of itself in charity.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	17	This then I say and testify in the Lord: That henceforward you walk not as also the Gentiles walk in the vanity of their mind,
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	18	Having their understanding darkened, being alienated from the life of God through the ignorance that is in them, because of the blindness of their hearts.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	19	Who despairing, have given themselves up to lasciviousness, unto the working of all uncleanness, unto covetousness.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	20	But you have not so learned Christ;
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	21	If so be that you have heard him, and have been taught in him, as the truth is in Jesus:
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	22	To put off, according to former conversation, the old man, who is corrupted according to the desire of error.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	23	And be renewed in the spirit of your mind:
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	24	And put on the new man, who according to God is created in justice and holiness of truth.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	25	Wherefore putting away lying, speak ye the truth every man with his neighbour; for we are members one of another.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	26	Be angry, and sin not. Let not the sun go down upon your anger.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	27	Give not place to the devil.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	28	He that stole, let him now steal no more; but rather let him labour, working with his hands the thing which is good, that he may have something to give to him that suffereth need.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	29	Let no evil speech proceed from your mouth; but that which is good, to the edification of faith, that it may administer grace to the hearers.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	30	And grieve not the holy Spirit of God: whereby you are sealed unto the day of redemption.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	31	Let all bitterness, and anger, and indignation, and clamour, and blasphemy, be put away from you, with all malice.
Ephesians	Eph	56	4	32	And be ye kind one to another; merciful, forgiving one another, even as God hath forgiven you in Christ.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	1	Be ye therefore followers of God, as most dear children;
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	2	And walk in love, as Christ also hath loved us, and hath delivered himself for us, an oblation and a sacrifice to God for an odour of sweetness.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	3	But fornication, and all uncleanness, or covetousness, let it not so much as be named among you, as becometh saints:
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	4	Or obscenity, or foolish talking, or scurrility, which is to no purpose; but rather giving of thanks.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	5	For know you this and understand, that no fornicator, or unclean, or covetous person (which is a serving of idols), hath inheritance in the kingdom of Christ and of God.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	6	Let no man deceive you with vain words. For because of these things cometh the anger of God upon the children of unbelief.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	7	Be ye not therefore partakers with them.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	8	For you were heretofore darkness, but now light in the Lord. Walk then as children of the light.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	9	For the fruit of the light is in all goodness, and justice, and truth;
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	10	Proving what is well pleasing to God:
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	11	And have no fellowship with the unfruitful works of darkness, but rather reprove them.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	12	For the things that are done by them in secret, it is a shame even to speak of.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	13	But all things that are reproved, are made manifest by the light; for all that is made manifest is light.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	14	Wherefore he saith: Rise thou that sleepest, and arise from the dead: and Christ shall enlighten thee.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	15	See therefore, brethren, how you walk circumspectly: not as unwise,
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	16	But as wise: redeeming the time, because the days are evil.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	17	Wherefore become not unwise, but understanding what is the will of God.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	18	And be not drunk with wine, wherein is luxury; but be ye filled with the holy Spirit,
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	19	Speaking to yourselves in psalms, and hymns, and spiritual canticles, singing and making melody in your hearts to the Lord;
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	20	Giving thanks always for all things, in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, to God and the Father:
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	21	Being subject one to another, in the fear of Christ.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	22	Let women be subject to their husbands, as to the Lord:
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	23	Because the husband is the head of the wife, as Christ is the head of the church. He is the saviour of his body.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	24	Therefore as the church is subject to Christ, so also let the wives be to their husbands in all things.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	25	Husbands, love your wives, as Christ also loved the church, and delivered himself up for it:
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	26	That he might sanctify it, cleansing it by the laver of water in the word of life:
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	27	That he might present it to himself a glorious church, not having spot or wrinkle, or any such thing; but that it should be holy, and without blemish.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	28	So also ought men to love their wives as their own bodies. He that loveth his wife, loveth himself.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	29	For no man ever hated his own flesh; but nourisheth and cherisheth it, as also Christ doth the church:
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	30	Because we are members of his body, of his flesh, and of his bones.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	31	For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother, and shall cleave to his wife, and they shall be two in one flesh.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	32	This is a great sacrament; but I speak in Christ and in the church.
Ephesians	Eph	56	5	33	Nevertheless let every one of you in particular love his wife as himself: and let the wife fear her husband.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	1	Children, obey your parents in the Lord, for this is just.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	2	Honour thy father and thy mother, which is the first commandment with a promise:
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	3	That it may be well with thee, and thou mayest be long lived upon earth.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	4	And you, fathers, provoke not your children to anger; but bring them up in the discipline and correction of the Lord.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	5	Servants, be obedient to them that are your lords according to the flesh, with fear and trembling, in the simplicity of your heart, as to Christ:
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	6	Not serving to the eye, as it were pleasing men, but, as the servants of Christ doing the will of God from the heart,
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	7	With a good will serving, as to the Lord, and not to men.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	8	Knowing that whatsoever good thing any man shall do, the same shall he receive from the Lord, whether he be bond, or free.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	9	And you, masters, do the same things to them, forbearing threatenings, knowing that the Lord both of them and you is in heaven; and there is no respect of persons with him.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	10	Finally, brethren, be strengthened in the Lord, and in the might of his power.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	11	Put you on the armour of God, that you may be able to stand against the deceits of the devil.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	12	For our wrestling is not against flesh and blood; but against principalities and powers, against the rulers of the world of this darkness, against the spirits of wickedness in the high places.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	13	Therefore take unto you the armour of God, that you may be able to resist in the evil day, and to stand in all things perfect.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	14	Stand therefore, having your loins girt about with truth, and having on the breastplate of justice,
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	15	And your feet shod with the preparation of the gospel of peace:
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	16	In all things taking the shield of faith, wherewith you may be able to extinguish all the fiery darts of the most wicked one.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	17	And take unto you the helmet of salvation, and the sword of the Spirit (which is the word of God).
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	18	By all prayer and supplication praying at all times in the spirit; and in the same watching with all instance and supplication for all the saints:
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	19	And for me, that speech may be given me, that I may open my mouth with confidence, to make known the mystery of the gospel.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	20	For which I am an ambassador in a chain, so that therein I may be bold to speak according as I ought.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	21	But that you also may know the things that concern me, and what I am doing, Tychicus, my dearest brother and faithful minister in the Lord, will make known to you all things:
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	22	Whom I have sent to you for this same purpose, that you may know the things concerning us, and that he may comfort your hearts.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	23	Peace be to the brethren and charity with faith, from God the Father, and the Lord Jesus Christ.
Ephesians	Eph	56	6	24	Grace be with all them that love our Lord Jesus Christ in incorruption. Amen.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	1	Paul and Timothy, the servants of Jesus Christ; to all the saints in Christ Jesus, who are at Philippi, with the bishops and deacons.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	2	Grace be unto you, and peace from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	3	I give thanks to my God in every remembrance of you,
Philippians	Phil	57	1	4	Always in all my prayers making supplication for you all, with joy;
Philippians	Phil	57	1	5	For your communication in the gospel of Christ from the first day until now.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	6	Being confident of this very thing, that he, who hath begun a good work in you, will perfect it unto the day of Christ Jesus.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	7	As it is meet for me to think this for you all, for that I have you in my heart; and that in my bands, and in the defence and confirmation of the gospel, you all are partakers of my joy.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	8	For God is my witness, how I long after you all in the bowels of Jesus Christ.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	9	And this I pray, that your charity may more and more abound in knowledge, and in all understanding:
Philippians	Phil	57	1	10	That you may approve the better things, that you may be sincere and without offence unto the day of Christ,
Philippians	Phil	57	1	11	Filled with the fruit of justice, through Jesus Christ, unto the glory and praise of God.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	12	Now, brethren, I desire you should know, that the things which have happened to me, have fallen out rather to the furtherance of the gospel:
Philippians	Phil	57	1	13	So that my bands are made manifest in Christ, in all the court, and in all other places;
Philippians	Phil	57	1	14	And many of the brethren in the Lord, growing confident by my bands, are much more bold to speak the word of God without fear.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	15	Some indeed, even out of envy and contention; but some also for good will preach Christ.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	16	Some out of charity, knowing that I am set for the defence of the gospel.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	17	And some out of contention preach Christ not sincerely: supposing that they raise affliction to my bands.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	18	But what then? So that by all means, whether by occasion, or by truth, Christ be preached: in this also I rejoice, yea, and will rejoice.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	19	For I know that this shall fall out to me unto salvation, through your prayer, and the supply of the Spirit of Jesus Christ,
Philippians	Phil	57	1	20	According to my expectation and hope; that in nothing I shall be confounded, but with all confidence, as always, so now also shall Christ be magnified in my body, wither it be by life, or by death.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	21	For to me, to live is Christ; and to die is gain.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	22	And if to live in the flesh, this is to me the fruit of labour, and what I shall choose I know not.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	23	But I am straitened between two: having a desire to be dissolved and to be with Christ, a thing by far the better.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	24	But to abide still in the flesh, is needful for you.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	25	And having this confidence, I know that I shall abide, and continue with you all, for your furtherance and joy of faith:
Philippians	Phil	57	1	26	That your rejoicing may abound in Christ Jesus for me, by my coming to you again.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	27	Only let your conversation be worthy of the gospel of Christ: that, whether I come and see you, or, being absent, may hear of you, that you stand fast in one spirit, with one mind labouring together for the faith of the gospel.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	28	And in nothing be ye terrified by the adversaries: which to them is a cause of perdition, but to you of salvation, and this from God:
Philippians	Phil	57	1	29	For unto you it is given for Christ, not only to believe in him, but also to suffer for him.
Philippians	Phil	57	1	30	Having the same conflict as that which you have seen in me, and now have heard of me.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	1	If there be therefore any consolation in Christ, if any comfort of charity, if any society of the spirit, if any bowels of commiseration:
Philippians	Phil	57	2	2	Fulfill ye my joy, that you may be of one mind, having the same charity, being of one accord, agreeing in sentiment.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	3	Let nothing be done through contention, neither by vain glory: but in humility, let each esteem others better than themselves:
Philippians	Phil	57	2	4	Each one not considering the things that are his own, but those that are other men's.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	5	For let this mind be in you, which was also in Christ Jesus:
Philippians	Phil	57	2	6	Who being in the form of God, thought it not robbery to be equal with God:
Philippians	Phil	57	2	7	But emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, being made in the likeness of men, and in habit found as a man.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	8	He humbled himself, becoming obedient unto death, even to the death of the cross.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	9	For which cause God also hath exalted him, and hath given him a name which is above all names:
Philippians	Phil	57	2	10	That in the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of those that are in heaven, on earth, and under the earth:
Philippians	Phil	57	2	11	And that every tongue should confess that the Lord Jesus Christ is in the glory of God the Father.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	12	Wherefore, my dearly beloved, (as you have always obeyed, not as in my presence only, but much more now in my absence,) with fear and trembling work out your salvation.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	13	For it is God who worketh in you, both to will and to accomplish, according to his good will.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	14	And do ye all things without murmurings and hesitations;
Philippians	Phil	57	2	15	That you may be blameless, and sincere children of God, without reproof, in the midst of a crooked and perverse generation; among whom you shine as lights in the world.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	16	Holding forth the word of life to my glory in the day of Christ, because I have not run in vain, nor laboured in vain.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	17	Yea, and if I be made a victim upon the sacrifice and service of your faith, I rejoice, and congratulate with you all.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	18	And for the selfsame thing do you also rejoice, and congratulate with me.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	19	And I hope in the Lord Jesus to send Timothy unto you shortly, that I also may be of good comfort, when I know the things concerning you.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	20	For I have no man so of the same mind, who with sincere affection is solicitous for you.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	21	For all seek the things that are their own; not the things that are Jesus Christ's.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	22	Now know ye the proof of him, that as a son with the father, so hath he served with me in the gospel.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	23	Him therefore I hope to send unto you immediately, so soon as I shall see how it will go with me.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	24	And I trust in the Lord, that I myself also shall come to you shortly.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	25	But I have thought it necessary to send to you Epaphroditus, my brother and fellow labourer, and fellow soldier, but your apostle, and he that hath ministered to my wants.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	26	For indeed he longed after you all: and was sad, for that you had heard that he was sick.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	27	For indeed he was sick, nigh unto death; but God had mercy on him; and not only on him, but on me also, lest I should have sorrow upon sorrow.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	28	Therefore I sent him the more speedily: that seeing him again, you may rejoice, and I may be without sorrow.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	29	Receive him therefore with all joy in the Lord; and treat with honour such as he is.
Philippians	Phil	57	2	30	Because for the work of Christ he came to the point of death: delivering his life, that he might fulfill that which on your part was wanting towards my service.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	1	As to the rest, my brethren, rejoice in the Lord. To write the same things to you, to me indeed is not wearisome, but to you it is necessary.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	2	Beware of dogs, beware of evil workers, beware of the concision.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	3	For we are the circumcision, who in spirit serve God; and glory in Christ Jesus, not having confidence in the flesh.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	4	Though I might also have confidence in the flesh. If any other thinketh he may have confidence in the flesh, I more,
Philippians	Phil	57	3	5	Being circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, an Hebrew of the Hebrews; according to the law, a Pharisee:
Philippians	Phil	57	3	6	According to zeal, persecuting the church of God; according to the justice that is in the law, conversing without blame.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	7	But the things that were gain to me, the same I have counted loss for Christ.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	8	Furthermore I count all things to be but loss for the excellent knowledge of Jesus Christ my Lord; for whom I have suffered the loss of all things, and count them but as dung, that I may gain Christ:
Philippians	Phil	57	3	9	And may be found in him, not having my justice, which is of the law, but that which is of the faith of Christ Jesus, which is of God, justice in faith:
Philippians	Phil	57	3	10	That I may know him, and the power of his resurrection, and the fellowship of his sufferings, being made conformable to his death,
Philippians	Phil	57	3	11	If by any means I may attain to the resurrection which is from the dead.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	12	Not as though I had already attained, or were already perfect; but I follow after, if I may by any means apprehend, wherein I am also apprehended by Christ Jesus.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	13	Brethren, I do not count myself to have apprehended. But one thing I do: forgetting the things that are behind, and stretching forth myself to those that are before,
Philippians	Phil	57	3	14	I press towards the mark, to the prize of the supernal vocation of God in Christ Jesus.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	15	Let us therefore, as many as are perfect, be thus minded; and if in any thing you be otherwise minded, this also God will reveal to you.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	16	Nevertheless whereunto we are come, that we be of the same mind, let us also continue in the same rule.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	17	Be ye followers of me, brethren, and observe them who walk so as you have our model.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	18	For many walk, of whom I have told you often (and now tell you weeping), that they are enemies of the cross of Christ;
Philippians	Phil	57	3	19	Whose end is destruction; whose God is their belly; and whose glory is in their shame; who mind earthly things.
Philippians	Phil	57	3	20	But our conversation is in heaven; from whence also we look for the Saviour, our Lord Jesus Christ,
Philippians	Phil	57	3	21	Who will reform the body of our lowness, made like to the body of his glory, according to the operation whereby also he is able to subdue all things unto himself.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	1	Therefore, my dearly beloved brethren, and most desired, my joy and my crown; so stand fast in the Lord, my dearly beloved.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	2	I beg of Evodia, and I beseech Syntyche, to be of one mind in the Lord.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	3	And I entreat thee also, my sincere companion, help those women who have laboured with me in the gospel, with Clement and the rest of my fellow labourers, whose names are in the book of life.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	4	Rejoice in the Lord always; again, I say, rejoice.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	5	Let your modesty be known to all men. The Lord is nigh.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	6	Be nothing solicitous; but in every thing, by prayer and supplication, with thanksgiving, let your petitions be made known to God.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	7	And the peace of God, which surpasseth all understanding, keep your hearts and minds in Christ Jesus.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	8	For the rest, brethren, whatsoever things are true, whatsoever modest, whatsoever just, whatsoever holy, whatsoever lovely, whatsoever of good fame, if there be any virtue, if any praise of discipline, think on these things.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	9	The things which you have both learned, and received, and heard, and seen in me, these do ye, and the God of peace shall be with you.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	10	Now I rejoice in the Lord exceedingly, that now at length your thought for me hath flourished again, as you did also think; but you were busied.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	11	I speak not as it were for want. For I have learned, in whatsoever state I am, to be content therewith.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	12	I know both how to be brought low, and I know how to abound: (everywhere, and in all things I am instructed) both to be full, and to be hungry; both to abound, and to suffer need.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	13	I can do all these things in him who strengtheneth me.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	14	Nevertheless you have done well in communicating to my tribulation.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	15	And you also know, O Philippians, that in the beginning of the gospel, when I departed from Macedonia, no church communicated with me as concerning giving and receiving, but you only:
Philippians	Phil	57	4	16	For unto Thessalonica also you sent once and again for my use.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	17	Not that I seek the gift, but I seek the fruit that may abound to your account.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	18	But I have all, and abound: I am filled, having received from Epaphroditus the things you sent, an odour of sweetness, an acceptable sacrifice, pleasing to God.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	19	And may my God supply all your want, according to his riches in glory in Christ Jesus.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	20	Now to God and our Father be glory world without end. Amen.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	21	Salute ye every saint in Christ Jesus.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	22	The brethren who are with me, salute you. All the saints salute you; especially they that are of Caesar's household.
Philippians	Phil	57	4	23	The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit. Amen.
Colossians	Col	58	1	1	Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, by the will of God, and Timothy, a brother,
Colossians	Col	58	1	2	To the saints and faithful brethren in Christ Jesus, who are at Colossa.
Colossians	Col	58	1	3	Grace be to you and peace from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ. We give thanks to God, and the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, praying always for you.
Colossians	Col	58	1	4	Hearing your faith in Christ Jesus, and the love which you have towards all the saints.
Colossians	Col	58	1	5	For the hope that is laid up for you in heaven, which you have heard in the word of the truth of the gospel,
Colossians	Col	58	1	6	Which is come unto you, as also it is in the whole world, and bringeth forth fruit and groweth, even as it doth in you, since the day you heard and knew the grace of God in truth.
Colossians	Col	58	1	7	As you learned of Epaphras, our most beloved fellow servant, who is for you a faithful minister of Christ Jesus;
Colossians	Col	58	1	8	Who also hath manifested to us your love in the spirit.
Colossians	Col	58	1	9	Therefore we also, from the day that we heard it, cease not to pray for you, and to beg that you may be filled with the knowledge of his will, in all wisdom, and spiritual understanding:
Colossians	Col	58	1	10	That you may walk worthy of God, in all things pleasing; being fruitful in every good work, and increasing in the knowledge of God:
Colossians	Col	58	1	11	Strengthened with all might, according to the power of his glory, in all patience and longsuffering with joy,
Colossians	Col	58	1	12	Giving thanks to God the Father, who hath made us worthy to be partakers of the lot of the saints in light:
Colossians	Col	58	1	13	Who hath delivered us from the power of darkness, and hath translated us into the kingdom of the Son of his love,
Colossians	Col	58	1	14	In whom we have redemption through his blood, the remission of sins;
Colossians	Col	58	1	15	Who is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature:
Colossians	Col	58	1	16	For in him were all things created in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible, whether thrones, or dominations, or principalities, or powers: all things were created by him and in him.
Colossians	Col	58	1	17	And he is before all, and by him all things consist.
Colossians	Col	58	1	18	And he is the head of the body, the church, who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead; that in all things he may hold the primacy:
Colossians	Col	58	1	19	Because in him, it hath well pleased the Father, that all fulness should dwell;
Colossians	Col	58	1	20	And through him to reconcile all things unto himself, making peace through the blood of his cross, both as to the things that are on earth, and the things that are in heaven.
Colossians	Col	58	1	21	And you, whereas you were some time alienated and enemies in mind in evil works:
Colossians	Col	58	1	22	Yet now he hath reconciled in the body of his flesh through death, to present you holy and unspotted, and blameless before him:
Colossians	Col	58	1	23	If so ye continue in the faith, grounded and settled, and immoveable from the hope of the gospel which you have heard, which is preached in all the creation that is under heaven, whereof I Paul am made a minister.
Colossians	Col	58	1	24	Who now rejoice in my sufferings for you, and fill up those things that are wanting of the sufferings of Christ, in my flesh, for his body, which is the church:
Colossians	Col	58	1	25	Whereof I am made a minister according to the dispensation of God, which is given me towards you, that I may fulfill the word of God:
Colossians	Col	58	1	26	The mystery which hath been hidden from ages and generations, but now is manifested to his saints,
Colossians	Col	58	1	27	To whom God would make known the riches of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles, which is Christ, in you the hope of glory.
Colossians	Col	58	1	28	Whom we preach, admonishing every man, and teaching every man in all wisdom, that we may present every man perfect in Christ Jesus.
Colossians	Col	58	1	29	Wherein also I labour, striving according to his working which he worketh in me in power.
Colossians	Col	58	2	1	For I would have you know, what manner of care I have for you and for them that are at Laodicea, and whosoever have not seen my face in the flesh:
Colossians	Col	58	2	2	That their hearts may be comforted, being instructed in charity, and unto all riches of fulness of understanding, unto the knowledge of the mystery of God the Father and of Christ Jesus:
Colossians	Col	58	2	3	In whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge.
Colossians	Col	58	2	4	Now this I say, that no man may deceive you by loftiness of words.
Colossians	Col	58	2	5	For though I be absent in body, yet in spirit I am with you; rejoicing, and beholding your order, and the steadfastness of your faith which is in Christ.
Colossians	Col	58	2	6	As therefore you have received Jesus Christ the Lord, walk ye in him;
Colossians	Col	58	2	7	Rooted and built up in him, and confirmed in the faith, as also you have learned, abounding in him in thanksgiving.
Colossians	Col	58	2	8	Beware lest any man cheat you by philosophy, and vain deceit; according to the tradition of men, according to the elements of the world, and not according to Christ:
Colossians	Col	58	2	9	For in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead corporeally;
Colossians	Col	58	2	10	And you are filled in him, who is the head of all principality and power:
Colossians	Col	58	2	11	In whom also you are circumcised with circumcision not made by hand, in despoiling of the body of the flesh, but in the circumcision of Christ:
Colossians	Col	58	2	12	Buried with him in baptism, in whom also you are risen again by the faith of the operation of God, who hath raised him up from the dead.
Colossians	Col	58	2	13	And you, when you were dead in your sins, and the uncircumcision of your flesh; he hath quickened together with him, forgiving you all offences:
Colossians	Col	58	2	14	Blotting out the handwriting of the decree that was against us, which was contrary to us. And he hath taken the same out of the way, fastening it to the cross:
Colossians	Col	58	2	15	And despoiling the principalities and powers, he hath exposed them confidently in open shew, triumphing over them in himself.
Colossians	Col	58	2	16	Let no man therefore judge you in meat or in drink, or in respect of a festival day, or of the new moon, or of the sabbaths,
Colossians	Col	58	2	17	Which are a shadow of things to come, but the body is of Christ.
Colossians	Col	58	2	18	Let no man seduce you, willing in humility, and religion of angels, walking in the things which he hath not seen, in vain puffed up by the sense of his flesh,
Colossians	Col	58	2	19	And not holding the head, from which the whole body, by joints and bands, being supplied with nourishment and compacted, groweth unto the increase of God.
Colossians	Col	58	2	20	If then you be dead with Christ from the elements of this world, why do you yet decree as though living in the world?
Colossians	Col	58	2	21	Touch not, taste not, handle not:
Colossians	Col	58	2	22	Which all are unto destruction by the very use, according to the precepts and doctrines of men.
Colossians	Col	58	2	23	Which things have indeed a shew of wisdom in superstition and humility, and not sparing the body; not in any honour to the filling of the flesh.
Colossians	Col	58	3	1	Therefore, if you be risen with Christ, seek the things that are above; where Christ is sitting at the right hand of God:
Colossians	Col	58	3	2	Mind the things that are above, not the things that are upon the earth.
Colossians	Col	58	3	3	For you are dead; and your life is hid with Christ in God.
Colossians	Col	58	3	4	When Christ shall appear, who is your life, then you also shall appear with him in glory.
Colossians	Col	58	3	5	Mortify therefore your members which are upon the earth; fornication, uncleanness, lust, evil concupiscence, and covetousness, which is the service of idols.
Colossians	Col	58	3	6	For which things the wrath of God cometh upon the children of unbelief,
Colossians	Col	58	3	7	In which you also walked some time, when you lived in them.
Colossians	Col	58	3	8	But now put you also all away: anger, indignation, malice, blasphemy, filthy speech out of your mouth.
Colossians	Col	58	3	9	Lie not one to another: stripping yourselves of the old man with his deeds,
Colossians	Col	58	3	10	And putting on the new, him who is renewed unto knowledge, according to the image of him that created him.
Colossians	Col	58	3	11	Where there is neither Gentile nor Jew, circumcision nor uncircumcision, Barbarian nor Scythian, bond nor free. But Christ is all, and in all.
Colossians	Col	58	3	12	Put ye on therefore, as the elect of God, holy, and beloved, the bowels of mercy, benignity, humility, modesty, patience:
Colossians	Col	58	3	13	Bearing with one another, and forgiving one another, if any have a complaint against another: even as the Lord hath forgiven you, so do you also.
Colossians	Col	58	3	14	But above all these things have charity, which is the bond of perfection:
Colossians	Col	58	3	15	And let the peace of Christ rejoice in your hearts, wherein also you are called in one body: and be ye thankful.
Colossians	Col	58	3	16	Let the word of Christ dwell in you abundantly, in all wisdom: teaching and admonishing one another in psalms, hymns, and spiritual canticles, singing in grace in your hearts to God.
Colossians	Col	58	3	17	All whatsoever you do in word or in work, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ, giving thanks to God and the Father by him.
Colossians	Col	58	3	18	Wives, be subject to your husbands, as it behoveth in the Lord.
Colossians	Col	58	3	19	Husbands, love your wives, and be not bitter towards them.
Colossians	Col	58	3	20	Children, obey your parents in all things: for this is well pleasing to the Lord.
Colossians	Col	58	3	21	Fathers, provoke not your children to indignation, lest they be discouraged.
Colossians	Col	58	3	22	Servants, obey in all things your masters according to the flesh, not serving to the eye, as pleasing men, but in simplicity of heart, fearing God.
Colossians	Col	58	3	23	Whatsoever you do, do it from the heart, as to the Lord, and not to men:
Colossians	Col	58	3	24	Knowing that you shall receive of the Lord the reward of inheritance. Serve ye the Lord Christ.
Colossians	Col	58	3	25	For he that doth wrong, shall receive for that which he hath done wrongfully: and there is no respect of persons with God.
Colossians	Col	58	4	1	Masters, do to your servants that which is just and equal: knowing that you also have a master in heaven.
Colossians	Col	58	4	2	Be instant in prayer; watching in it with thanksgiving:
Colossians	Col	58	4	3	Praying withal for us also, that God may open unto us a door of speech to speak the mystery of Christ (for which also I am bound;)
Colossians	Col	58	4	4	That I may make it manifest as I ought to speak.
Colossians	Col	58	4	5	Walk with wisdom towards them that are without, redeeming the time.
Colossians	Col	58	4	6	Let your speech be always in grace seasoned with salt: that you may know how you ought to answer every man.
Colossians	Col	58	4	7	All the things that concern me, Tychicus, our dearest brother, and faithful minister and fellow servant in the Lord, will make known to you,
Colossians	Col	58	4	8	Whom I have sent to you for this same purpose, that he may know the things that concern you, and comfort your hearts,
Colossians	Col	58	4	9	With Onesimus, a most beloved and faithful brother, who is one of you. All things that are done here, they shall make known to you.
Colossians	Col	58	4	10	Aristarchus, my fellow prisoner, saluteth you, and Mark, the cousin german of Barnabus, touching whom you have received commandments; if he come unto you, receive him:
Colossians	Col	58	4	11	And Jesus, that is called Justus: who are of the circumcision: these only are my helpers in the kingdom of God; who have been a comfort to me.
Colossians	Col	58	4	12	Epaphras saluteth you, who is one of you, a servant of Christ Jesus, who is always solicitous for you in prayers, that you may stand perfect, and full in all the will of God.
Colossians	Col	58	4	13	For I bear him testimony that he hath much labour for you, and for them that are at Laodicea, and them at Hierapolis.
Colossians	Col	58	4	14	Luke, the most dear physician, saluteth you: and Demas.
Colossians	Col	58	4	15	Salute the brethren who are at Laodicea, and Nymphas, and the church that is in his house.
Colossians	Col	58	4	16	And when this epistle shall have been read with you, cause that it be read also in the church of the Laodiceans: and that you read that which is of the Laodiceans.
Colossians	Col	58	4	17	And say to Archippus: Take heed to the ministry which thou hast received in the Lord, that thou fulfill it.
Colossians	Col	58	4	18	The salutation of Paul with my own hand. Be mindful of my bands. Grace be with you. Amen.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	1	Paul and Sylvanus and Timothy: to the church of the Thessalonians, in God the Father, and in the Lord Jesus Christ.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	2	Grace be to you and peace. We give thanks to God always for you all; making a remembrance of you in our prayers without ceasing,
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	3	Being mindful of the work of your faith, and labour, and charity, and of the enduring of the hope of our Lord Jesus Christ before God and our Father:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	4	Knowing, brethren beloved of God, your election:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	5	For our gospel hath not been unto you in word only, but in power also, and in the Holy Ghost, and in much fulness, as you know what manner of men we have been among you for your sakes.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	6	And you became followers of us, and of the Lord; receiving the word in much tribulation, with joy of the Holy Ghost:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	7	So that you were made a pattern to all that believe in Macedonia and in Achaia.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	8	For from you was spread abroad the word of the Lord, not only in Macedonia, and in Achaia, but also in every place, your faith which is towards God, is gone forth, so that we need not to speak any thing.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	9	For they themselves relate of us, what manner of entering in we had unto you; and how you turned to God from idols, to serve the living and true God.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	1	10	And to wait for his Son from heaven (whom he raised up from the dead,) Jesus, who hath delivered us from the wrath to come.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	1	For yourselves know, brethren, our entrance in unto you, that it was not in vain:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	2	But having suffered many things before, and been shamefully treated (as you know) at Philippi, we had confidence in our God, to speak unto you the gospel of God in much carefulness.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	3	For our exhortation was not of error, nor of uncleanness, nor in deceit:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	4	But as we were approved by God that the gospel should be committed to us: even so we speak, not as pleasing men, but God, who proveth our hearts.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	5	For neither have we used, at any time, the speech of flattery, as you know; nor taken an occasion of covetousness, God is witness:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	6	Nor sought we glory of men, neither of you, nor of others.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	7	Whereas we might have been burdensome to you, as the apostles of Christ: but we became little ones in the midst of you, as if a nurse should cherish her children:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	8	So desirous of you, we would gladly impart unto you not only the gospel of God, but also our own souls: because you were become most dear unto us.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	9	For you remember, brethren, our labour and toil: working night and day, lest we should be chargeable to any of you, we preached among you the gospel of God.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	10	You are witnesses, and God also, how holily, and justly, and without blame, we have been to you that have believed:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	11	As you know in what manner, entreating and comforting you, (as a father doth his children,)
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	12	We testified to every one of you, that you would walk worthy of God, who hath called you unto his kingdom and glory.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	13	Therefore, we also give thanks to God without ceasing: because, that when you had received of us the word of the hearing of God, you received it not as the word of men, but (as it is indeed) the word of God, who worketh in you that have believed.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	14	For you, brethren, are become followers of the churches of God which are in Judea, in Christ Jesus: for you also have suffered the same things from your own coutrymen, even as they have from the Jews,
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	15	Who both killed the Lord Jesus, and the prophets, and have persecuted us, and please not God, and are adversaries to all men;
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	16	Prohibiting us to speak to the Gentiles, that they may be saved, to fill up their sins always: for the wrath of God is come upon them to the end.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	17	But we, brethren, being taken away from you for a short time, in sight, not in heart, have hastened the more abundantly to see your face with great desire.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	18	For we would have come unto you, I Paul indeed, once and again: but Satan hath hindered us.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	19	For what is our hope, or joy, or crown of glory? Are not you, in the presence of our Lord Jesus Christ at his coming?
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	2	20	For you are our glory and joy.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	1	For which cause, forbearing no longer, we thought it good to remain at Athens alone:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	2	And we sent Timothy, our brother, and the minister of God in the gospel of Christ, to confirm you and exhort you concerning your faith:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	3	That no man should be moved in these tribulations: for yourselves know, that we are appointed thereunto.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	4	For even when we were with you, we foretold you that we should suffer tribulations, as also it is come to pass, and you know.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	5	For this cause also, I, forbearing no longer, sent to know your faith: lest perhaps he that tempteth should have tempted you, and our labour should be made vain.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	6	But now when Timothy came to us from you, and related to us your faith and charity, and that you have a good remembrance of us always, desiring to see us as we also to see you;
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	7	Therefore we were comforted, brethren, in you, in all our necessity and tribulation, by your faith,
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	8	Because now we live, if you stand in the Lord.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	9	For what thanks can we return to God for you, in all the joy wherewith we rejoice for you before our God,
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	10	Night and day more abundantly praying that we may see your face, and may accomplish those things that are wanting to your faith?
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	11	Now God himself and our Father, and our Lord Jesus Christ, direct our way unto you.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	12	And may the Lord multiply you, and make you abound in charity towards one another, and towards all men: as we do also towards you,
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	3	13	To confirm your hearts without blame, in holiness, before God and our Father, at the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, with all his saints. Amen.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	1	For the rest therefore, brethren, we pray and beseech you in the Lord Jesus, that as you have received from us, how you ought to walk, and to please God, so also you would walk, that you may abound the more.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	2	For you know what precepts I have given to you by the Lord Jesus.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	3	For this is the will of God, your sanctification; that you should abstain from fornication;
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	4	That every one of you should know how to possess his vessel in sanctification and honour:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	5	Not in the passion of lust, like the Gentiles that know not God:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	6	And that no man overreach, nor circumvent his brother in business: because the Lord is the avenger of all these things, as we have told you before, and have testified.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	7	For God hath not called us unto uncleanness, but unto sanctification.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	8	Therefore, he that despiseth these things, despiseth not man, but God, who also hath given his holy Spirit in us.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	9	But as touching the charity of brotherhood, we have no need to write to you: for yourselves have learned of God to love one another.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	10	For indeed you do it towards all the brethren in all Macedonia. But we entreat you, brethren, that you abound more:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	11	And that you use your endeavour to be quiet, and that you do your own business, and work with your own hands, as we commanded you: and that you walk honestly towards them that are without; and that you want nothing of any man's.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	12	And we will not have you ignorant, brethren, concerning them that are asleep, that you be not sorrowful, even as others who have no hope.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	13	For if we believe that Jesus died, and rose again; even so them who have slept through Jesus, will God bring with him.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	14	For this we say unto you in the word of the Lord, that we who are alive, who remain unto the coming of the Lord, shall not prevent them who have slept.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	15	For the Lord himself shall come down from heaven with commandment, and with the voice of an archangel, and with the trumpet of God: and the dead who are in Christ, shall rise first.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	16	Then we who are alive, who are left, shall be taken up together with them in the clouds to meet Christ, into the air, and so shall we be always with the Lord.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	4	17	Wherefore, comfort ye one another with these words.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	1	But of the times and moments, brethren, you need not, that we should write to you;
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	2	For yourselves know perfectly, that the day of the Lord shall so come, as a thief in the night.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	3	For when they shall say, peace and security; then shall sudden destruction come upon them, as the pains upon her that is with child, and they shall not escape.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	4	But you, brethren, are not in darkness, that that day should overtake you as a thief.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	5	For all you are the children of light, and children of the day: we are not of the night, nor of darkness.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	6	Therefore, let us not sleep, as others do; but let us watch, and be sober.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	7	For they that sleep, sleep in the night; and they that are drunk, are drunk in the night.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	8	But let us, who are of the day, be sober, having on the breastplate of faith and charity, and for a helmet the hope of salvation.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	9	For God hath not appointed us unto wrath, but unto the purchasing of salvation by our Lord Jesus Christ,
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	10	Who died for us; that, whether we watch or sleep, we may live together with him.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	11	For which cause comfort one another; and edify one another, as you also do.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	12	And we beseech you, brethren, to know them who labour among you, and are over you in the Lord, and admonish you:
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	13	That you esteem them more abundantly in charity, for their work's sake. Have peace with them.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	14	And we beseech you, brethren, rebuke the unquiet, comfort the feeble minded, support the weak, be patient towards all men.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	15	See that none render evil for evil to any man; but ever follow that which is good towards each other, and towards all men.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	16	Always rejoice.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	17	Pray without ceasing.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	18	In all things give thanks; for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus concerning you all.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	19	Extinguish not the spirit.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	20	Despise not prophecies.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	21	But prove all things; hold fast that which is good.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	22	From all appearance of evil refrain yourselves.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	23	And may the God of peace himself sanctify you in all things; that your whole spirit, and soul, and body, may be preserved blameless in the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	24	He is faithful who hath called you, who also will do it.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	25	Brethren, pray for us.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	26	Salute all the brethren with a holy kiss.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	27	I charge you by the Lord, that this epistle be read to all the holy brethren.
1 Thessalonians	1Thess	59	5	28	The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you. Amen.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	1	Paul, and Sylvanus, and Timothy, to the church of the Thessalonians in God our Father, and the Lord Jesus Christ.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	2	Grace unto you, and peace from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	3	We are bound to give thanks always to God for you, brethren, as it is fitting, because your faith groweth exceedingly, and the charity of every one of you towards each other, aboundeth:
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	4	So that we ourselves also glory in you in the churches of God, for your patience and faith, and in all your persecutions and tribulations, which you endure,
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	5	For an example of the just judgment of God, that you may be counted worthy of the kingdom of God, for which also you suffer.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	6	Seeing it is a just thing with God to repay tribulation to them that trouble you:
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	7	And to you who are troubled, rest with us when the Lord Jesus shall be revealed from heaven, with the angels of his power:
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	8	In a flame of fire, giving vengeance to them who know not God, and who obey not the gospel of our Lord Jesus Christ.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	9	Who shall suffer eternal punishment in destruction, from the face of the Lord, and from the glory of his power:
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	10	When he shall come to be glorified in his saints, and to be made wonderful in all them who have believed; because our testimony was believed upon you in that day.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	11	Wherefore also we pray always for you; that our God would make you worthy of his vocation, and fulfill all the good pleasure of his goodness and the work of faith in power;
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	1	12	That the name of our Lord Jesus may be glorified in you, and you in him, according to the grace of our God, and of the Lord Jesus Christ.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	1	And we beseech you, brethren, by the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, and of our gathering together unto him:
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	2	That you be not easily moved from your sense, nor be terrified, neither by spirit, nor by word, nor by epistle, as sent from us, as if the day of the Lord were at hand.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	3	Let no man deceive you by any means, for unless there come a revolt first, and the man of sin be revealed, the son of perdition,
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	4	Who opposeth, and is lifted up above all that is called God, or that is worshipped, so that he sitteth in the temple of God, shewing himself as if he were God.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	5	Remember you not, that when I was yet with you, I told you these things?
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	6	And now you know what withholdeth, that he may be revealed in his time.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	7	For the mystery of iniquity already worketh; only that he who now holdeth, do hold, until he be taken out of the way.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	8	And then that wicked one shall be revealed whom the Lord Jesus shall kill with the spirit of his mouth; and shall destroy with the brightness of his coming, him,
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	9	Whose coming is according to the working of Satan, in all power, and signs, and lying wonders,
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	10	And in all seduction of iniquity to them that perish; because they receive not the love of the truth, that they might be saved. Therefore God shall send them the operation of error, to believe lying:
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	11	That all may be judged who have not believed the truth, but have consented to iniquity.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	12	But we ought to give thanks to God always for you, brethren, beloved of God, for that God hath chosen you firstfruits unto salvation, in sanctification of the spirit, and faith of the truth:
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	13	Whereunto also he hath called you by our gospel, unto the purchasing of the glory of our Lord Jesus Christ.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	14	Therefore, brethren, stand fast; and hold the traditions which you have learned, whether by word, or by our epistle.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	15	Now our Lord Jesus Christ himself, and God and our Father, who hath loved us, and hath given us everlasting consolation, and good hope in grace,
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	2	16	Exhort your hearts, and confirm you in every good work and word.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	1	For the rest, brethren, pray for us, that the word of God may run, and may be glorified, even as among you;
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	2	And that we may be delivered from importunate and evil men; for all men have not faith.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	3	But God is faithful, who will strengthen and keep you from evil.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	4	And we have confidence concerning you in the Lord, that the things which we command, you both do, and will do.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	5	And the Lord direct your hearts, in the charity of God, and the patience of Christ.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	6	And we charge you, brethren, in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that you withdraw yourselves from every brother walking disorderly, and not according to the tradition which they have received of us.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	7	For yourselves know how you ought to imitate us: for we were not disorderly among you;
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	8	Neither did we eat any man's bread for nothing, but in labour and in toil we worked night and day, lest we should be chargeable to any of you.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	9	Not as if we had not power: but that we might give ourselves a pattern unto you, to imitate us.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	10	For also when we were with you, this we declared to you: that, if any man will not work, neither let him eat.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	11	For we have heard there are some among you who walk disorderly, working not at all, but curiously meddling.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	12	Now we charge them that are such, and beseech them by the Lord Jesus Christ, that, working with silence, they would eat their own bread.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	13	But you, brethren, be not weary in well doing.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	14	And if any man obey not our word by this epistle, note that man, and do not keep company with him, that he may be ashamed:
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	15	Yet do not esteem him as an enemy, but admonish him as a brother.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	16	Now the Lord of peace himself give you everlasting peace in every place. The Lord be with you all.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	17	The salutation of Paul with my own hand; which is the sign in every epistle. So I write.
2 Thessalonians	2Thess	60	3	18	The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	1	Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, according to the commandment of God our Saviour, and of Christ Jesus our hope:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	2	To Timothy, his beloved son in faith. Grace, mercy, and peace from God the Father, and from Christ Jesus our Lord.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	3	As I desired thee to remain at Ephesus when I went into Macedonia, that thou mightest charge some not to teach otherwise,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	4	Not to give heed to fables and endless genealogies: which furnish questions rather than the edification of God, which is in faith.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	5	Now the end of the commandment is charity, from a pure heart, and a good conscience, and an unfeigned faith.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	6	From which things some going astray, are turned aside unto vain babbling:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	7	Desiring to be teachers of the law, understanding neither the things they say, nor whereof they affirm.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	8	But we know that the law is good, if a man use it lawfully:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	9	Knowing this, that the law is not made for the just man, but for the unjust and disobedient, for the ungodly, and for sinners, for the wicked and defiled, for murderers of fathers, and murderers of mothers, for manslayers,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	10	For fornicators, for them who defile themselves with mankind, for menstealers, for liars, for perjured persons, and whatever other thing is contrary to sound doctrine,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	11	Which is according to the gospel of the glory of the blessed God, which hath been committed to my trust.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	12	I give thanks who hath strengthened me, even to Christ Jesus our Lord, for that he hath counted me faithful, putting me in the ministry;
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	13	Who before was a blasphemer, and a persecutor, and contumelious. But I obtained the mercy of God, because I did it ignorantly in unbelief.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	14	Now the grace of our Lord hath abounded exceedingly with faith and love, which is in Christ Jesus.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	15	A faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into this world to save sinners, of whom I am the chief.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	16	But for this cause have I obtained mercy: that in me first Christ Jesus might shew forth all patience, for the information of them that shall believe in him unto life everlasting.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	17	Now to the king of ages, immortal, invisible, the only God, be honour and glory for ever and ever. Amen.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	18	This precept I commend to thee, O son Timothy; according to the prophecies going before on thee, that thou war in them a good warfare,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	19	Having faith and a good conscience, which some rejecting have made shipwreck concerning the faith.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	1	20	Of whom is Hymeneus and Alexander, whom I have delivered up to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	1	I desire therefore, first of all, that supplications, prayers, intercessions, and thanksgivings be made for all men:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	2	For kings, and for all that are in high station: that we may lead a quiet and a peaceable life in all piety and chastity.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	3	For this is good and acceptable in the sight of God our Saviour,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	4	Who will have all men to be saved, and to come to the knowledge of the truth.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	5	For there is one God, and one mediator of God and men, the man Christ Jesus:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	6	Who gave himself a redemption for all, a testimony in due times.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	7	Whereunto I am appointed a preacher and an apostle, (I say the truth, I lie not,) a doctor of the Gentiles in faith and truth.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	8	I will therefore that men pray in every place, lifting up pure hands, without anger and contention.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	9	In like manner women also in decent apparel: adorning themselves with modesty and sobriety, not with plaited hair, or gold, or pearls, or costly attire,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	10	But as it becometh women professing godliness, with good works.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	11	Let the woman learn in silence, with all subjection.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	12	But I suffer not a woman to teach, nor to use authority over the man: but to be in silence.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	13	For Adam was first formed; then Eve.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	14	And Adam was not seduced; but the woman being seduced, was in the transgression.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	2	15	Yet she shall be saved through childbearing; if she continue in faith, and love, and sanctification, with sobriety.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	1	A faithful saying: if a man desire the office of a bishop, he desireth a good work.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	2	It behoveth therefore a bishop to be blameless, the husband of one wife, sober, prudent, of good behaviour, chaste, given to hospitality, a teacher,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	3	Not given to wine, no striker, but modest, not quarrelsome, not covetous, but
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	4	One that ruleth well his own house, having his children in subjection with all chastity.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	5	But if a man know not how to rule his own house, how shall he take care of the church of God?
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	6	Not a neophyte: lest being puffed up with pride, he fall into the judgment of the devil.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	7	Moreover he must have a good testimony of them who are without: lest he fall into reproach and the snare of the devil.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	8	Deacons in like manner chaste, not double tongued, not given to much wine, not greedy of filthy lucre:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	9	Holding the mystery of faith in a pure conscience.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	10	And let these also first be proved: and so let them minister, having no crime.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	11	The women in like manner chaste, not slanderers, but sober, faithful in all things.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	12	Let deacons be the husbands of one wife: who rule well their children, and their own houses.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	13	For they that have ministered well, shall purchase to themselves a good degree, and much confidence in the faith which is in Christ Jesus.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	14	These things I write to thee, hoping that I shall come to thee shortly.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	15	But if I tarry long, that thou mayest know how thou oughtest to behave thyself in the house of God, which is the church of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	3	16	And evidently great is the mystery of godliness, which was manifested in the flesh, was justified in the spirit, appeared unto angels, hath been preached unto the Gentiles, is believed in the world, is taken up in glory.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	1	Now the Spirit manifestly saith, that in the last times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to spirits of error, and doctrines of devils,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	2	Speaking lies in hypocrisy, and having their conscience seared,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	3	Forbidding to marry, to abstain from meats, which God hath created to be received with thanksgiving by the faithful, and by them that have known the truth.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	4	For every creature of God is good, and nothing to be rejected that is received with thanksgiving:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	5	For it is sanctified by the word of God and prayer.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	6	These things proposing to the brethren, thou shalt be a good minister of Christ Jesus, nourished up in the words of faith, and of the good doctrine which thou hast attained unto.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	7	But avoid foolish and old wives' fables: and exercise thyself unto godliness.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	8	For bodily exercise is profitable to little: but godliness is profitable to all things, having promise of the life that now is, and of that which is to come.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	9	A faithful saying and worthy of all acceptation.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	10	For therefore we labor and are reviled, because we hope in the living God, who is the Saviour of all men, especially of the faithful.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	11	These things command and teach.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	12	Let no man despise thy youth: but be thou an example of the faithful in word, in conversation, in charity, in faith, in chastity.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	13	Till I come, attend unto reading, to exhortation, and to doctrine.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	14	Neglect not the grace that is in thee, which was given thee by prophesy, with imposition of the hands of the priesthood.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	15	Meditate upon these things, be wholly in these things: that thy profiting may be manifest to all.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	4	16	Take heed to thyself and to doctrine: be earnest in them. For in doing this thou shalt both save thyself and them that hear thee.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	1	An ancient man rebuke not, but entreat him as a father: young men, as brethren:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	2	Old women, as mothers: young women, as sisters, in all chastity.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	3	Honour widows, that are widows indeed.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	4	But if any widow have children, or grandchildren, let her learn first to govern her own house, and to make a return of duty to her parents: for this is acceptable before God.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	5	But she that is a widow indeed, and desolate, let her trust in God, and continue in supplications and prayers night and day.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	6	For she that liveth in pleasures, is dead while she is living.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	7	And this give in charge, that they may be blameless.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	8	But if any man have not care of his own, and especially of those of his house, he hath denied the faith, and is worse than an infidel.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	9	Let a widow be chosen of no less than threescore years of age, who hath been the wife of one husband.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	10	Having testimony for her good works, if she have brought up children, if she have received to harbour, if she have washed the saints' feet, if she have ministered to them that suffer tribulation, if she have diligently followed every good work.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	11	But the younger widows avoid. For when they have grown wanton in Christ, they will marry:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	12	Having damnation, because they have made void their first faith.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	13	And withal being idle they learn to go about from house to house: and are not only idle, but tattlers also, and busybodies, speaking things which they ought not.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	14	I will therefore that the younger should marry, bear children, be mistresses of families, give no occasion to the adversary to speak evil.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	15	For some are already turned aside after Satan.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	16	If any of the faithful have widows, let him minister to them, and let not the church be charged: that there may be sufficient for them that are widows indeed.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	17	Let the priests that rule well, be esteemed worthy of double honour: especially they who labour in the word and doctrine:
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	18	For the scripture saith: Thou shalt not muzzle the ox that treadeth out the corn: and, The labourer is worthy of his reward.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	19	Against a priest receive not an accusation, but under two or three witnesses.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	20	Them that sin reprove before all: that the rest also may have fear.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	21	I charge thee before God, and Christ Jesus, and the elect angels, that thou observe these things without prejudice, doing nothing by declining to either side.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	22	Impose not hands lightly upon any man, neither be partaker of other men's sins. Keep thyself chaste.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	23	Do not still drink water, but use a little wine for thy stomach's sake, and thy frequent infirmities.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	24	Some men's sins are manifest, going before to judgment: and some men they follow after.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	5	25	In like manner also good deeds are manifest: and they that are otherwise, cannot be hid.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	1	Whosoever are servants under the yoke, let them count their masters worthy of all honour; lest the name of the Lord and his doctrine be blasphemed.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	2	But they that have believing masters, let them not despise them, because they are brethren; but serve them the rather, because they are faithful and beloved, who are partakers of the benefit. These things teach and exhort.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	3	If any man teach otherwise, and consent not to the sound words of our Lord Jesus Christ, and to that doctrine which is according to godliness,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	4	He is proud, knowing nothing, but sick about questions and strifes of words; from which arise envies, contentions, blasphemies, evil suspicions,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	5	Conflicts of men corrupted in mind, and who are destitute of the truth, supposing gain to be godliness.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	6	But godliness with contentment is great gain.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	7	For we brought nothing into this world: and certainly we can carry nothing out.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	8	But having food, and wherewith to be covered, with these we are content.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	9	For they that will become rich, fall into temptation, and into the snare of the devil, and into many unprofitable and hurtful desires, which drown men into destruction and perdition.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	10	For the desire of money is the root of all evils; which some coveting have erred from the faith, and have entangled themselves in many sorrows.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	11	But thou, O man of God, fly these things: and pursue justice, godliness, faith, charity, patience, mildness.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	12	Fight the good fight of faith: lay hold on eternal life, whereunto thou art called, and hast confessed a good confession before many witnesses.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	13	I charge thee before God, who quickeneth all things, and before Christ Jesus, who gave testimony under Pontius Pilate, a good confession,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	14	That thou keep the commandment without spot, blameless, unto the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	15	Which in his times he shall shew who is the Blessed and only Mighty, the King of kings, and Lord of lords;
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	16	Who only hath immortality, and inhabiteth light inaccessible, whom no man hath seen, nor can see: to whom be honour and empire everlasting. Amen.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	17	Charge the rich of this world not to be highminded, nor to trust in the uncertainty of riches, but in the living God, (who giveth us abundantly all things to enjoy,)
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	18	To do good, to be rich in good works, to give easily, to communicate to others,
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	19	To lay up in store for themselves a good foundation against the time to come, that they may lay hold on the true life.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	20	O Timothy, keep that which is committed to thy trust, avoiding the profane novelties of words, and oppositions of knowledge falsely so called.
1 Timothy	1Tim	61	6	21	Which some promising, have erred concerning the faith. Grace be with thee. Amen.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	1	Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, by the will of God, according to the promise of life, which is in Christ Jesus.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	2	To Timothy my dearly beloved son, grace, mercy, and peace, from God the Father, and from Christ Jesus our Lord.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	3	I give thanks to God, whom I serve from my forefathers with a pure conscience, that without ceasing, I have a remembrance of thee in my prayers, night and day.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	4	Desiring to see thee, being mindful of thy tears, that I may be filled with joy,
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	5	Calling to mind that faith which is in thee unfeigned, which also dwelt first in thy grandmother Lois, and in thy mother Eunice, and I am certain that in thee also.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	6	For which cause I admonish thee, that thou stir up the grace of God which is in thee, by the imposition of my hands.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	7	For God hath not given us the spirit of fear: but of power, and of love, and of sobriety.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	8	Be not thou therefore ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner: but labour with the gospel, according to the power of God,
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	9	Who hath delivered us and called us by his holy calling, not according to our works, but according to his own purpose and grace, which was given us in Christ Jesus before the times of the world.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	10	But is now made manifest by the illumination of our Saviour Jesus Christ, who hath destroyed death, and hath brought to light life and incorruption by the gospel:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	11	Wherein I am appointed a preacher, and an apostle, and teacher of the Gentiles.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	12	For which cause I also suffer these things: but I am not ashamed. For I know whom I have believed, and I am certain that he is able to keep that which I have committed unto him, against that day.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	13	Hold the form of sound words, which thou hast heard of me in faith, and in the love which is in Christ Jesus.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	14	Keep the good thing committed to thy trust by the Holy Ghost, who dwelleth in us.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	15	Thou knowest this, that all they who are in Asia, are turned away from me: of whom are Phigellus and Hermogenes.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	16	The Lord give mercy to the house of Onesiphorus: because he hath often refreshed me, and hath not been ashamed of my chain:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	17	But when he was come to Rome, he carefully sought me, and found me.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	1	18	The Lord grant unto him to find mercy of the Lord in that day: and in how many things he ministered unto me at Ephesus, thou very well knowest.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	1	Thou therefore, my son, be strong in the grace which is in Christ Jesus:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	2	And the things which thou hast heard of me by many witnesses, the same commend to faithful men, who shall be fit to teach others also.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	3	Labour as a good soldier of Christ Jesus.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	4	No man, being a soldier to God, entangleth himself with secular businesses; that he may please him to whom he hath engaged himself.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	5	For he also that striveth for the mastery, is not crowned, except he strive lawfully.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	6	The husbandman, that laboureth, must first partake of the fruits.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	7	Understand what I say: for the Lord will give thee in all things understanding.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	8	Be mindful that the Lord Jesus Christ is risen again from the dead, of the seed of David, according to my gospel.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	9	Wherein I labour even unto bands, as an evildoer; but the word of God is not bound.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	10	Therefore I endure all things for the sake of the elect, that they also may obtain the salvation, which is in Christ Jesus, with heavenly glory.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	11	A faithful saying: for if we be dead with him, we shall live also with him.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	12	If we suffer, we shall also reign with him. If we deny him, he will also deny us.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	13	If we believe not, he continueth faithful, he can not deny himself.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	14	Of these things put them in mind, charging them before the Lord. Contend not in words, for it is to no profit, but to the subverting of the hearers.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	15	Carefully study to present thyself approved unto God, a workman that needeth not to be ashamed, rightly handling the word of truth.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	16	But shun profane and vain babblings: for they grow much towards ungodliness.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	17	And their speech spreadeth like a canker: of whom are Hymeneus and Philetus:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	18	Who have erred from the truth, saying, that the resurrection is past already, and have subverted the faith of some.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	19	But the sure foundation of God standeth firm, having this seal: the Lord knoweth who are his; and let every one depart from iniquity who nameth the name of the Lord.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	20	But in a great house there are not only vessels of gold and silver, but also of wood and earth: and some indeed unto honour, but some unto dishonour.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	21	If any man therefore shall cleanse himself from these, he shall be a vessel unto honour, sanctified and profitable to the Lord, prepared unto every good work.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	22	But flee thou youthful desires, and pursue justice, faith, charity, and peace, with them that call on the Lord out of a pure heart.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	23	And avoid foolish and unlearned questions, knowing that they beget strifes.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	24	But the servant of the Lord must not wrangle: but be mild towards all men, apt to teach, patient,
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	25	With modesty admonishing them that resist the truth: if peradventure God may give them repentance to know the truth,
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	2	26	And they may recover themselves from the snares of the devil, by whom they are held captive at his will.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	1	Know also this, that, in the last days, shall come dangerous times.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	2	Men shall be lovers of themselves, covetous, haughty, proud, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, ungrateful, wicked,
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	3	Without affection, without peace, slanderers, incontinent, unmerciful, without kindness,
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	4	Traitors, stubborn, puffed up, and lovers of pleasures more than of God:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	5	Having an appearance indeed of godliness, but denying the power thereof. Now these avoid.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	6	For of these sort are they who creep into houses, and lead captive silly women laden with sins, who are led away with divers desires:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	7	Ever learning, and never attaining to the knowledge of the truth.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	8	Now as Jannes and Mambres resisted Moses, so these also resist the truth, men corrupted in mind, reprobate concerning the faith.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	9	But they shall proceed no farther; for their folly shall be manifest to all men, as theirs also was.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	10	But thou hast fully known my doctrine, manner of life, purpose, faith, longsuffering, love, patience,
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	11	Persecutions, afflictions: such as came upon me at Antioch, at Iconium, and at Lystra: what persecutions I endured, and out of them all the Lord delivered me.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	12	And all that will live godly in Christ Jesus, shall suffer persecution.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	13	But evil men and seducers shall grow worse and worse: erring, and driving into error.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	14	But continue thou in those things which thou hast learned, and which have been committed to thee: knowing of whom thou hast learned them;
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	15	And because from thy infancy thou hast known the holy scriptures, which can instruct thee to salvation, by the faith which is in Christ Jesus.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	16	All scripture, inspired of God, is profitable to teach, to reprove, to correct, to instruct in justice,
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	3	17	That the man of God may be perfect, furnished to every good work.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	1	I charge thee, before God and Jesus Christ, who shall judge the living and the dead, by his coming, and his kingdom:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	2	Preach the word: be instant in season, out of season: reprove, entreat, rebuke in all patience and doctrine.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	3	For there shall be a time, when they will not endure sound doctrine; but, according to their own desires, they will heap to themselves teachers, having itching ears:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	4	And will indeed turn away their hearing from the truth, but will be turned unto fables.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	5	But be thou vigilant, labour in all things, do the work of an evangelist, fulfill thy ministry. Be sober.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	6	For I am even now ready to be sacrificed: and the time of my dissolution is at hand.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	7	I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	8	As to the rest, there is laid up for me a crown of justice, which the Lord the just judge will render to me in that day: and not only to me, but to them also that love his coming. Make haste to come to me quickly.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	9	For Demas hath left me, loving this world, and is gone to Thessalonica:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	10	Crescens into Galatia, Titus into Dalmatia.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	11	Only Luke is with me. Take Mark, and bring him with thee: for he is profitable to me for the ministry.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	12	But Tychicus I have sent to Ephesus.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	13	The cloak that I left at Troas, with Carpus, when thou comest, bring with thee, and the books, especially the parchments.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	14	Alexander the coppersmith hath done me much evil: the Lord will reward him according to his works:
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	15	Whom do thou also avoid, for he hath greatly withstood our words.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	16	At my first answer no man stood with me, but all forsook me: may it not be laid to their charge.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	17	But the Lord stood by me, and strengthened me, that by me the preaching may be accomplished, and that all the Gentiles may hear: and I was delivered out of the mouth of the lion.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	18	The Lord hath delivered me from every evil work: and will preserve me unto his heavenly kingdom, to whom be glory for ever and ever. Amen.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	19	Salute Prisca and Aquila, and the household of Onesiphorus.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	20	Erastus remained at Corinth. And Trophimus I left sick at Miletus.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	21	Make haste to come before winter. Eubulus and Pudens, and Linus and Claudia, and all the brethren, salute thee.
2 Timothy	2Tim	62	4	22	The Lord Jesus Christ be with thy spirit. Grace be with you. Amen.
Titus	Tit	63	1	1	Paul, a servant of God, and an apostle of Jesus Christ, according to the faith of the elect of God and the acknowledging of the truth, which is according to godliness:
Titus	Tit	63	1	2	Unto the hope of life everlasting, which God, who lieth not, hath promised before the times of the world:
Titus	Tit	63	1	3	But hath in due times manifested his word in preaching, which is committed to me according to the commandment of God our Saviour:
Titus	Tit	63	1	4	To Titus my beloved son, according to the common faith, grace and peace from God the Father, and from Christ Jesus our Saviour.
Titus	Tit	63	1	5	For this cause I left thee in Crete, that thou shouldest set in order the things that are wanting, and shouldest ordain priests in every city, as I also appointed thee:
Titus	Tit	63	1	6	If any be without crime, the husband of one wife, having faithful children, not accused of riot, or unruly.
Titus	Tit	63	1	7	For a bishop must be without crime, as the steward of God: not proud, not subject to anger, not given to wine, no striker, not greedy of filthy lucre:
Titus	Tit	63	1	8	But given to hospitality, gentle, sober, just, holy, continent:
Titus	Tit	63	1	9	Embracing that faithful word which is according to doctrine, that he may be able to exhort in sound doctrine, and to convince the gainsayers.
Titus	Tit	63	1	10	For there are also many disobedient, vain talkers, and seducers: especially they who are of the circumcision:
Titus	Tit	63	1	11	Who must be reproved, who subvert whole houses, teaching things which they ought not, for filthy lucre's sake.
Titus	Tit	63	1	12	One of them a prophet of their own, said, The Cretians are always liars, evil beasts, slothful bellies.
Titus	Tit	63	1	13	This testimony is true. Wherefore rebuke them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith;
Titus	Tit	63	1	14	Not giving heed to Jewish fables and commandments of men, who turn themselves away from the truth.
Titus	Tit	63	1	15	All things are clean to the clean: but to them that are defiled, and to unbelievers, nothing is clean: but both their mind and their conscience are defiled.
Titus	Tit	63	1	16	They profess that they know God: but in their works they deny him; being abominable, and incredulous, and to every good work reprobate.
Titus	Tit	63	2	1	But speak thou the things that become sound doctrine:
Titus	Tit	63	2	2	That the aged men be sober, chaste, prudent, sound in faith, in love, in patience.
Titus	Tit	63	2	3	The aged women, in like manner, in holy attire, not false accusers, not given to much wine, teaching well:
Titus	Tit	63	2	4	That they may teach the young women to be wise, to love their husbands, to love their children,
Titus	Tit	63	2	5	To be discreet, chaste, sober, having a care of the house, gentle, obedient to their husbands, that the word of God be not blasphemed.
Titus	Tit	63	2	6	Young men, in like manner, exhort that they be sober.
Titus	Tit	63	2	7	In all things shew thyself an example of good works, in doctrine, in integrity, in gravity,
Titus	Tit	63	2	8	The sound word that can not be blamed: that he, who is on the contrary part, may be afraid, having no evil to say of us.
Titus	Tit	63	2	9	Exhort servants to be obedient to their masters, in all things pleasing, not gainsaying:
Titus	Tit	63	2	10	Not defrauding, but in all things shewing good fidelity, that they may adorn the doctrine of God our Saviour in all things:
Titus	Tit	63	2	11	For the grace of God our Saviour hath appeared to all men;
Titus	Tit	63	2	12	Instructing us, that, denying ungodliness and worldly desires, we should live soberly, and justly, and godly in this world,
Titus	Tit	63	2	13	Looking for the blessed hope and coming of the glory of the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ,
Titus	Tit	63	2	14	Who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and might cleanse to himself a people acceptable, a pursuer of good works.
Titus	Tit	63	2	15	These things speak, and exhort and rebuke with all authority. Let no man despise thee.
Titus	Tit	63	3	1	Admonish them to be subject to princes and powers, to obey at a word, to be ready to every good work.
Titus	Tit	63	3	2	To speak evil of no man, not to be litigious, but gentle: shewing all mildness towards all men.
Titus	Tit	63	3	3	For we ourselves also were some time unwise, incredulous, erring, slaves to divers desires and pleasures, living in malice and envy, hateful, and hating one another.
Titus	Tit	63	3	4	But when the goodness and kindness of God our Saviour appeared:
Titus	Tit	63	3	5	Not by the works of justice, which we have done, but according to his mercy, he saved us, by the laver of regeneration, and renovation of the Holy Ghost;
Titus	Tit	63	3	6	Whom he hath poured forth upon us abundantly, through Jesus Christ our Saviour:
Titus	Tit	63	3	7	That, being justified by his grace, we may be heirs, according to hope of life everlasting.
Titus	Tit	63	3	8	It is a faithful saying: and these things I will have thee affirm constantly: that they, who believe in God, may be careful to excel in good works. These things are good and profitable unto men.
Titus	Tit	63	3	9	But avoid foolish questions, and genealogies, and contentions, and strivings about the law. For they are unprofitable and vain.
Titus	Tit	63	3	10	A man that is a heretic, after the first and second admonition, avoid:
Titus	Tit	63	3	11	Knowing that he, that is such an one, is subverted, and sinneth, being condemned by his own judgment.
Titus	Tit	63	3	12	When I shall send to thee Artemas or Tychicus, make haste to come unto me to Nicopolis. For there I have determined to winter.
Titus	Tit	63	3	13	Send forward Zenas, the lawyer, and Apollo, with care, that nothing be wanting to them.
Titus	Tit	63	3	14	And let our men also learn to excel in good works for necessary uses: that they be not unfruitful.
Titus	Tit	63	3	15	All that are with me salute thee: salute them that love us in the faith. The grace of God be with you all. Amen.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	1	Paul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy, a brother: to Philemon, our beloved and fellow labourer;
Philemon	Phm	64	1	2	And to Appia, our dearest sister, and to Archippus, our fellow soldier, and to the church which is in thy house:
Philemon	Phm	64	1	3	Grace to you and peace from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	4	I give thanks to my God, always making a remembrance of thee in my prayers.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	5	Hearing of thy charity and faith, which thou hast in the Lord Jesus, and towards all the saints:
Philemon	Phm	64	1	6	That the communication of thy faith may be made evident in the acknowledgment of every good work, that is in you in Christ Jesus.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	7	For I have had great joy and consolation in thy charity, because the bowels of the saints have been refreshed by thee, brother.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	8	Wherefore though I have much confidence in Christ Jesus, to command thee that which is to the purpose:
Philemon	Phm	64	1	9	For charity sake I rather beseech, whereas thou art such a one, as Paul an old man, and now a prisoner also of Jesus Christ.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	10	I beseech thee for my son, whom I have begotten in my bands, Onesimus,
Philemon	Phm	64	1	11	Who hath been heretofore unprofitable to thee, but now is profitable both to me and thee,
Philemon	Phm	64	1	12	Whom I have sent back to thee. And do thou receive him as my own bowels.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	13	Whom I would have retained with me, that in thy stead he might have ministered to me in the bands of the gospel:
Philemon	Phm	64	1	14	But without thy counsel I would do nothing: that thy good deed might not be as it were of necessity, but voluntary.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	15	For perhaps he therefore departed for a season from thee, that thou mightest receive him again for ever:
Philemon	Phm	64	1	16	Not now as a servant, but instead of a servant, a most dear brother, especially to me: but how much more to thee both in the flesh and in the Lord?
Philemon	Phm	64	1	17	If therefore thou count me a partner, receive him as myself.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	18	And if he hath wronged thee in any thing, or is in thy debt, put that to my account.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	19	I Paul have written it with my own hand: I will repay it: not to say to thee, that thou owest me thy own self also.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	20	Yea, brother. May I enjoy thee in the Lord. Refresh my bowels in the Lord.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	21	Trusting in thy obedience, I have written to thee: knowing that thou wilt also do more than I say.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	22	But withal prepare me also a lodging. For I hope that through your prayers I shall be given unto you.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	23	There salute thee Epaphras, my fellow prisoner in Christ Jesus;
Philemon	Phm	64	1	24	Mark, Aristarchus, Demas, and Luke my fellow labourers.
Philemon	Phm	64	1	25	The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit. Amen.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	1	God, who, at sundry times and in divers manners, spoke in times past to the fathers by the prophets, last of all,
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	2	In these days hath spoken to us by his Son, whom he hath appointed heir of all things, by whom also he made the world.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	3	Who being the brightness of his glory, and the figure of his substance, and upholding all things by the word of his power, making purgation of sins, sitteth on the right hand of the majesty on high.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	4	Being made so much better than the angels, as he hath inherited a more excellent name than they.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	5	For to which of the angels hath he said at any time, Thou art my Son, today have I begotten thee? And again, I will be to him a Father, and he shall be to me a Son?
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	6	And again, when he bringeth in the first begotten into the world, he saith: And let all the angels of God adore him.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	7	And to the angels indeed he saith: He that maketh his angels spirits, and his ministers a flame of fire.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	8	But to the Son: Thy throne, O God, is for ever and ever: a sceptre of justice is the sceptre of thy kingdom.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	9	Thou hast loved justice, and hated iniquity: therefore God, thy God, hath anointed thee with the oil of gladness above thy fellows.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	10	And: Thou in the beginning, O Lord, didst found the earth: and the works of thy hands are the heavens.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	11	They shall perish, but thou shalt continue: and they shall all grow old as a garment.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	12	And as a vesture shalt thou change them, and they shall be changed: but thou art the selfsame, and thy years shall not fail.
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	13	But to which of the angels said he at any time: Sit on my right hand, until I make thy enemies thy footstool?
Hebrews	Heb	65	1	14	Are they not all ministering spirits, sent to minister for them, who shall receive the inheritance of salvation?
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	1	Therefore ought we more diligently to observe the things which we have heard, lest perhaps we should let them slip.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	2	For if the word, spoken by angels, became steadfast, and every transgression and disobedience received a just recompense of reward:
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	3	How shall we escape if we neglect so great salvation? which having begun to be declared by the Lord, was confirmed unto us by them that heard him.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	4	God also bearing them witness by signs, and wonders, and divers miracles, and distributions of the Holy Ghost, according to his own will.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	5	For God hath not subjected unto angels the world to come, whereof we speak.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	6	But one in a certain place hath testified, saying: What is man, that thou art mindful of him: or the son of man, that thou visitest him?
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	7	Thou hast made him a little lower than the angels: thou hast crowned him with glory and honour, and hast set him over the works of thy hands:
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	8	Thou hast subjected all things under his feet. For in that he hath subjected all things to him, he left nothing not subject to him. But now we see not as yet all things subject to him.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	9	But we see Jesus, who was made a little lower than the angels, for the suffering of death, crowned with glory and honour: that, through the grace of God, he might taste death for all.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	10	For it became him, for whom are all things, and by whom are all things, who had brought many children into glory, to perfect the author of their salvation, by his passion.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	11	For both he that sanctifieth, and they who are sanctified, are all of one. For which cause he is not ashamed to call them brethren, saying:
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	12	I will declare thy name to my brethren; in the midst of the church will I praise thee.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	13	And again: I will put my trust in him. And again: Behold I and my children, whom God hath given me.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	14	Therefore because the children are partakers of flesh and blood, he also himself in like manner hath been partaker of the same: that, through death, he might destroy him who had the empire of death, that is to say, the devil:
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	15	And might deliver them, who through the fear of death were all their lifetime subject to servitude.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	16	For no where doth he take hold of the angels: but of the seed of Abraham he taketh hold.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	17	Wherefore it behoved him in all things to be made like unto his brethren, that he might become a merciful and faithful priest before God, that he might be a propitiation for the sins of the people.
Hebrews	Heb	65	2	18	For in that, wherein he himself hath suffered and been tempted, he is able to succour them also that are tempted.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	1	Wherefore, holy brethren, partakers of the heavenly vocation, consider the apostle and high priest of our confession, Jesus:
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	2	Who is faithful to him that made him, as was also Moses in all his house.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	3	For this man was counted worthy of greater glory than Moses, by so much as he that hath built the house, hath greater honour than the house.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	4	For every house is built by some man: but he that created all things, is God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	5	And Moses indeed was faithful in all his house as a servant, for a testimony of those things which were to be said:
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	6	But Christ as the Son in his own house: which house are we, if we hold fast the confidence and glory of hope unto the end.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	7	Wherefore, as the Holy Ghost saith: Today if you shall hear his voice,
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	8	Harden not your hearts, as in the provocation; in the day of temptation in the desert,
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	9	Where your fathers tempted me, proved and saw my works,
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	10	Forty years: for which cause I was offended with this generation, and I said: They always err in heart. And they have not known my ways,
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	11	As I have sworn in my wrath: If they shall enter into my rest.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	12	Take heed, brethren, lest perhaps there be in any of you an evil heart of unbelief, to depart from the living God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	13	But exhort one another every day, whilst it is called today, that none of you be hardened through the deceitfulness of sin.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	14	For we are made partakers of Christ: yet so, if we hold the beginning of his substance firm unto the end.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	15	While it is said, Today if you shall hear his voice, harden not your hearts, as in that provocation.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	16	For some who heard did provoke: but not all that came out of Egypt by Moses.
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	17	And with whom was he offended forty years? Was it not with them that sinned, whose carcasses were overthrown in the desert?
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	18	And to whom did he swear, that they should not enter into his rest: but to them that were incredulous?
Hebrews	Heb	65	3	19	And we see that they could not enter in, because of unbelief.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	1	Let us fear therefore lest the promise being left of entering into his rest, any of you should be thought to be wanting.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	2	For unto us also it hath been declared, in like manner as unto them. But the word of hearing did not profit them, not being mixed with faith of those things they heard.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	3	For we, who have believed, shall enter into rest; as he said: As I have sworn in my wrath; If they shall enter into my rest; and this indeed when the works from the foundation of the world were finished.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	4	For in a certain place he spoke of the seventh day thus: And God rested the seventh day from all his works.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	5	And in this place again: If they shall enter into my rest.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	6	Seeing then it remaineth that some are to enter into it, and they, to whom it was first preached, did not enter because of unbelief:
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	7	Again he limiteth a certain day, saying in David, Today, after so long a time, as it is above said: Today if you shall hear his voice, harden not your hearts.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	8	For if Jesus had given them rest, he would never have afterwards spoken of another day.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	9	There remaineth therefore a day of rest for the people of God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	10	For he that is entered into his rest, the same also hath rested from his works, as God did from his.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	11	Let us hasten therefore to enter into that rest; lest any man fall into the same example of unbelief.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	12	For the word of God is living and effectual, and more piercing than any two edged sword; and reaching unto the division of the soul and the spirit, of the joints also and the marrow, and is a discerner of the thoughts and intents of the heart.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	13	Neither is there any creature invisible in his sight: but all things are naked and open to his eyes, to whom our speech is.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	14	Having therefore a great high priest that hath passed into the heavens, Jesus the Son of God: let us hold fast our confession.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	15	For we have not a high priest, who can not have compassion on our infirmities: but one tempted in all things like as we are, without sin.
Hebrews	Heb	65	4	16	Let us go therefore with confidence to the throne of grace: that we may obtain mercy, and find grace in seasonable aid.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	1	For every high priest taken from among men, is ordained for men in the things that appertain to God, that he may offer up gifts and sacrifices for sins:
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	2	Who can have compassion on them that are ignorant and that err: because he himself also is compassed with infirmity.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	3	And therefore he ought, as for the people, so also for himself, to offer for sins.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	4	Neither doth any man take the honour to himself, but he that is called by God, as Aaron was.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	5	So Christ also did not glorify himself, that he might be made a high priest: but he that said unto him: Thou art my Son, this day have I begotten thee.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	6	As he saith also in another place: Thou art a priest for ever, according to the order of Melchisedech.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	7	Who in the days of his flesh, with a strong cry and tears, offering up prayers and supplications to him that was able to save him from death, was heard for his reverence.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	8	And whereas indeed he was the Son of God, he learned obedience by the things which he suffered:
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	9	And being consummated, he became, to all that obey him, the cause of eternal salvation.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	10	Called by God a high priest according to the order of Melchisedech.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	11	Of whom we have much to say, and hard to be intelligibly uttered: because you are become weak to hear.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	12	For whereas for the time you ought to be masters, you have need to be taught again what are the first elements of the words of God: and you are become such as have need of milk, and not of strong meat.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	13	For every one that is a partaker of milk, is unskillful in the word of justice: for he is a little child.
Hebrews	Heb	65	5	14	But strong meat is for the perfect; for them who by custom have their senses exercised to the discerning of good and evil.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	1	Wherefore leaving the word of the beginning of Christ, let us go on to things more perfect, not laying again the foundation of penance from dead works, and of faith towards God,
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	2	Of the doctrine of baptisms, and imposition of hands, and of the resurrection of the dead, and of eternal judgment.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	3	And this will we do, if God permit.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	4	For it is impossible for those who were once illuminated, have tasted also the heavenly gift, and were made partakers of the Holy Ghost,
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	5	Have moreover tasted the good word of God, and the powers of the world to come,
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	6	And are fallen away: to be renewed again to penance, crucifying again to themselves the Son of God, and making him a mockery.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	7	For the earth that drinketh in the rain which cometh often upon it, and bringeth forth herbs meet for them by whom it is tilled, receiveth blessing from God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	8	But that which bringeth forth thorns and briers, is reprobate, and very near unto a curse, whose end is to be burnt.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	9	But, my dearly beloved, we trust better things of you, and nearer to salvation; though we speak thus.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	10	For God is not unjust, that he should forget your work, and the love which you have shewn in his name, you who have ministered, and do minister to the saints.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	11	And we desire that every one of you shew forth the same carefulness to the accomplishing of hope unto the end:
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	12	That you become not slothful, but followers of them, who through faith and patience shall inherit the promises.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	13	For God making promise to Abraham, because he had no one greater by whom he might swear, swore by himself,
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	14	Saying: Unless blessing I shall bless thee, and multiplying I shall multiply thee.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	15	And so patiently enduring he obtained the promise.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	16	For men swear by one greater than themselves: and an oath for confirmation is the end of all their controversy.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	17	Wherein God, meaning more abundantly to shew to the heirs of the promise the immutability of his counsel, interposed an oath:
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	18	That by two immutable things, in which it is impossible for God to lie, we may have the strongest comfort, who have fled for refuge to hold fast the hope set before us.
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	19	Which we have as an anchor of the soul, sure and firm, and which entereth in even within the veil;
Hebrews	Heb	65	6	20	Where the forerunner Jesus is entered for us, made a high priest for ever according to the order of Melchisedech.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	1	For this Melchisedech was king of Salem, priest of the most high God, who met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings, and blessed him:
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	2	To whom also Abraham divided the tithes of all: who first indeed by interpretation, is king of justice: and then also king of Salem, that is, king of peace:
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	3	Without father, without mother, without genealogy, having neither beginning of days nor end of life, but likened unto the Son of God, continueth a priest for ever.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	4	Now consider how great this man is, to whom also Abraham the patriarch gave tithes out of the principal things.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	5	And indeed they that are of the sons of Levi, who receive the priesthood, have a commandment to take tithes of the people according to the law, that is to say, of their brethren: though they themselves also came out of the loins of Abraham.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	6	But he, whose pedigree is not numbered among them, received tithes of Abraham, and blessed him that had the promises.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	7	And without all contradiction, that which is less, is blessed by the better.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	8	And here indeed, men that die, receive thithes: but there he hath witness, that he liveth.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	9	And (as it may be said) even Levi who received tithes, paid tithes in Abraham:
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	10	For he was yet in the loins of his father, when Melchisedech met him.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	11	If then perfection was by the Levitical priesthood, (for under it the people received the law,) what further need was there that another priest should rise according to the order of Melchisedech, and not be called according to the order of Aaron?
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	12	For the priesthood being translated, it is necessary that a translation also be made of the law.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	13	For he, of whom these things are spoken, is of another tribe, of which no one attended on the altar.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	14	For it is evident that our Lord sprung out of Juda: in which tribe Moses spoke nothing concerning priests.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	15	And it is yet far more evident: if according to the similitude of Melchisedech there ariseth another priest,
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	16	Who is made not according to the law of a carnal commandment, but according to the power of an indissoluble life:
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	17	For he testifieth: Thou art a priest for ever, according to the order of Melchisedech.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	18	There is indeed a setting aside of the former commandment, because of the weakness and unprofitableness thereof:
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	19	(For the law brought nothing to perfection,) but a bringing in of a better hope, by which we draw nigh to God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	20	And inasmuch as it is not without an oath, (for the others indeed were made priests without an oath;
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	21	But this with an oath, by him that said unto him: The Lord hath sworn, and he will not repent, Thou art a priest for ever.)
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	22	By so much is Jesus made a surety of a better testament.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	23	And the others indeed were made many priests, because by reason of death they were not suffered to continue:
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	24	But this, for that he continueth for ever, hath an everlasting priesthood,
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	25	Whereby he is able also to save for ever them that come to God by him; always living to make intercession for us.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	26	For it was fitting that we should have such a high priest, holy, innocent, undefiled, separated from sinners, and made higher than the heavens;
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	27	Who needeth not daily (as the other priests) to offer sacrifices first for his own sins, and then for the people's: for this he did once, in offering himself.
Hebrews	Heb	65	7	28	For the law maketh men priests, who have infirmity: but the word of the oath, which was since the law, the Son who is perfected for evermore.
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	1	Now of the things which we have spoken, this is the sum: We have such an high priest, who is set on the right hand of the throne of majesty in the heavens,
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	2	A minister of the holies, and of the true tabernacle, which the Lord hath pitched, and not man.
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	3	For every high priest is appointed to offer gifts and sacrifices: wherefore it is necessary that he also should have some thing to offer.
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	4	If then he were on earth, he would not be a priest: seeing that there would be others to offer gifts according to the law,
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	5	Who serve unto the example and shadow of heavenly things. As it was answered to Moses, when he was to finish the tabernacle: See (saith he) that thou make all things according to the pattern which was shewn thee on the mount.
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	6	But now he hath obtained a better ministry, by how much also he is a mediator of a better testament, which is established on better promises.
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	7	For if that former had been faultless, there should not indeed a place have been sought for a second.
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	8	For finding fault with them, he saith: Behold, the days shall come, saith the Lord: and I will perfect unto the house of Israel, and unto the house of Juda, a new testament:
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	9	Not according to the testament which I made to their fathers, on the day when I took them by the hand to lead them out of the land of Egypt: because they continued not in my testament: and I regarded them not, saith the Lord.
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	10	For this is the testament which I will make to the house of Israel after those days, saith the Lord: I will give my laws into their mind, and in their heart will I write them: and I will be their God, and they shall be my people:
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	11	And they shall not teach every man his neighbour and every man his brother, saying, Know the Lord: for all shall know me from the least to the greatest of them:
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	12	Because I will be merciful to their iniquities, and their sins I will remember no more.
Hebrews	Heb	65	8	13	Now in saying a new, he hath made the former old. And that which decayeth and groweth old, is near its end.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	1	The former indeed had also justifications of divine service, and a worldly sanctuary.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	2	For there was a tabernacle made the first, wherein were the candlesticks, and the table, and the setting forth of loaves, which is called the holy.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	3	And after the second veil, the tabernacle, which is called the holy of holies:
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	4	Having a golden censer, and the ark of the testament covered about on every part with gold, in which was a golden pot that had manna, and the rod of Aaron, that had blossomed, and the tables of the testament.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	5	And over it were the cherubims of glory overshadowing the propitiatory: of which it is not needful to speak now particularly.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	6	Now these things being thus ordered, into the first tabernacle the priests indeed always entered, accomplishing the offices of sacrifices.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	7	But into the second, the high priest alone, once a year: not without blood, which he offereth for his own, and the people's ignorance:
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	8	The Holy Ghost signifying this, that the way into the holies was not yet made manifest, whilst the former tabernacle was yet standing.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	9	Which is a parable of the time present: according to which gifts and sacrifices are offered, which can not, as to the conscience, make him perfect that serveth, only in meats and in drinks,
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	10	And divers washings, and justices of the flesh laid on them until the time of correction.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	11	But Christ, being come an high priest of the good things to come, by a greater and more perfect tabernacle not made with hand, that is, not of this creation:
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	12	Neither by the blood of goats, or of calves, but by his own blood, entered once into the holies, having obtained eternal redemption.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	13	For if the blood of goats and of oxen, and the ashes of an heifer being sprinkled, sanctify such as are defiled, to the cleansing of the flesh:
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	14	How much more shall the blood of Christ, who by the Holy Ghost offered himself unspotted unto God, cleanse our conscience from dead works, to serve the living God?
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	15	And therefore he is the mediator of the new testament: that by means of his death, for the redemption of those transgressions, which were under the former testament, they that are called may receive the promise of eternal inheritance.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	16	For where there is a testament, the death of the testator must of necessity come in.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	17	For a testament is of force, after men are dead: otherwise it is as yet of no strength, whilst the testator liveth.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	18	Whereupon neither was the first indeed dedicated without blood.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	19	For when every commandment of the law had been read by Moses to all the people, he took the blood of calves and goats, with water, and scarlet wool and hyssop, and sprinkled both the book itself and all the people,
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	20	Saying: This is the blood of the testament, which God hath enjoined unto you.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	21	The tabernacle also and all the vessels of the ministry, in like manner, he sprinkled with blood.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	22	And almost all things, according to the law, are cleansed with blood: and without shedding of blood there is no remission.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	23	It is necessary therefore that the patterns of heavenly things should be cleansed with these: but the heavenly things themselves with better sacrifices than these.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	24	For Jesus is not entered into the holies made with hands, the patterns of the true: but into heaven itself, that he may appear now in the presence of God for us.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	25	Nor yet that he should offer himself often, as the high priest entereth into the holies, every year with the blood of others:
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	26	For then he ought to have suffered often from the beginning of the world: but now once at the end of ages, he hath appeared for the destruction of sin, by the sacrifice of himself.
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	27	And as it is appointed unto men once to die, and after this the judgment:
Hebrews	Heb	65	9	28	So also Christ was offered once to exhaust the sins of many; the second time he shall appear without sin to them that expect him unto salvation.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	1	For the law having a shadow of the good things to come, not the very image of the things; by the selfsame sacrifices which they offer continually every year, can never make the comers thereunto perfect:
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	2	For then they would have ceased to be offered: because the worshippers once cleansed should have no conscience of sin any longer:
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	3	But in them there is made a commemoration of sins every year.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	4	For it is impossible that with the blood of oxen and goats sin should be taken away.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	5	Wherefore when he cometh into the world, he saith: Sacrifice and oblation thou wouldest not: but a body thou hast fitted to me:
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	6	Holocausts for sin did not please thee.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	7	Then said I: Behold I come: in the head of the book it is written of me: that I should do thy will, O God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	8	In saying before, Sacrifices, and oblations, and holocausts for sin thou wouldest not, neither are they pleasing to thee, which are offered according to the law.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	9	Then said I: Behold, I come to do thy will, O God: he taketh away the first, that he may establish that which followeth.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	10	In the which will, we are sanctified by the oblation of the body of Jesus Christ once.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	11	And every priest indeed standeth daily ministering, and often offering the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	12	But this man offering one sacrifice for sins, for ever sitteth on the right hand of God,
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	13	From henceforth expecting, until his enemies be made his footstool.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	14	For by one oblation he hath perfected for ever them that are sanctified.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	15	And the Holy Ghost also doth testify this to us. For after that he said:
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	16	And this is the testament which I will make unto them after those days, saith the Lord. I will give my laws in their hearts, and on their minds will I write them:
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	17	And their sins and iniquities I will remember no more.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	18	Now where there is a remission of these, there is no more an oblation for sin.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	19	Having therefore, brethren, a confidence in the entering into the holies by the blood of Christ;
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	20	A new and living way which he hath dedicated for us through the veil, that is to say, his flesh,
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	21	And a high priest over the house of God:
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	22	Let us draw near with a true heart in fulness of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience, and our bodies washed with clean water.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	23	Let us hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering (for he is faithful that hath promised),
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	24	And let us consider one another, to provoke unto charity and to good works:
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	25	Not forsaking our assembly, as some are accustomed; but comforting one another, and so much the more as you see the day approaching.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	26	For if we sin wilfully after having the knowledge of the truth, there is now left no sacrifice for sins,
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	27	But a certain dreadful expectation of judgment, and the rage of a fire which shall consume the adversaries.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	28	A man making void the law of Moses, dieth without any mercy under two or three witnesses:
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	29	How much more, do you think he deserveth worse punishments, who hath trodden under foot the Son of God, and hath esteemed the blood of the testament unclean, by which he was sanctified, and hath offered an affront to the Spirit of grace?
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	30	For we know him that hath said: Vengeance belongeth to me, and I will repay. And again: The Lord shall judge his people.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	31	It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	32	But call to mind the former days, wherein, being illuminated, you endured a great fight of afflictions.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	33	And on the one hand indeed, by reproaches and tribulations, were made a gazingstock; and on the other, became companions of them that were used in such sort.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	34	For you both had compassion on them that were in bands, and took with joy the being stripped of your own goods, knowing that you have a better and a lasting substance.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	35	Do not therefore lose your confidence, which hath a great reward.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	36	For patience is necessary for you; that, doing the will of God, you may receive the promise.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	37	For yet a little and a very little while, and he that is to come, will come, and will not delay.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	38	But my just man liveth by faith; but if he withdraw himself, he shall not please my soul.
Hebrews	Heb	65	10	39	But we are not the children of withdrawing unto perdition, but of faith to the saving of the soul.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	1	Now faith is the substance of things to be hoped for, the evidence of things that appear not.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	2	For by this the ancients obtained a testimony.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	3	By faith we understand that the world was framed by the word of God; that from invisible things visible things might be made.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	4	By faith Abel offered to God a sacrifice exceeding that of Cain, by which he obtained a testimony that he was just, God giving testimony to his gifts; and by it he being dead yet speaketh.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	5	By faith Henoch was translated, that he should not see death; and he was not found, because God had translated him: for before his translation he had testimony that he pleased God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	6	But without faith it is impossible to please God. For he that cometh to God, must believe that he is, and is a rewarder to them that seek him.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	7	By faith Noe, having received an answer concerning those things which as yet were not seen, moved with fear, framed the ark for the saving of his house, by the which he condemned the world; and was instituted heir of the justice which is by faith.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	8	By faith he that is called Abraham, obeyed to go out into a place which he was to receive for an inheritance; and he went out, not knowing whither he went.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	9	By faith he abode in the land, dwelling in cottages, with Isaac and Jacob, the co-heirs of the same promise.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	10	For he looked for a city that hath foundations; whose builder and maker is God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	11	By faith also Sara herself, being barren, received strength to conceive seed, even past the time of age; because she believed that he was faithful who had promised,
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	12	For which cause there sprung even from one (and him as good as dead) as the stars of heaven in multitude, and as the sand which is by the sea shore innumerable.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	13	All these died according to faith, not having received the promises, but beholding them afar off, and saluting them, and confessing that they are pilgrims and strangers on the earth.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	14	For they that say these things, do signify that they seek a country.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	15	And truly if they had been mindful of that from whence they came out, they had doubtless time to return.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	16	But now they desire a better, that is to say, a heavenly country. Therefore God is not ashamed to be called their God; for he hath prepared for them a city.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	17	By faith Abraham, when he was tried, offered Isaac: and he that had received the promises, offered up his only begotten son;
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	18	(To whom it was said: In Isaac shall thy seed be called.)
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	19	Accounting that God is able to raise up even from the dead. Whereupon also he received him for a parable.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	20	By faith also of things to come, Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	21	By faith Jacob dying, blessed each of the sons of Joseph, and adored the top of his rod.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	22	By faith Joseph, when he was dying, made mention of the going out of the children of Israel; and gave commandment concerning his bones.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	23	By faith Moses, when he was born, was hid three months by his parents; because they saw he was a comely babe, and they feared not the king's edict.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	24	By faith Moses, when he was grown up, denied himself to be the son of Pharao's daughter;
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	25	Rather choosing to be afflicted with the people of God, than to have the pleasure of sin for a time,
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	26	Esteeming the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasure of the Egyptians. For he looked unto the reward.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	27	By faith he left Egypt, not fearing the fierceness of the king: for he endured as seeing him that is invisible.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	28	By faith he celebrated the pasch, and the shedding of the blood; that he, who destroyed the firstborn, might not touch them.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	29	By faith they passed through the Red Sea, as by dry land: which the Egyptians attempting, were swallowed up.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	30	By faith the walls of Jericho fell down, by the going round them seven days.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	31	By faith Rahab the harlot perished not with the unbelievers, receiving the spies with peace.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	32	And what shall I yet say? For the time would fail me to tell of Gedeon, Barac, Samson, Jephthe, David, Samuel, and the prophets:
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	33	Who by faith conquered kingdoms, wrought justice, obtained promises, stopped the mouths of lions,
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	34	Quenched the violence of fire, escaped the edge of the sword, recovered strength from weakness, became valiant in battle, put to flight the armies of foreigners:
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	35	Women received their dead raised to life again. But others were racked, not accepting deliverance, that they might find a better resurrection.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	36	And others had trial of mockeries and stripes, moreover also of bands and prisons.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	37	They were stoned, they were cut asunder, they were tempted, they were put to death by the sword, they wandered about in sheepskins, in goatskins, being in want, distressed, afflicted:
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	38	Of whom the world was not worthy; wandering in deserts, in mountains, and in dens, and in caves of the earth.
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	39	And all these being approved by the testimony of faith, received not the promise;
Hebrews	Heb	65	11	40	God providing some better thing for us, that they should not be perfected without us.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	1	And therefore we also having so great a cloud of witnesses over our head, laying aside every weight and sin which surrounds us, let us run by patience to the fight proposed to us:
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	2	Looking on Jesus, the author and finisher of faith, who having joy set before him, endured the cross, despising the shame, and now sitteth on the right hand of the throne of God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	3	For think diligently upon him that endured such opposition from sinners against himself; that you be not wearied, fainting in your minds.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	4	For you have not yet resisted unto blood, striving against sin:
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	5	And you have forgotten the consolation, which speaketh to you, as unto children, saying: My son, neglect not the discipline of the Lord; neither be thou wearied whilst thou art rebuked by him.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	6	For whom the Lord loveth, he chastiseth; and he scourgeth every son whom he receiveth.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	7	Persevere under discipline. God dealeth with you as with his sons; for what son is there, whom the father doth not correct?
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	8	But if you be without chastisement, whereof all are made partakers, then are you bastards, and not sons.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	9	Moreover we have had fathers of our flesh, for instructors, and we reverenced them: shall we not much more obey the Father of spirits, and live?
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	10	And they indeed for a few days, according to their own pleasure, instructed us: but he, for our profit, that we might receive his sanctification.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	11	Now all chastisement for the present indeed seemeth not to bring with it joy, but sorrow: but afterwards it will yield, to them that are exercised by it, the most peaceable fruit of justice.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	12	Wherefore lift up the hands which hang down, and the feeble knees,
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	13	And make straight steps with your feet: that no one, halting, may go out of the way; but rather be healed.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	14	Follow peace with all men, and holiness: without which no man shall see God.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	15	Looking diligently, lest any man be wanting to the grace of God; lest any root of bitterness springing up do hinder, and by it many be defiled.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	16	Lest there be any fornicator, or profane person, as Esau; who for one mess, sold his first birthright.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	17	For know ye that afterwards, when he desired to inherit the benediction, he was rejected; for he found no place of repentance, although with tears he had sought it.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	18	For you are not come to a mountain that might be touched, and a burning fire, and a whirlwind, and darkness, and storm,
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	19	And the sound of a trumpet, and the voice of words, which they that heard excused themselves, that the word might not be spoken to them:
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	20	For they did not endure that which was said: And if so much as a beast shall touch the mount, it shall be stoned.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	21	And so terrible was that which was seen, Moses said: I am frighted, and tremble.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	22	But you are come to mount Sion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and to the company of many thousands of angels,
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	23	And to the church of the firstborn, who are written in the heavens, and to God the judge of all, and to the spirits of the just made perfect,
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	24	And to Jesus the mediator of the new testament, and to the sprinkling of blood which speaketh better than that of Abel.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	25	See that you refuse him not that speaketh. For if they escaped not who refused him that spoke upon the earth, much more shall not we, that turn away from him that speaketh to us from heaven.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	26	Whose voice then moved the earth; but now he promiseth, saying: Yet once more, and I will move not only the earth, but heaven also.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	27	And in that he saith, Yet once more, he signifieth the translation of the moveable things as made, that those things may remain which are immoveable.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	28	Therefore receiving an immoveable kingdom, we have grace; whereby let us serve, pleasing God, with fear and reverence.
Hebrews	Heb	65	12	29	For our God is a consuming fire.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	1	Let the charity of the brotherhood abide in you.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	2	And hospitality do not forget; for by this some, being not aware of it, have entertained angels.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	3	Remember them that are in bands, as if you were bound with them; and them that labour, as being yourselves also in the body.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	4	Marriage honourable in all, and the bed undefiled. For fornicators and adulterers God will judge.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	5	Let your manners be without covetousness, contented with such things as you have; for he hath said: I will not leave thee, neither will I forsake thee.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	6	So that we may confidently say: The Lord is my helper: I will not fear what man shall do to me.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	7	Remember your prelates who have spoken the word of God to you; whose faith follow, considering the end of their conversation,
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	8	Jesus Christ, yesterday, and today; and the same for ever.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	9	Be not led away with various and strange doctrines. For it is best that the heart be established with grace, not with meats; which have not profited those that walk in them.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	10	We have an altar, whereof they have no power to eat who serve the tabernacle.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	11	For the bodies of those beasts, whose blood is brought into the holies by the high priest for sin, are burned without the camp.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	12	Wherefore Jesus also, that he might sanctify the people by his own blood, suffered without the gate.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	13	Let us go forth therefore to him without the camp, bearing his reproach.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	14	For we have not here a lasting city, but we seek one that is to come.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	15	By him therefore let us offer the sacrifice of praise always to God, that is to say, the fruit of lips confessing to his name.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	16	And do not forget to do good, and to impart; for by such sacrifices God's favour is obtained.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	17	Obey your prelates, and be subject to them. For they watch as being to render an account of your souls; that they may do this with joy, and not with grief. For this is not expedient for you.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	18	Pray for us. For we trust we have a good conscience, being willing to behave ourselves well in all things.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	19	And I beseech you the more to do this, that I may be restored to you the sooner.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	20	And may the God of peace, who brought again from the dead the great pastor of the sheep, our Lord Jesus Christ, in the blood of the everlasting testament,
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	21	Fit you in all goodness, that you may do his will; doing in you that which is well pleasing in his sight, through Jesus Christ, to whom is glory for ever and ever. Amen.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	22	And I beseech you, brethren, that you suffer this word of consolation. For I have written to you in a few words.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	23	Know ye that our brother Timothy is set at liberty: with whom (if he come shortly) I will see you.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	24	Salute all your prelates, and all the saints. The brethren from Italy salute you.
Hebrews	Heb	65	13	25	Grace be with you all. Amen.
James	Jas	66	1	1	James the servant of God, and of our Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, greeting.
James	Jas	66	1	2	My brethren, count it all joy, when you shall fall into divers temptations;
James	Jas	66	1	3	Knowing that the trying of your faith worketh patience.
James	Jas	66	1	4	And patience hath a perfect work; that you may be perfect and entire, failing in nothing.
James	Jas	66	1	5	But if any of you want wisdom, let him ask of God, who giveth to all men abundantly, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him.
James	Jas	66	1	6	But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea, which is moved and carried about by the wind.
James	Jas	66	1	7	Therefore let not that man think that he shall receive any thing of the Lord.
James	Jas	66	1	8	A double minded man is inconstant in all his ways.
James	Jas	66	1	9	But let the brother of low condition glory in his exaltation:
James	Jas	66	1	10	And the rich, in his being low; because as the flower of the grass shall he pass away.
James	Jas	66	1	11	For the sun rose with a burning heat, and parched the grass, and the flower thereof fell off, and the beauty of the shape thereof perished: so also shall the rich man fade away in his ways.
James	Jas	66	1	12	Blessed is the man that endureth temptation; for when he hath been proved, he shall receive a crown of life, which God hath promised to them that love him.
James	Jas	66	1	13	Let no man, when he is tempted, say that he is tempted by God. For God is not a tempter of evils, and he tempteth no man.
James	Jas	66	1	14	But every man is tempted by his own concupiscence, being drawn away and allured.
James	Jas	66	1	15	Then when concupiscence hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin. But sin, when it is completed, begetteth death.
James	Jas	66	1	16	Do not err, therefore, my dearest brethren.
James	Jas	66	1	17	Every best gift, and every perfect gift, is from above, coming down from the Father of lights, with whom there is no change, nor shadow of alteration.
James	Jas	66	1	18	For of his own will hath he begotten us by the word of truth, that we might be some beginning of his creatures.
James	Jas	66	1	19	You know, my dearest brethren. And let every man be swift to hear, but slow to speak, and slow to anger.
James	Jas	66	1	20	For the anger of man worketh not the justice of God.
James	Jas	66	1	21	Wherefore casting away all uncleanness, and abundance of naughtiness, with meekness receive the ingrafted word, which is able to save your souls.
James	Jas	66	1	22	But be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving your own selves.
James	Jas	66	1	23	For if a man be a hearer of the word, and not a doer, he shall be compared to a man beholding his own countenance in a glass.
James	Jas	66	1	24	For he beheld himself, and went his way, and presently forgot what manner of man he was.
James	Jas	66	1	25	But he that hath looked into the perfect law of liberty, and hath continued therein, not becoming a forgetful hearer, but a doer of the work; this man shall be blessed in his deed.
James	Jas	66	1	26	And if any man think himself to be religious, not bridling his tongue, but deceiving his own heart, this man's religion is vain.
James	Jas	66	1	27	Religion clean and undefiled before God and the Father, is this: to visit the fatherless and widows in their tribulation: and to keep one's self unspotted from this world.
James	Jas	66	2	1	My brethren, have not the faith of our Lord Jesus Christ of glory with respect of persons.
James	Jas	66	2	2	For if there shall come into your assembly a man having a golden ring, in fine apparel, and there shall come in also a poor man in mean attire,
James	Jas	66	2	3	And you have respect to him that is clothed with the fine apparel, and shall say to him: Sit thou here well; but say to the poor man: Stand thou there, or sit under my footstool:
James	Jas	66	2	4	Do you not judge within yourselves, and are become judges of unjust thoughts?
James	Jas	66	2	5	Hearken, my dearest brethren: hath not God chosen the poor in this world, rich in faith, and heirs of the kingdom which God hath promised to them that love him?
James	Jas	66	2	6	But you have dishonoured the poor man. Do not the rich oppress you by might? and do not they draw you before the judgment seats?
James	Jas	66	2	7	Do not they blaspheme the good name that is invoked upon you?
James	Jas	66	2	8	If then you fulfill the royal law, according to the scriptures, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself; you do well.
James	Jas	66	2	9	But if you have respect to persons, you commit sin, being reproved by the law as transgressors.
James	Jas	66	2	10	And whosoever shall keep the whole law, but offend in one point, is become guilty of all.
James	Jas	66	2	11	For he that said, Thou shalt not commit adultery, said also, Thou shalt not kill. Now if thou do not commit adultery, but shalt kill, thou art become a transgressor of the law.
James	Jas	66	2	12	So speak ye, and so do, as being to be judged by the law of liberty.
James	Jas	66	2	13	For judgment without mercy to him that hath not done mercy. And mercy exalteth itself above judgment.
James	Jas	66	2	14	What shall it profit, my brethren, if a man say he hath faith, but hath not works? Shall faith be able to save him?
James	Jas	66	2	15	And if a brother or sister be naked, and want daily food:
James	Jas	66	2	16	And one of you say to them: Go in peace, be ye warmed and filled; yet give them not those things that are necessary for the body, what shall it profit?
James	Jas	66	2	17	So faith also, if it have not works, is dead in itself.
James	Jas	66	2	18	But some man will say: Thou hast faith, and I have works: shew me thy faith without works; and I will shew thee, by works, my faith.
James	Jas	66	2	19	Thou believest that there is one God. Thou dost well: the devils also believe and tremble.
James	Jas	66	2	20	But wilt thou know, O vain man, that faith without works is dead?
James	Jas	66	2	21	Was not Abraham our father justified by works, offering up Isaac his son upon the altar?
James	Jas	66	2	22	Seest thou, that faith did co-operate with his works; and by works faith was made perfect?
James	Jas	66	2	23	And the scripture was fulfilled, saying: Abraham believed God, and it was reputed to him to justice, and he was called the friend of God.
James	Jas	66	2	24	Do you see that by works a man is justified; and not by faith only?
James	Jas	66	2	25	And in like manner also Rahab the harlot, was not she justified by works, receiving the messengers, and sending them out another way?
James	Jas	66	2	26	For even as the body without the spirit is dead; so also faith without works is dead.
James	Jas	66	3	1	Be ye not many masters, my brethren, knowing that you receive the greater judgment.
James	Jas	66	3	2	For in many things we all offend. If any man offend not in word, the same is a perfect man. He is able also with a bridle to lead about the whole body.
James	Jas	66	3	3	For if we put bits into the mouths of horses, that they may obey us, and we turn about their whole body.
James	Jas	66	3	4	Behold also ships, whereas they are great, and are driven by strong winds, yet are they turned about with a small helm, whithersoever the force of the governor willeth.
James	Jas	66	3	5	Even so the tongue is indeed a little member, and boasteth great things. Behold how small a fire kindleth a great wood.
James	Jas	66	3	6	And the tongue is a fire, a world of iniquity. The tongue is placed among our members, which defileth the whole body, and inflameth the wheel of our nativity, being set on fire by hell.
James	Jas	66	3	7	For every nature of beasts, and of birds, and of serpents, and of the rest, is tamed, and hath been tamed, by the nature of man:
James	Jas	66	3	8	But the tongue no man can tame, an unquiet evil, full of deadly poison.
James	Jas	66	3	9	By it we bless God and the Father: and by it we curse men, who are made after the likeness of God.
James	Jas	66	3	10	Out of the same mouth proceedeth blessing and cursing. My brethren, these things ought not so to be.
James	Jas	66	3	11	Doth a fountain send forth, out of the same hole, sweet and bitter water?
James	Jas	66	3	12	Can the fig tree, my brethren, bear grapes; or the vine, figs? So neither can the salt water yield sweet.
James	Jas	66	3	13	Who is a wise man, and endued with knowledge among you? Let him shew, by a good conversation, his work in the meekness of wisdom.
James	Jas	66	3	14	But if you have bitter zeal, and there be contentions in your hearts; glory not, and be not liars against the truth.
James	Jas	66	3	15	For this is not wisdom, descending from above: but earthly, sensual, devilish.
James	Jas	66	3	16	For where envying and contention is, there is inconstancy, and every evil work.
James	Jas	66	3	17	But the wisdom, that is from above, first indeed is chaste, then peaceable, modest, easy to be persuaded, consenting to the good, full of mercy and good fruits, without judging, without dissimulation.
James	Jas	66	3	18	And the fruit of justice is sown in peace, to them that make peace.
James	Jas	66	4	1	From whence are wars and contentions among you? Are they not hence, from your concupiscences, which war in your members?
James	Jas	66	4	2	You covet, and have not: you kill, and envy, and can not obtain. You contend and war, and you have not, because you ask not.
James	Jas	66	4	3	You ask, and receive not; because you ask amiss: that you may consume it on your concupiscences.
James	Jas	66	4	4	Adulterers, know you not that the friendship of this world is the enemy of God? Whosoever therefore will be a friend of this world, becometh an enemy of God.
James	Jas	66	4	5	Or do you think that the scripture saith in vain: To envy doth the spirit covet which dwelleth in you?
James	Jas	66	4	6	But he giveth greater grace. Wherefore he saith: God resisteth the proud, and giveth grace to the humble.
James	Jas	66	4	7	Be subject therefore to God, but resist the devil, and he will fly from you.
James	Jas	66	4	8	Draw nigh to God, and he will draw nigh to you. Cleanse your hands, ye sinners: and purify your hearts, ye double minded.
James	Jas	66	4	9	Be afflicted, and mourn, and weep: let your laughter be turned into mourning, and your joy into sorrow.
James	Jas	66	4	10	Be humbled in the sight of the Lord, and he will exalt you.
James	Jas	66	4	11	Detract not one another, my brethren. He that detracteth his brother, or he that judgeth his brother, detracteth the law, and judgeth the law. But if thou judge the law, thou art not a doer of the law, but a judge.
James	Jas	66	4	12	There is one lawgiver, and judge, that is able to destroy and to deliver.
James	Jas	66	4	13	But who art thou that judgest thy neighbour? Behold, now you that say: Today or tomorrow we will go into such a city, and there we will spend a year, and will traffic, and make our gain.
James	Jas	66	4	14	Whereas you know not what shall be on the morrow.
James	Jas	66	4	15	For what is your life? It is a vapour which appeareth for a little while, and afterwards shall vanish away. For that you should say: If the Lord will, and if we shall live, we will do this or that.
James	Jas	66	4	16	But now you rejoice in your arrogancies. All such rejoicing is wicked.
James	Jas	66	4	17	To him therefore who knoweth to do good, and doth it not, to him it is sin.
James	Jas	66	5	1	Go to now, ye rich men, weep and howl in your miseries, which shall come upon you.
James	Jas	66	5	2	Your riches are corrupted: and your garments are motheaten.
James	Jas	66	5	3	Your gold and silver is cankered: and the rust of them shall be for a testimony against you, and shall eat your flesh like fire. You have stored up to yourselves wrath against the last days.
James	Jas	66	5	4	Behold the hire of the labourers, who have reaped down your fields, which by fraud has been kept back by you, crieth: and the cry of them hath entered into the ears of the Lord of Sabaoth.
James	Jas	66	5	5	You have feasted upon earth: and in riotousness you have nourished your hearts, in the day of slaughter.
James	Jas	66	5	6	You have condemned and put to death the Just One, and he resisted you not.
James	Jas	66	5	7	Be patient therefore, brethren, until the coming of the Lord. Behold, the husbandman waiteth for the precious fruit of the earth: patiently bearing till he receive the early and latter rain.
James	Jas	66	5	8	Be you therefore also patient, and strengthen your hearts: for the coming of the Lord is at hand.
James	Jas	66	5	9	Grudge not, brethren, one against another, that you may not be judged. Behold the judge standeth before the door.
James	Jas	66	5	10	Take, my brethren, for an example of suffering evil, of labour and patience, the prophets, who spoke in the name of the Lord.
James	Jas	66	5	11	Behold, we account them blessed who have endured. You have heard of the patience of Job, and you have seen the end of the Lord, that the Lord is merciful and compassionate.
James	Jas	66	5	12	But above all things, my brethren, swear not, neither by heaven, nor by the earth, nor by any other oath. But let your speech be, yea, yea: no, no: that you fall not under judgment.
James	Jas	66	5	13	Is any of you sad? Let him pray. Is he cheerful in mind? Let him sing.
James	Jas	66	5	14	Is any man sick among you? Let him bring in the priests of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord.
James	Jas	66	5	15	And the prayer of faith shall save the sick man: and the Lord shall raise him up: and if he be in sins, they shall be forgiven him.
James	Jas	66	5	16	Confess therefore your sins one to another: and pray one for another, that you may be saved. For the continual prayer of a just man availeth much.
James	Jas	66	5	17	Elias was a man passible like unto us: and with prayer he prayed that it might not rain upon the earth, and it rained not for three years and six months.
James	Jas	66	5	18	And he prayed again: and the heaven gave rain, and the earth brought forth her fruit.
James	Jas	66	5	19	My brethren, if any of you err from the truth, and one convert him:
James	Jas	66	5	20	He must know that he who causeth a sinner to be converted from the error of his way, shall save his soul from death, and shall cover a multitude of sins.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	1	Peter, an apostle of Jesus Christ, to the strangers dispersed through Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia, elect,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	2	According to the foreknowledge of God the Father, unto the sanctification of the Spirit, unto obedience and sprinkling of the blood of Jesus Christ: Grace unto you and peace be multiplied.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	3	Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to his great mercy hath regenerated us unto a lively hope, by the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	4	Unto an inheritance incorruptible, and undefiled, and that can not fade, reserved in heaven for you,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	5	Who, by the power of God, are kept by faith unto salvation, ready to be revealed in the last time.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	6	Wherein you shall greatly rejoice, if now you must be for a little time made sorrowful in divers temptations:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	7	That the trial of your faith (much more precious than gold which is tried by the fire) may be found unto praise and glory and honour at the appearing of Jesus Christ:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	8	Whom having not seen, you love: in whom also now, though you see him not, you believe: and believing shall rejoice with joy unspeakable and glorified;
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	9	Receiving the end of your faith, even the salvation of your souls.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	10	Of which salvation the prophets have inquired and diligently searched, who prophesied of the grace to come in you.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	11	Searching what or what manner of time the Spirit of Christ in them did signify: when it foretold those sufferings that are in Christ, and the glories that should follow:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	12	To whom it was revealed, that not to themselves, but to you they ministered those things which are now declared to you by them that have preached the gospel to you, the Holy Ghost being sent down from heaven, on whom the angels desire to look.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	13	Wherefore having the loins of your mind girt up, being sober, trust perfectly in the grace which is offered you in the revelation of Jesus Christ,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	14	As children of obedience, not fashioned according to the former desires of your ignorance:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	15	But according to him that hath called you, who is holy, be you also in all manner of conversation holy:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	16	Because it is written: You shall be holy, for I am holy.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	17	And if you invoke as Father him who, without respect of persons, judgeth according to every one's work: converse in fear during the time of your sojourning here.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	18	Knowing that you were not redeemed with corruptible things as gold or silver, from your vain conversation of the tradition of your fathers:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	19	But with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb unspotted and undefiled,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	20	Foreknown indeed before the foundation of the world, but manifested in the last times for you,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	21	Who through him are faithful in God, who raised him up from the dead, and hath given him glory, that your faith and hope might be in God.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	22	Purifying your souls in the obedience of charity, with a brotherly love, from a sincere heart love one another earnestly:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	23	Being born again not of corruptible seed, but incorruptible, by the word of God who liveth and remaineth for ever.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	24	For all flesh is as grass; and all the glory thereof as the flower of grass. The grass is withered, and the flower thereof is fallen away.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	1	25	But the word of the Lord endureth for ever. And this is the word which by the gospel hath been preached unto you.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	1	Wherefore laying away all malice, and all guile, and dissimulations, and envies, and all detractions,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	2	As newborn babes, desire the rational milk without guile, that thereby you may grow unto salvation:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	3	If so be you have tasted that the Lord is sweet.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	4	Unto whom coming, as to a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen and made honourable by God:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	5	Be you also as living stones built up, a spiritual house, a holy priesthood, to offer up spiritual sacrifices, acceptable to God by Jesus Christ.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	6	Wherefore it is said in the scripture: Behold, I lay in Sion a chief corner stone, elect, precious. And he that shall believe in him, shall not be confounded.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	7	To you therefore that believe, he is honour: but to them that believe not, the stone which the builders rejected, the same is made the head of the corner:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	8	And a stone of stumbling, and a rock of scandal, to them who stumble at the word, neither do believe, whereunto also they are set.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	9	But you are a chosen generation, a kingly priesthood, a holy nation, a purchased people: that you may declare his virtues, who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	10	Who in time past were not a people: but are now the people of God. Who had not obtained mercy; but now have obtained mercy.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	11	Dearly beloved, I beseech you as strangers and pilgrims, to refrain yourselves from carnal desires which war against the soul,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	12	Having your conversation good among the Gentiles: that whereas they speak against you as evildoers, they may, by the good works, which they shall behold in you, glorify God in the day of visitation.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	13	Be ye subject therefore to every human creature for God's sake: whether it be to the king as excelling;
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	14	Or to governors as sent by him for the punishment of evildoers, and for the praise of the good:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	15	For so is the will of God, that by doing well you may put to silence the ignorance of foolish men:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	16	As free, and not as making liberty a cloak for malice, but as the servants of God.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	17	Honour all men. Love the brotherhood. Fear God. Honour the king.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	18	Servants, be subject to your masters with all fear, not only to the good and gentle, but also to the froward.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	19	For this is thankworthy, if for conscience towards God, a man endure sorrows, suffering wrongfully.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	20	For what glory is it, if committing sin, and being buffeted for it, you endure? But if doing well you suffer patiently; this is thankworthy before God.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	21	For unto this are you called: because Christ also suffered for us, leaving you an example that you should follow his steps.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	22	Who did no sin, neither was guile found in his mouth.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	23	Who, when he was reviled, did not revile: when he suffered, he threatened not: but delivered himself to him that judged him unjustly.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	24	Who his own self bore our sins in his body upon the tree: that we, being dead to sins, should live to justice: by whose stripes you were healed.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	2	25	For you were as sheep going astray; but you are now converted to the shepherd and bishop of your souls.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	1	In like manner also let wives be subject to their husbands: that if any believe not the word, they may be won without the word, by the conversation of the wives.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	2	Considering your chaste conversation with fear.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	3	Whose adorning let it not be the outward plaiting of the hair, or the wearing of gold, or the putting on of apparel:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	4	But the hidden man of the heart in the incorruptibility of a quiet and a meek spirit, which is rich in the sight of God.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	5	For after this manner heretofore the holy women also, who trusted in God, adorned themselves, being in subjection to their own husbands:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	6	As Sara obeyed Abraham, calling him lord: whose daughters you are, doing well, and not fearing any disturbance.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	7	Ye husbands, likewise dwelling with them according to knowledge, giving honour to the female as to the weaker vessel, and as to the co-heirs of the grace of life: that your prayers be not hindered.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	8	And in fine, be ye all of one mind, having compassion one of another, being lovers of the brotherhood, merciful, modest, humble:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	9	Not rendering evil for evil, nor railing for railing, but contrariwise, blessing: for unto this are you called, that you may inherit a blessing.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	10	For he that will love life, and see good days, let him refrain his tongue from evil, and his lips that they speak no guile.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	11	Let him decline from evil, and do good: let him seek after peace and pursue it:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	12	Because the eyes of the Lord are upon the just, and his ears unto their prayers: but the countenance of the Lord upon them that do evil things.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	13	And who is he that can hurt you, if you be zealous of good?
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	14	But if also you suffer any thing for justice' sake, blessed are ye. And be not afraid of their fear, and be not troubled.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	15	But sanctify the Lord Christ in your hearts, being ready always to satisfy every one that asketh you a reason of that hope which is in you.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	16	But with modesty and fear, having a good conscience: that whereas they speak evil of you, they may be ashamed who falsely accuse your good conversation in Christ.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	17	For it is better doing well (if such be the will of God) to suffer, than doing ill.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	18	Because Christ also died once for our sins, the just for the unjust: that he might offer us to God, being put to death indeed in the flesh, but enlivened in the spirit,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	19	In which also coming he preached to those spirits that were in prison:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	20	Which had been some time incredulous, when they waited for the patience of God in the days of Noe, when the ark was a building: wherein a few, that is, eight souls, were saved by water.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	21	Whereunto baptism being of the like form, now saveth you also: not the putting away of the filth of the flesh, but the examination of a good conscience towards God by the resurrection of Jesus Christ.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	3	22	Who is on the right hand of God, swallowing down death, that we might be made heirs of life everlasting: being gone into heaven, the angels and powers and virtues being made subject to him.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	1	Christ therefore having suffered in the flesh, be you also armed with the same thought: for he that hath suffered in the flesh, hath ceased from sins:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	2	That now he may live the rest of his time in the flesh, not after the desires of men, but according to the will of God.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	3	For the time past is sufficient to have fulfilled the will of the Gentiles, for them who have walked in riotousness, lusts, excess of wine, revellings, banquetings, and unlawful worshipping of idols.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	4	Wherein they think it strange, that you run not with them into the same confusion of riotousness, speaking evil of you.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	5	Who shall render account to him, who is ready to judge the living and the dead.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	6	For, for this cause was the gospel preached also to the dead: that they might be judged indeed according to men, in the flesh; but may live according to God, in the Spirit.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	7	But the end of all is at hand. Be prudent therefore, and watch in prayers.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	8	But before all things have a constant mutual charity among yourselves: for charity covereth a multitude of sins.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	9	Using hospitality one towards another, without murmuring,
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	10	As every man hath received grace, ministering the same one to another: as good stewards of the manifold grace of God.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	11	If any man speak, let him speak, as the words of God. If any man minister, let him do it, as of the power, which God administereth: that in all things God may be honoured through Jesus Christ: to whom is glory and empire for ever and ever. Amen.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	12	Dearly beloved, think not strange the burning heat which is to try you, as if some new thing happened to you;
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	13	But if you partake of the sufferings of Christ, rejoice that when his glory shall be revealed, you may also be glad with exceeding joy.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	14	If you be reproached for the name of Christ, you shall be blessed: for that which is of the honour, glory, and power of God, and that which is his Spirit, resteth upon you.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	15	But let none of you suffer as a murderer, or a thief, or a railer, or a coveter of other men's things.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	16	But if as a Christian, let him not be ashamed, but let him glorify God in that name.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	17	For the time is, that judgment should begin at the house of God. And if first at us, what shall be the end of them that believe not the gospel of God?
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	18	And if the just man shall scarcely be saved, where shall the ungodly and the sinner appear?
1 Peter	1Pet	67	4	19	Wherefore let them also that suffer according to the will of God, commend their souls in good deeds to the faithful Creator.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	1	The ancients therefore that are among you, I beseech, who am myself also an ancient, and a witness of the sufferings of Christ: as also a partaker of that glory which is to be revealed in time to come:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	2	Feed the flock of God which is among you, taking care of it, not by constraint, but willingly, according to God: not for filthy lucre's sake, but voluntarily:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	3	Neither as lording it over the clergy, but being made a pattern of the flock from the heart.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	4	And when the prince of pastors shall appear, you shall receive a never fading crown of glory.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	5	In like manner, ye young men, be subject to the ancients. And do you all insinuate humility one to another, for God resisteth the proud, but to the humble he giveth grace.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	6	Be you humbled therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in the time of visitation:
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	7	Casting all your care upon him, for he hath care of you.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	8	Be sober and watch: because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, goeth about seeking whom he may devour.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	9	Whom resist ye, strong in faith: knowing that the same affliction befalls your brethren who are in the world.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	10	But the God of all grace, who hath called us into his eternal glory in Christ Jesus, after you have suffered a little, will himself perfect you, and confirm you, and establish you.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	11	To him be glory and empire for ever and ever. Amen.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	12	By Sylvanus, a faithful brother unto you, as I think, I have written briefly: beseeching and testifying that this is the true grace of God, wherein you stand.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	13	The church that is in Babylon, elected together with you, saluteth you: and so doth my son Mark.
1 Peter	1Pet	67	5	14	Salute one another with a holy kiss. Grace be to all you, who are in Christ Jesus. Amen.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	1	Simon Peter, servant and apostle of Jesus Christ, to them that have obtained equal faith with us in the justice of our God and Saviour Jesus Christ.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	2	Grace to you and peace be accomplished in the knowledge of God and of Christ Jesus our Lord:
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	3	As all things of his divine power which appertain to life and godliness, are given us, through the knowledge of him who hath called us by his own proper glory and virtue.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	4	By whom he hath given us most great and precious promises: that by these you may be made partakers of the divine nature: flying the corruption of that concupiscence which is in the world.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	5	And you, employing all care, minister in your faith, virtue; and in virtue, knowledge;
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	6	And in knowledge, abstinence; and in abstinence, patience; and in patience, godliness;
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	7	And in godliness, love of brotherhood; and in love of brotherhood, charity.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	8	For if these things be with you and abound, they will make you to be neither empty nor unfruitful in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	9	For he that hath not these things with him, is blind, and groping, having forgotten that he was purged from his old sins.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	10	Wherefore, brethren, labour the more, that by good works you may make sure your calling and election. For doing these things, you shall not sin at any time.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	11	For so an entrance shall be ministered to you abundantly into the everlasting kingdom of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	12	For which cause I will begin to put you always in remembrance of these things: though indeed you know them, and are confirmed in the present truth.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	13	But I think it meet as long as I am in this tabernacle, to stir you up by putting you in remembrance.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	14	Being assured that the laying away of this my tabernacle is at hand, according as our Lord Jesus Christ also hath signified to me.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	15	And I will endeavour, that you frequently have after my decease, whereby you may keep a memory of these things.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	16	For we have not by following artificial fables, made known to you the power, and presence of our Lord Jesus Christ; but we were eyewitnesses of his greatness.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	17	For he received from God the Father, honour and glory: this voice coming down to him from the excellent glory: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear ye him.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	18	And this voice we heard brought from heaven, when we were with him in the holy mount.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	19	And we have the more firm prophetical word: whereunto you do well to attend, as to a light that shineth in a dark place, until the day dawn, and the day star arise in your hearts:
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	20	Understanding this first, that no prophecy of scripture is made by private interpretation.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	1	21	For prophecy came not by the will of man at any time: but the holy men of God spoke, inspired by the Holy Ghost.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	1	But there were also false prophets among the people, even as there shall be among you lying teachers, who shall bring in sects of perdition, and deny the Lord who bought them: bringing upon themselves swift destruction.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	2	And many shall follow their riotousnesses, through whom the way of truth shall be evil spoken of.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	3	And through covetousness shall they with feigned words make merchandise of you. Whose judgment now of a long time lingereth not, and their perdition slumbereth not.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	4	For if God spared not the angels that sinned, but delivered them, drawn down by infernal ropes to the lower hell, unto torments, to be reserved unto judgment:
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	5	And spared not the original world, but preserved Noe, the eighth person, the preacher of justice, bringing in the flood upon the world of the ungodly.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	6	And reducing the cities of the Sodomites, and of the Gomorrhites, into ashes, condemned them to be overthrown, making them an example to those that should after act wickedly.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	7	And delivered just Lot, oppressed by the injustice and lewd conversation of the wicked.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	8	For in sight and hearing he was just: dwelling among them, who from day to day vexed the just soul with unjust works.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	9	The Lord knoweth how to deliver the godly from temptation, but to reserve the unjust unto the day of judgment to be tormented.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	10	And especially them who walk after the flesh in the lust of uncleanness, and despise government, audacious, self willed, they fear not to bring in sects, blaspheming.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	11	Whereas angels who are greater in strength and power, bring not against themselves a railing judgment.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	12	But these men, as irrational beasts, naturally tending to the snare and to destruction, blaspheming those things which they know not, shall perish in their corruption,
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	13	Receiving the reward of their injustice, counting for a pleasure the delights of a day: stains and spots, sporting themselves to excess, rioting in their feasts with you:
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	14	Having eyes full of adultery and of sin that ceaseth not: alluring unstable souls, having their heart exercised with covetousness, children of malediction:
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	15	Leaving the right way they have gone astray, having followed the way of Balaam of Bosor, who loved the wages of iniquity,
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	16	But had a check of his madness, the dumb beast used to the yoke, which speaking with man's voice, forbade the folly of the prophet.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	17	These are fountains without water, and clouds tossed with whirlwinds, to whom the mist of darkness is reserved.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	18	For, speaking proud words of vanity, they allure by the desires of fleshly riotousness, those who for a little while escape, such as converse in error:
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	19	Promising them liberty, whereas they themselves are the slaves of corruption. For by whom a man is overcome, of the same also he is the slave.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	20	For if, flying from the pollutions of the world, through the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ, they be again entangled in them and overcome: their latter state is become unto them worse than the former.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	21	For it had been better for them not to have known the way of justice, than after they have known it, to turn back from that holy commandment which was delivered to them.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	2	22	For, that of the true proverb has happened to them: The dog is returned to his vomit: and, The sow that was washed, to her wallowing in the mire.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	1	Behold this second epistle I write to you, my dearly beloved, in which I stir up by way of admonition your sincere mind:
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	2	That you may be mindful of those words which I told you before from the holy prophets, and of your apostles, of the precepts of the Lord and Saviour.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	3	Knowing this first, that in the last days there shall come deceitful scoffers, walking after their own lusts,
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	4	Saying: Where is his promise or his coming? for since the time that the fathers slept, all things continue as they were from the beginning of the creation.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	5	For this they are wilfully ignorant of, that the heavens were before, and the earth out of water, and through water, consisting by the word of God.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	6	Whereby the world that then was, being overflowed with water, perished.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	7	But the heavens and the earth which are now, by the same word are kept in store, reserved unto fire against the day of judgment and perdition of the ungodly men.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	8	But of this one thing be not ignorant, my beloved, that one day with the Lord is as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	9	The Lord delayeth not his promise, as some imagine, but dealeth patiently for your sake, not willing that any should perish, but that all should return to penance.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	10	But the day of the Lord shall come as a thief, in which the heavens shall pass away with great violence, and the elements shall be melted with heat, and the earth and the works which are in it, shall be burnt up.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	11	Seeing then that all these things are to be dissolved, what manner of people ought you to be in holy conversation and godliness?
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	12	Looking for and hasting unto the coming of the day of the Lord, by which the heavens being on fire shall be dissolved, and the elements shall melt with the burning heat?
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	13	But we look for new heavens and a new earth according to his promises, in which justice dwelleth.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	14	Wherefore, dearly beloved, waiting for these things, be diligent that you may be found before him unspotted and blameless in peace.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	15	And account the longsuffering of our Lord, salvation; as also our most dear brother Paul, according to the wisdom given him, hath written to you:
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	16	As also in all his epistles, speaking in them of these things; in which are certain things hard to be understood, which the unlearned and unstable wrest, as they do also the other scriptures, to their own destruction.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	17	You therefore, brethren, knowing these things before, take heed, lest being led aside by the error of the unwise, you fall from your own steadfastness.
2 Peter	2Pet	68	3	18	But grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ. To him be glory both now and unto the day of eternity. Amen.
1 John	1Jn	69	1	1	That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled, of the word of life:
1 John	1Jn	69	1	2	For the life was manifested; and we have seen and do bear witness, and declare unto you the life eternal, which was with the Father, and hath appeared to us:
1 John	1Jn	69	1	3	That which we have seen and have heard, we declare unto you, that you also may have fellowship with us, and our fellowship may be with the Father, and with his Son Jesus Christ.
1 John	1Jn	69	1	4	And these things we write to you, that you may rejoice, and your joy may be full.
1 John	1Jn	69	1	5	And this is the declaration which we have heard from him, and declare unto you: That God is light, and in him there is no darkness.
1 John	1Jn	69	1	6	If we say that we have fellowship with him, and walk in darkness, we lie, and do not the truth.
1 John	1Jn	69	1	7	But if we walk in the light, as he also is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin.
1 John	1Jn	69	1	8	If we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us.
1 John	1Jn	69	1	9	If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just, to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all iniquity.
1 John	1Jn	69	1	10	If we say that we have not sinned, we make him a liar, and his word is not in us.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	1	My little children, these things I write to you, that you may not sin. But if any man sin, we have an advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the just:
1 John	1Jn	69	2	2	And he is the propitiation for our sins: and not for ours only, but also for those of the whole world.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	3	And by this we know that we have known him, if we keep his commandments.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	4	He who saith that he knoweth him, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar, and the truth is not in him.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	5	But he that keepeth his word, in him in very deed the charity of God is perfected; and by this we know that we are in him.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	6	He that saith he abideth in him, ought himself also to walk, even as he walked.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	7	Dearly beloved, I write not a new commandment to you, but an old commandment which you had from the beginning. The old commandment is the word which you have heard.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	8	Again a new commandment I write unto you, which thing is true both in him and in you; because the darkness is passed, and the true light now shineth.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	9	He that saith he is in the light, and hateth his brother, is in darkness even until now.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	10	He that loveth his brother, abideth in the light, and there is no scandal in him.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	11	But he that hateth his brother, is in darkness, and walketh in darkness, and knoweth not whither he goeth; because the darkness hath blinded his eyes.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	12	I write unto you, little children, because your sins are forgiven you for his name's sake.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	13	I write unto you, fathers, because you have known him, who is from the beginning. I write unto you, young men, because you have overcome the wicked one.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	14	I write unto you, babes, because you have known the Father. I write unto you, young men, because you are strong, and the word of God abideth in you, and you have overcome the wicked one.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	15	Love not the world, nor the things which are in the world. If any man love the world, the charity of the Father is not in him.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	16	For all that is in the world, is the concupiscence of the flesh, and the concupiscence of the eyes, and the pride of life, which is not of the Father, but is of the world.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	17	And the world passeth away, and the concupiscence thereof: but he that doth the will of God, abideth for ever.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	18	Little children, it is the last hour; and as you have heard that Antichrist cometh, even now there are become many Antichrists: whereby we know that it is the last hour.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	19	They went out from us, but they were not of us. For if they had been of us, they would no doubt have remained with us; but that they may be manifest, that they are not all of us.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	20	But you have the unction from the Holy One, and know all things.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	21	I have not written to you as to them that know not the truth, but as to them that know it: and that no lie is of the truth.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	22	Who is a liar, but he who denieth that Jesus is the Christ? This is Antichrist, who denieth the Father, and the Son.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	23	Whosoever denieth the Son, the same hath not the Father. He that confesseth the Son, hath the Father also.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	24	As for you, let that which you have heard from the beginning, abide in you. If that abide in you, which you have heard from the beginning, you also shall abide in the Son, and in the Father.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	25	And this is the promise which he hath promised us, life everlasting.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	26	These things have I written to you, concerning them that seduce you.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	27	And as for you, let the unction, which you have received from him, abide in you. And you have no need that any man teach you; but as his unction teacheth you of all things, and is truth, and is no lie. And as it hath taught you, abide in him.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	28	And now, little children, abide in him, that when he shall appear, we may have confidence, and not be confounded by him at his coming.
1 John	1Jn	69	2	29	If you know, that he is just, know ye, that every one also, who doth justice, is born of him.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	1	Behold what manner of charity the Father hath bestowed upon us, that we should be called, and should be the sons of God. Therefore the world knoweth not us, because it knew not him.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	2	Dearly beloved, we are now the sons of God; and it hath not yet appeared what we shall be. We know, that, when he shall appear, we shall be like to him: because we shall see him as he is.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	3	And every one that hath this hope in him, sanctifieth himself, as he also is holy.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	4	Whosoever committeth sin committeth also iniquity; and sin is iniquity.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	5	And you know that he appeared to take away our sins, and in him there is no sin.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	6	Whosoever abideth in him, sinneth not; and whosoever sinneth, hath not seen him, nor known him.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	7	Little children, let no man deceive you. He that doth justice is just, even as he is just.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	8	He that committeth sin is of the devil: for the devil sinneth from the beginning. For this purpose, the Son of God appeared, that he might destroy the works of the devil.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	9	Whosoever is born of God, committeth not sin: for his seed abideth in him, and he can not sin, because he is born of God.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	10	In this the children of God are manifest, and the children of the devil. Whosoever is not just, is not of God, nor he that loveth not his brother.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	11	For this is the declaration, which you have heard from the beginning, that you should love one another.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	12	Not as Cain, who was of the wicked one, and killed his brother. And wherefore did he kill him? Because his own works were wicked: and his brother's just.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	13	Wonder not, brethren, if the world hate you.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	14	We know that we have passed from death to life, because we love the brethren. He that loveth not, abideth in death.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	15	Whosoever hateth his brother is a murderer. And you know that no murderer hath eternal life abiding in himself.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	16	In this we have known the charity of God, because he hath laid down his life for us: and we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	17	He that hath the substance of this world, and shall see his brother in need, and shall shut up his bowels from him: how doth the charity of God abide in him?
1 John	1Jn	69	3	18	My little children, let us not love in word, nor in tongue, but in deed, and in truth.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	19	In this we know that we are of the truth: and in his sight shall persuade our hearts.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	20	For if our heart reprehend us, God is greater than our heart, and knoweth all things.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	21	Dearly beloved, if our heart do not reprehend us, we have confidence towards God:
1 John	1Jn	69	3	22	And whatsoever we shall ask, we shall receive of him: because we keep his commandments, and do those things which are pleasing in his sight.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	23	And this is his commandment, that we should believe in the name of his Son Jesus Christ: and love one another, as he hath given commandment unto us.
1 John	1Jn	69	3	24	And he that keepeth his commandments, abideth in him, and he in him. And in this we know that he abideth in us, by the Spirit which he hath given us.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	1	Dearly beloved, believe not every spirit, but try the spirits if they be of God: because many false prophets are gone out into the world.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	2	By this is the spirit of God known. Every spirit which confesseth that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh, is of God:
1 John	1Jn	69	4	3	And every spirit that dissolveth Jesus, is not of God: and this is Antichrist, of whom you have heard that he cometh, and he is now already in the world.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	4	You are of God, little children, and have overcome him. Because greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	5	They are of the world: therefore of the world they speak, and the world heareth them.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	6	We are of God. He that knoweth God, heareth us. He that is not of God, heareth us not. By this we know the spirit of truth, and the spirit of error.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	7	Dearly beloved, let us love one another, for charity is of God. And every one that loveth, is born of God, and knoweth God.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	8	He that loveth not, knoweth not God: for God is charity.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	9	By this hath the charity of God appeared towards us, because God hath sent his only begotten Son into the world, that we may live by him.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	10	In this is charity: not as though we had loved God, but because he hath first loved us, and sent his Son to be a propitiation for our sins.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	11	My dearest, if God hath so loved us; we also ought to love one another.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	12	No man hath seen God at any time. If we love one another, God abideth in us, and his charity is perfected in us.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	13	In this we know that we abide in him, and he in us: because he hath given us of his spirit.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	14	And we have seen, and do testify, that the Father hath sent his Son to be the Saviour of the world.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	15	Whosoever shall confess that Jesus is the Son of God, God abideth in him, and he in God.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	16	And we have known, and have believed the charity, which God hath to us. God is charity: and he that abideth in charity, abideth in God, and God in him.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	17	In this is the charity of God perfected with us, that we may have confidence in the day of judgment: because as he is, we also are in this world.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	18	Fear is not in charity: but perfect charity casteth out fear, because fear hath pain. And he that feareth, is not perfected in charity.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	19	Let us therefore love God, because God first hath loved us.
1 John	1Jn	69	4	20	If any man say, I love God, and hateth his brother; he is a liar. For he that loveth not his brother, whom he seeth, how can he love God, whom he seeth not?
1 John	1Jn	69	4	21	And this commandment we have from God, that he, who loveth God, love also his brother.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	1	Whosoever believeth that Jesus is the Christ, is born of God. And every one that loveth him who begot, loveth him also who is born of him.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	2	In this we know that we love the children of God: when we love God, and keep his commandments.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	3	For this is the charity of God, that we keep his commandments: and his commandments are not heavy.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	4	For whatsoever is born of God, overcometh the world: and this is the victory which overcometh the world, our faith.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	5	Who is he that overcometh the world, but he that believeth that Jesus is the Son of God?
1 John	1Jn	69	5	6	This is he that came by water and blood, Jesus Christ: not by water only, but by water and blood. And it is the Spirit which testifieth, that Christ is the truth.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	7	And there are three who give testimony in heaven, the Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost. And these three are one.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	8	And there are three that give testimony on earth: the spirit, and the water, and the blood: and these three are one.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	9	If we receive the testimony of men, the testimony of God is greater. For this is the testimony of God, which is greater, because he hath testified of his Son.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	10	He that believeth in the Son of God, hath the testimony of God in himself. He that believeth not the Son, maketh him a liar: because he believeth not in the testimony which God hath testified of his Son.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	11	And this is the testimony, that God hath given to us eternal life. And this life is in his Son.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	12	He that hath the Son, hath life. He that hath not the Son, hath not life.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	13	These things I write to you, that you may know that you have eternal life, you who believe in the name of the Son of God.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	14	And this is the confidence which we have towards him: That, whatsoever we shall ask according to his will, he heareth us.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	15	And we know that he heareth us whatsoever we ask: we know that we have the petitions which we request of him.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	16	He that knoweth his brother to sin a sin which is not to death, let him ask, and life shall be given to him, who sinneth not to death. There is a sin unto death: for that I say not that any man ask.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	17	All iniquity is sin. And there is a sin unto death.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	18	We know that whosoever is born of God, sinneth not: but the generation of God preserveth him, and the wicked one toucheth him not.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	19	We know that we are of God, and the whole world is seated in wickedness.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	20	And we know that the Son of God is come: and he hath given us understanding that we may know the true God, and may be in his true Son. This is the true God and life eternal.
1 John	1Jn	69	5	21	Little children, keep yourselves from idols. Amen.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	1	The ancient to the lady Elect, and her children, whom I love in the truth, and not I only, but also all they that have known the truth,
2 John	2Jn	70	1	2	For the sake of the truth which dwelleth in us, and shall be with us for ever.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	3	Grace be with you, mercy, and peace from God the Father, and from Christ Jesus the Son of the Father; in truth and charity.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	4	I was exceeding glad, that I found of thy children walking in truth, as we have received a commandment from the Father.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	5	And now I beseech thee, lady, not as writing a new commandment to thee, but that which we have had from the beginning, that we love one another.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	6	And this is charity, that we walk according to his commandments. For this is the commandment, that, as you have heard from the beginning, you should walk in the same:
2 John	2Jn	70	1	7	For many seducers are gone out into the world, who confess not that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh: this is a seducer and an antichrist.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	8	Look to yourselves, that you lose not the things which you have wrought: but that you may receive a full reward.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	9	Whosoever revolteth, and continueth not in the doctrine of Christ, hath not God. He that continueth in the doctrine, the same hath both the Father and the Son.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	10	If any man come to you, and bring not this doctrine, receive him not into the house nor say to him, God speed you.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	11	For he that saith unto him, God speed you, communicateth with his wicked works.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	12	Having more things to write unto you, I would not by paper and ink: for I hope that I shall be with you, and speak face to face: that your joy may be full.
2 John	2Jn	70	1	13	The children of thy sister Elect salute thee.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	1	The ancient to the dearly beloved Gaius, whom I love in truth.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	2	Dearly beloved, concerning all things I make it my prayer that thou mayest proceed prosperously, and fare well as thy soul doth prosperously.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	3	I was exceedingly glad when the brethren came and gave testimony to the truth in thee, even as thou walkest in the truth.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	4	I have no greater grace than this, to hear that my children walk in truth.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	5	Dearly beloved, thou dost faithfully whatever thou dost for the brethren, and that for strangers,
3 John	3Jn	71	1	6	Who have given testimony to thy charity in the sight of the church: whom thou shalt do well to bring forward on their way in a manner worthy of God.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	7	Because, for his name they went out, taking nothing of the Gentiles.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	8	We therefore ought to receive such, that we may be fellow helpers of the truth.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	9	I had written perhaps to the church: but Diotrephes, who loveth to have the pre-eminence among them, doth not receive us.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	10	For this cause, if I come, I will advertise his works which he doth, with malicious words prating against us. And as if these things were not enough for him, neither doth he himself receive the brethren, and them that do receive them he forbiddeth, and casteth out of the church.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	11	Dearly beloved, follow not that which is evil, but that which is good. He that doth good, is of God: he that doth evil, hath not seen God.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	12	To Demetrius testimony is given by all, and by the truth itself, yea and we also give testimony: and thou knowest that our testimony is true.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	13	I had many things to write unto thee: but I would not by ink and pen write to thee.
3 John	3Jn	71	1	14	But I hope speedily to see thee, and we will speak mouth to mouth. Peace be to thee. Our friends salute thee. Salute the friends by name.
Jude	Jude	72	1	1	Jude, the servant of Jesus Christ, and brother of James: to them that are beloved in God the Father, and preserved in Jesus Christ, and called.
Jude	Jude	72	1	2	Mercy unto you, and peace, and charity be fulfilled.
Jude	Jude	72	1	3	Dearly beloved, taking all care to write unto you concerning your common salvation, I was under a necessity to write unto you: to beseech you to contend earnestly for the faith once delivered to the saints.
Jude	Jude	72	1	4	For certain men are secretly entered in, (who were written of long ago unto this judgment,) ungodly men, turning the grace of our Lord God into riotousness, and denying the only sovereign Ruler, and our Lord Jesus Christ.
Jude	Jude	72	1	5	I will therefore admonish you, though ye once knew all things, that Jesus, having saved the people out of the land of Egypt, did afterwards destroy them that believed not:
Jude	Jude	72	1	6	And the angels who kept not their principality, but forsook their own habitation, he hath reserved under darkness in everlasting chains, unto the judgment of the great day.
Jude	Jude	72	1	7	As Sodom and Gomorrha, and the neighbouring cities, in like manner, having given themselves to fornication, and going after other flesh, were made an example, suffering the punishment of eternal fire.
Jude	Jude	72	1	8	In like manner these men also defile the flesh, and despise dominion, and blaspheme majesty.
Jude	Jude	72	1	9	When Michael the archangel, disputing with the devil, contended about the body of Moses, he durst not bring against him the judgment of railing speech, but said: The Lord command thee.
Jude	Jude	72	1	10	But these men blaspheme whatever things they know not: and what things soever they naturally know, like dumb beasts, in these they are corrupted.
Jude	Jude	72	1	11	Woe unto them, for they have gone in the way of Cain: and after the error of Balaam they have for reward poured out themselves, and have perished in the contradiction of Core.
Jude	Jude	72	1	12	These are spots in their banquets, feasting together without fear, feeding themselves, clouds without water, which are carried about by winds, trees of the autumn, unfruitful, twice dead, plucked up by the roots,
Jude	Jude	72	1	13	Raging waves of the sea, foaming out their own confusion; wandering stars, to whom the storm of darkness is reserved for ever.
Jude	Jude	72	1	14	Now of these Enoch also, the seventh from Adam, prophesied, saying: Behold, the Lord cometh with thousands of his saints,
Jude	Jude	72	1	15	To execute judgment upon all, and to reprove all the ungodly for all the works of their ungodliness, whereby they have done ungodly, and of all the hard things which ungodly sinners have spoken against God.
Jude	Jude	72	1	16	These are murmurers, full of complaints, walking according to their own desires, and their mouth speaketh proud things, admiring persons for gain's sake.
Jude	Jude	72	1	17	But you, my dearly beloved, be mindful of the words which have been spoken before by the apostles of our Lord Jesus Christ,
Jude	Jude	72	1	18	Who told you, that in the last time there should come mockers, walking according to their own desires in ungodlinesses.
Jude	Jude	72	1	19	These are they, who separate themselves, sensual men, having not the Spirit.
Jude	Jude	72	1	20	But you, my beloved, building yourselves upon your most holy faith, praying in the Holy Ghost,
Jude	Jude	72	1	21	Keep yourselves in the love of God, waiting for the mercy of our Lord Jesus Christ, unto life everlasting.
Jude	Jude	72	1	22	And some indeed reprove, being judged:
Jude	Jude	72	1	23	But others save, pulling them out of the fire. And on others have mercy, in fear, hating also the spotted garment which is carnal.
Jude	Jude	72	1	24	Now to him who is able to preserve you without sin, and to present you spotless before the presence of his glory with exceeding joy, in the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ,
Jude	Jude	72	1	25	To the only God our Saviour through Jesus Christ our Lord, be glory and magnificence, empire and power, before all ages, and now, and for all ages of ages. Amen.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	1	The Revelation of Jesus Christ, which God gave unto him, to make known to his servants the things which must shortly come to pass: and signified, sending by his angel to his servant John,
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	2	Who hath given testimony to the word of God, and the testimony of Jesus Christ, what things soever he hath seen.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	3	Blessed is he, that readeth and heareth the words of this prophecy; and keepeth those things which are written in it; for the time is at hand.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	4	John to the seven churches which are in Asia. Grace be unto you and peace from him that is, and that was, and that is to come, and from the seven spirits which are before his throne,
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	5	And from Jesus Christ, who is the faithful witness, the first begotten of the dead, and the prince of the kings of the earth, who hath loved us, and washed us from our sins in his own blood,
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	6	And hath made us a kingdom, and priests to God and his Father, to him be glory and empire for ever and ever. Amen.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	7	Behold, he cometh with the clouds, and every eye shall see him, and they also that pierced him. And all the tribes of the earth shall bewail themselves because of him. Even so. Amen.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	8	I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the end, saith the Lord God, who is, and who was, and who is to come, the Almighty.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	9	I John, your brother and your partner in tribulation, and in the kingdom, and patience in Christ Jesus, was in the island, which is called Patmos, for the word of God, and for the testimony of Jesus.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	10	I was in the spirit on the Lord's day, and heard behind me a great voice, as of a trumpet,
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	11	Saying: What thou seest, write in a book, and send to the seven churches which are in Asia, to Ephesus, and to Smyrna, and to Pergamus, and to Thyatira, and to Sardis, and to Philadelphia, and to Laodicea.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	12	And I turned to see the voice that spoke with me. And being turned, I saw seven golden candlesticks:
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	13	And in the midst of the seven golden candlesticks, one like to the Son of man, clothed with a garment down to the feet, and girt about the paps with a golden girdle.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	14	And his head and his hairs were white, as white wool, and as snow, and his eyes were as a flame of fire,
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	15	And his feet like unto fine brass, as in a burning furnace. And his voice as the sound of many waters.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	16	And he had in his right hand seven stars. And from his mouth came out a sharp two edged sword: and his face was as the sun shineth in his power.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	17	And when I had seen him, I fell at his feet as dead. And he laid his right hand upon me, saying: Fear not. I am the First and the Last,
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	18	And alive, and was dead, and behold I am living for ever and ever, and have the keys of death and of hell.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	19	Write therefore the things which thou hast seen, and which are, and which must be done hereafter.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	1	20	The mystery of the seven stars, which thou sawest in my right hand, and the seven golden candlesticks. The seven stars are the angels of the seven churches. And the seven candlesticks are the seven churches.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	1	Unto the angel of the church of Ephesus write: These things saith he, who holdeth the seven stars in his right hand, who walketh in the midst of the seven golden candlesticks:
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	2	I know thy works, and thy labour, and thy patience, and how thou canst not bear them that are evil, and thou hast tried them, who say they are apostles, and are not, and hast found them liars:
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	3	And thou hast patience, and hast endured for my name, and hast not fainted.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	4	But I have somewhat against thee, because thou hast left thy first charity.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	5	Be mindful therefore from whence thou art fallen: and do penance, and do the first works. Or else I come to thee, and will move thy candlestick out of its place, except thou do penance.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	6	But this thou hast, that thou hatest the deeds of the Nicolaites, which I also hate.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	7	He, that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches: To him, that overcometh, I will give to eat of the tree of life, which is in the paradise of my God.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	8	And to the angel of the church of Smyrna write: These things saith the First and the Last, who was dead, and is alive:
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	9	I know thy tribulation and thy poverty, but thou art rich: and thou art blasphemed by them that say they are Jews and are not, but are the synagogue of Satan.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	10	Fear none of those things which thou shalt suffer. Behold, the devil will cast some of you into prison that you may be tried: and you shall have tribulation ten days. Be thou faithful until death: and I will give thee the crown of life.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	11	He, that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches: He that shall overcome, shall not be hurt by the second death.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	12	And to the angel of the church of Pergamus write: These things, saith he, that hath the sharp two edged sword:
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	13	I know where thou dwellest, where the seat of Satan is: and thou holdest fast my name, and hast not denied my faith. Even in those days when Antipas was my faithful witness, who was slain among you, where Satan dwelleth.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	14	But I have against thee a few things: because thou hast there them that hold the doctrine of Balaam, who taught Balac to cast a stumblingblock before the children of Israel, to eat, and to commit fornication:
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	15	So hast thou also them that hold the doctrine of the Nicolaites.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	16	In like manner do penance: if not, I will come to thee quickly, and will fight against them with the sword of my mouth.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	17	He, that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches: To him that overcometh, I will give the hidden manna, and will give him a white counter, and in the counter, a new name written, which no man knoweth, but he that receiveth it.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	18	And to the angel of the church of Thyatira write: These things saith the Son of God, who hath his eyes like to a flame of fire, and his feet like to fine brass.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	19	I know thy works, and thy faith, and thy charity, and thy ministry, and thy patience, and thy last works which are more than the former.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	20	But I have against thee a few things: because thou sufferest the woman Jezabel, who calleth herself a prophetess, to teach, and to seduce my servants, to commit fornication, and to eat of things sacrificed to idols.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	21	And I gave her a time that she might do penance, and she will not repent of her fornication.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	22	Behold, I will cast her into a bed: and they that commit adultery with her shall be in very great tribulation, except they do penance from their deeds.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	23	And I will kill her children with death, and all the churches shall know that I am he that searcheth the reins and hearts, and I will give to every one of you according to your works. But to you I say,
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	24	And to the rest who are at Thyatira: Whosoever have not this doctrine, and who have not known the depths of Satan, as they say, I will not put upon you any other burthen.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	25	Yet that, which you have, hold fast till I come.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	26	And he that shall overcome, and keep my works unto the end, I will give him power over the nations.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	27	And he shall rule them with a rod of iron, and as the vessel of a potter they shall be broken,
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	28	As I also have received of my Father: and I will give him the morning star.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	2	29	He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	1	And to the angel of the church of Sardis, write: These things saith he, that hath the seven spirits of God, and the seven stars: I know thy works, that thou hast the name of being alive: and thou art dead.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	2	Be watchful and strengthen the things that remain, which are ready to die. For I find not thy works full before my God.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	3	Have in mind therefore in what manner thou hast received and heard: and observe, and do penance. If then thou shalt not watch, I will come to thee as a thief, and thou shalt not know at what hour I will come to thee.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	4	But thou hast a few names in Sardis, which have not defiled their garments: and they shall walk with me in white, because they are worthy.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	5	He that shall overcome, shall thus be clothed in white garments, and I will not blot out his name out of the book of life, and I will confess his name before my Father, and before his angels.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	6	He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	7	And to the angel of the church of Philadelphia, write: These things saith the Holy One and the true one, he that hath the key of David; he that openeth, and no man shutteth; shutteth, and no man openeth:
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	8	I know thy works. Behold, I have given before thee a door opened, which no man can shut: because thou hast a little strength, and hast kept my word, and hast not denied my name.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	9	Behold, I will bring of the synagogue of Satan, who say they are Jews, and are not, but do lie. Behold, I will make them to come and adore before thy feet. And they shall know that I have loved thee.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	10	Because thou hast kept the word of my patience, I will also keep thee from the hour of the temptation, which shall come upon the whole world to try them that dwell upon the earth.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	11	Behold, I come quickly: hold fast that which thou hast, that no man take thy crown.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	12	He that shall overcome, I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God; and he shall go out no more; and I will write upon him the name of my God, and the name of the city of my God, the new Jerusalem, which cometh down out of heaven from my God, and my new name.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	13	He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	14	And to the angel of the church of Laodicea, write: These things saith the Amen, the faithful and true witness, who is the beginning of the creation of God:
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	15	I know thy works, that thou art neither cold, nor hot. I would thou wert cold, or hot.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	16	But because thou art lukewarm, and neither cold, nor hot, I will begin to vomit thee out of my mouth.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	17	Because thou sayest: I am rich, and made wealthy, and have need of nothing: and knowest not, that thou art wretched, and miserable, and poor, and blind, and naked.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	18	I counsel thee to buy of me gold fire tried, that thou mayest be made rich; and mayest be clothed in white garments, and that the shame of thy nakedness may not appear; and anoint thy eyes with eyesalve, that thou mayest see.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	19	Such as I love, I rebuke and chastise. Be zealous therefore, and do penance.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	20	Behold, I stand at the gate, and knock. If any man shall hear my voice, and open to me the door, I will come in to him, and will sup with him, and he with me.
Apocalypse	Apoc	73	3	21	To him that shall overcome, I will give to sit with me in my throne: as I also have overcome, and am set down with my Father in his throne.
